declare
  v_plan constant jsonb := $audit_plan${"generatedBy":"scripts/audit/apply-audit-fixes.ts","problems":[{"patch_id":"R6-STAT2-MC28","problem_id":"74b5384e-aae6-4ca7-acf7-e693ca0bbcbd","from_version_id":"3edfadc1-bd20-45cd-bab8-f80e1a71b6df","expect":{"options":["$H_0$: age group and preferred way to read the news are independent in the population of adult residents of a county; $df = 11$","$H_0$: age group and preferred way to read the news are independent in the sample; $df = 6$","$H_0$: age group and preferred way to read the news are independent in the population of adult residents of a county; $df = 6$","$H_0$: the distribution of preferred way to read the news is the same for all $3$ age groups in the population of adult residents of a county; $df = 6$"],"explanation":"The correct answer is $H_0$: age group and preferred way to read the news are independent in the population of adult residents of a county; $df = 6$. One random sample of $392$ was classified on two categorical variables, so this is a test of independence: the null hypothesis says that the variables are independent (no association) in the population; $df = (3-1)(4-1) = 6$.\n$H_0$: age group and preferred way to read the news are independent in the population of adult residents of a county; $df = 11$ is incorrect: Uses the number of cells minus $1$ for the degrees of freedom instead of $(3-1)(4-1)$.\n$H_0$: age group and preferred way to read the news are independent in the sample; $df = 6$ is incorrect: States the hypothesis about the sample rather than the population; the counts in the sample will not be exactly independent even if the variables are independent in the population.\n$H_0$: the distribution of preferred way to read the news is the same for all $3$ age groups in the population of adult residents of a county; $df = 6$ is incorrect: Describes a test of homogeneity, which is for separate samples from several populations; here one sample was classified on both variables.","explanation_en