s that the variables are independent (no association) in the population; $df = (3-1)(4-1) = 6$.\n$H_0$: age group and preferred way to read the news are independent in the population of adult residents of a county; $df = 11$ is incorrect: Uses the number of cells minus $1$ for the degrees of freedom instead of $(3-1)(4-1)$.\n$H_0$: age group and preferred way to read the news are independent in the sample; $df = 6$ is incorrect: States the hypothesis about the sample rather than the population; the counts in the sample will not be exactly independent even if the variables are independent in the population.\n$H_0$: age group and preferred way to read the news are associated in the population of adult residents of a county; $df = 6$ is incorrect: States the alternative hypothesis (an association) as the null hypothesis; the null hypothesis of a chi-square test of independence says there is no association.","explanation_en":"The correct answer is $H_0$: age group and preferred way to read the news are independent in the population of adult residents of a county; $df = 6$. One random sample of $392$ was classified on two categorical variables, so this is a test of independence: the null hypothesis says that the variables are independent (no association) in the population; $df = (3-1)(4-1) = 6$.\n$H_0$: age group and preferred way to read the news are independent in the population of adult residents of a county; $df = 11$ is incorrect: Uses the number of cells minus $1$ for the degrees of freedom instead of $(3-1)(4-1)$.\n$H_0$: age group and preferred way to read the news are independent in the sample; $df = 6$ is incorrect: States the hypothesis about the sample rather than the population; the counts in the sample will not be exactly independent even if the variables are independent in the population.\n$H_0$: age group and preferred way to read the news are associated in the population of adult residents of a county; $df = 6$ is incorrect: States the alternative hypothesis (an association) as the null hypothesis; the null hypothesis of a chi-square test of independence says there is no association."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-15T15:30:39.078Z","issues":[],"alt":"Two-way table with row variable Age group. 18 to 34: Print 39, Website 45, Phone app 22, Television 36; 35 to 54: Print 31, Website 20, Phone app 17, Television 40; 55 or older: Print 40, Website 23, Phone app 48, Television 31."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R6-STAT2-MC28","fromVersionId":"3edfadc1-bd20-45cd-bab8-f80e1a71b6df","fromVersionNo":1},"ap":{"candidate_key":"stats-s3b-d:ap_statistics-m05-k0","item_index":0}},{"patch_id":"R6-CSA2-FRQ4","problem_id":"660364db-1156-4d7c-99d7-57ea9c6b9212","from_version_id":"f45556dc-3154-4c20-a7c5-669cdfc3abe7","expect":{"explanation":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(a) [9 pt] One correct implementation (ALTON reference answer):\n```java\n    public static int bestRow(int[][] grid) {\n        int best = 0;\n        int bestSum = -1000000;\n        for (int r = 0; r < grid.length; r++) {\n            int sum = 0;\n            for (int c = 0; c < grid[r].length; c++) {\n                sum += grid[r][c];\n            }\n            if (sum > bestSum) {\n                bestSum = sum;\n                best = r;\n            }\n        }\n        return best;\n    }\n```\n   - 1 pt: Iterates over every row with a loop bounded by grid.length\n   - 1 pt: Iterates over every element of the current row with bounds based on grid[r].length (or an enhanced for)\n   - 1 pt: Accesses elements with the row index first and the column index second\n   - 1 pt: Accumulates the sum of the current row (and resets it for each row)\n   - 1 pt: Keeps track of the largest row sum seen so far, initialized so negative sums work\n   - 1 pt: Compares the current row sum with the best using a strict comparison so ties keep the smaller index\n   - 1 pt: Records the row index (not the sum) of the best row when a new best is found\n   - 1 pt: Returns the row index after all rows have been examined\n   - 1 pt: The method compiles with correct 2D array syntax and return type int","explanation_en":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(a) [9 pt] One correct implementation (ALTON reference answer):\n```java\n    public static int bestRow(int[][] grid) {\n        int best = 0;\n        int bestSum = -1000000;\n        for (int r = 0; r < grid.length; r++) {\n            int sum = 0;\n            for (int c = 0; c < grid[r].length; c++) {\n                sum += grid[r][c];\n            }\n            if (sum > bestSum) {\n                bestSum = sum;\n                best = r;\n            }\n        }\n        return best;\n    }\n```\n   - 1 pt: Iterates over every row with a loop bounded by grid.length\n   - 1 pt: Iterates over every element of the current row with bounds based on grid[r].length (or an enhanced for)\n   - 1 pt: Accesses elements with the row index first and the column index second\n   - 1 pt: Accumulates the sum of the current row (and resets it for each row)\n   - 1 pt: Keeps track of the largest row sum seen so far, initialized so negative sums work\n   - 1 pt: Compares the current row sum with the best using a strict comparison so ties keep the smaller index\n   - 1 pt: Records the row index (not the sum) of the best row when a new best is found\n   - 1 pt: Returns the row index after all rows have been examined\n   - 1 pt: The method compiles with correct 2D array syntax and return type int"},"set":{"explanation":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(a) [9 pt] One correct implementation (ALTON reference answer):\n```java\n    public static int bestRow(int[][] grid) {\n        int best = 0;\n        int bestSum = 0;\n        for (int r = 0; r < grid.length; r++) {\n            int sum = 0;\n            for (int c = 0; c < grid[r].length; c++) {\n                sum += grid[r][c];\n            }\n            if (r == 0 || sum > bestSum) {\n                bestSum = sum;\n                best = r;\n            }\n        }\n        return best;\n    }\n```\n   - 1 pt: Iterates over every row with a loop bounded by grid.length\n   - 1 pt: Iterates over every element of the current row with bounds based on grid[r].length (or an enhanced for)\n   - 1 pt: Accesses elements with the row index first and the column index second\n   - 1 pt: Accumulates the sum of the current row (and resets it for each row)\n   - 1 pt: Keeps track of the largest row sum seen so far, initialized so that negative sums work (for example from the first row's sum or with Integer.MIN_VALUE, not an arbitrary fixed value such as -1000000)\n   - 1 pt: Compares the current row sum with the best using a strict comparison so ties keep the smaller index\n   - 1 pt: Records the row index (not the sum) of the best row when a new best is found\n   - 1 pt: Returns the row index after all rows have been examined\n   - 1 pt: The method compiles with correct 2D array syntax and return type int","explanation_en":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(a) [9 pt] One correct implementation (ALTON reference answer):\n```java\n    public static int bestRow(int[][] grid) {\n        int best = 0;\n        int bestSum = 0;\n        for (int r = 0; r < grid.length; r++) {\n            int sum = 0;\n            for (int c = 0; c < grid[r].length; c++) {\n                sum += grid[r][c];\n            }\n            if (r == 0 || sum > bestSum) {\n                bestSum = sum;\n                best = r;\n            }\n        }\n        return best;\n    }\n```\n   - 1 pt: Iterates over every row with a loop bounded by grid.length\n   - 1 pt: Iterates over every element of the current row with bounds based on grid[r].length (or an enhanced for)\n   - 1 pt: Accesses elements with the row index first and the column index second\n   - 1 pt: Accumulates the sum of the current row (and resets it for each row)\n   - 1 pt: Keeps track of the largest row sum seen so far, initialized so that negative sums work (for example from the first row's sum or with Integer.MIN_VALUE, not an arbitrary fixed value such as -1000000)\n   - 1 pt: Compares the current row sum with the best using a strict comparison so ties keep the smaller index\n   - 1 pt: Records the row index (not the sum) of the best row when a new best is found\n   - 1 pt: Returns the row index after all rows have been examined\n   - 1 pt: The method compiles with correct 2D array syntax and return type int"},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-15T15:30:39.078Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R6-CSA2-FRQ4","fromVersionId":"f45556dc-3154-4c20-a7c5-669cdfc3abe7","fromVersionNo":1},"ap":{"candidate_key":"csa-s2-short-frq:ap_computer_science_a-f01-k0","item_index":0}},{"patch_id":"R6-PHYS1-MC9","problem_id":"b6d4eaa9-9661-4b23-9b56-33c86e25a0fd","from_version_id":"e8ef9b52-571f-4123-a236-551c337d1a68","expect":{"question":"A block of mass $m$ slides down a rough incline that makes an angle $\\theta$ with the horizontal. The coefficient of kinetic friction between the block and the incline is $\\mu$. Which of the following is the magnitude of the acceleration of the block?","explanation":"The correct answer is $g(\\sin\\theta-\\mu\\cos\\theta)$. Along the incline $ma=mg\\sin\\theta-\\mu N$ and perpendicular to it $N=mg\\cos\\theta$; combining them gives $a=g(\\sin\\theta-\\mu\\cos\\theta)$.\n$g\\sin\\theta$ is incorrect: Ignores friction and keeps only the component of gravity along the incline.\n$\\mu g\\cos\\theta$ is incorrect: Gives only the friction force divided by the mass, leaving out the component of gravity along the incline.\n$g(\\sin\\theta+\\mu\\cos\\theta)$ is incorrect: Points the kinetic friction force in the wrong direction along the incline, so the friction term has the opposite sign.","explanation_en":"The correct answer is $g(\\sin\\theta-\\mu\\cos\\theta)$. Along the incline $ma=mg\\sin\\theta-\\mu N$ and perpendicular to it $N=mg\\cos\\theta$; combining them gives $a=g(\\sin\\theta-\\mu\\cos\\theta)$.\n$g\\sin\\theta$ is incorrect: Ignores friction and keeps only the component of gravity along the incline.\n$\\mu g\\cos\\theta$ is incorrect: Gives only the friction force divided by the mass, leaving out the component of gravity along the incline.\n$g(\\sin\\theta+\\mu\\cos\\theta)$ is incorrect: Points the kinetic friction force in the wrong direction along the incline, so the friction term has the opposite sign."},"set":{"question":"A block of mass $m$ is released from rest on a rough incline that makes an angle $\\theta$ with the horizontal, and it accelerates down the incline. The coefficient of kinetic friction between the block and the incline is $\\mu$. Which of the following is the magnitude of the acceleration of the block?","explanation":"The correct answer is $g(\\sin\\theta-\\mu\\cos\\theta)$. Along the incline $ma=mg\\sin\\theta-\\mu N$ and perpendicular to it $N=mg\\cos\\theta$; combining them gives $a=g(\\sin\\theta-\\mu\\cos\\theta)$. Because the block is released from rest and accelerates down the incline, $\\sin\\theta>\\mu\\cos\\theta$, so this expression is positive and equals the magnitude of the acceleration.\n$g\\sin\\theta$ is incorrect: Ignores friction and keeps only the component of gravity along the incline.\n$\\mu g\\cos\\theta$ is incorrect: Gives only the friction force divided by the mass, leaving out the component of gravity along the incline.\n$g(\\sin\\theta+\\mu\\cos\\theta)$ is incorrect: Points the kinetic friction force in the wrong direction along the incline, so the friction term has the opposite sign.","explanation_en":"The correct answer is $g(\\sin\\theta-\\mu\\cos\\theta)$. Along the incline $ma=mg\\sin\\theta-\\mu N$ and perpendicular to it $N=mg\\cos\\theta$; combining them gives $a=g(\\sin\\theta-\\mu\\cos\\theta)$. Because the block is released from rest and accelerates down the incline, $\\sin\\theta>\\mu\\cos\\theta$, so this expression is positive and equals the magnitude of the acceleration.\n$g\\sin\\theta$ is incorrect: Ignores friction and keeps only the component of gravity along the incline.\n$\\mu g\\cos\\theta$ is incorrect: Gives only the friction force divided by the mass, leaving out the component of gravity along the incline.\n$g(\\sin\\theta+\\mu\\cos\\theta)$ is incorrect: Points the kinetic friction force in the wrong direction along the incline, so the friction term has the opposite sign."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-15T15:30:39.080Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R6-PHYS1-MC9","fromVersionId":"e8ef9b52-571f-4123-a236-551c337d1a68","fromVersionNo":1},"ap":{"candidate_key":"phys-s3-a:ap_physics_1-m03-k1","item_index":0}},{"patch_id":"R6-MICRO1-MC53","problem_id":"ce9ec65e-9f24-436a-a8ac-c2af6df55454","from_version_id":"6d71984f-e767-417c-be55-b36d09f01ba4","expect":{"question":"A firm in perfectly competitive output and labor markets pays a wage of \\$26. The marginal revenue product of its last worker is \\$30. To maximize profit, what should the firm do?","explanation":"The correct answer is Hire more workers, because the marginal revenue product of the next worker exceeds the wage.. A competitive firm hires labor until MRP equals the wage; here MRP is \\$30 and the wage is \\$26, so it should adjust employment accordingly.\nShut down, because the wage and MRP differ. is incorrect: Confuses a hiring adjustment with a shutdown decision.\nKeep employment unchanged, because both the wage and the marginal revenue product are positive. is incorrect: Positive values do not imply that profit is maximized; MRP must equal the wage.\nHire fewer workers, because total revenue exceeds total cost. is incorrect: Compares totals instead of the marginal hiring rule.\nHire more workers, because the average product of labor is above the wage. is incorrect: Compares average product, not marginal revenue product, with the wage.","explanation_en":"The correct answer is Hire more workers, because the marginal revenue product of the next worker exceeds the wage.. A competitive firm hires labor until MRP equals the wage; here MRP is \\$30 and the wage is \\$26, so it should adjust employment accordingly.\nShut down, because the wage and MRP differ. is incorrect: Confuses a hiring adjustment with a shutdown decision.\nKeep employment unchanged, because both the wage and the marginal revenue product are positive. is incorrect: Positive values do not imply that profit is maximized; MRP must equal the wage.\nHire fewer workers, because total revenue exceeds total cost. is incorrect: Compares totals instead of the marginal hiring rule.\nHire more workers, because the average product of labor is above the wage. is incorrect: Compares average product, not marginal revenue product, with the wage."},"set":{"question":"A firm in perfectly competitive output and labor markets pays a wage of \\$26. The marginal revenue product of the next worker it could hire is \\$30. To maximize profit, what should the firm do?","explanation":"The correct answer is Hire more workers, because the marginal revenue product of the next worker exceeds the wage.. A competitive firm hires labor until MRP equals the wage; the next worker would add \\$30 of revenue but cost only the \\$26 wage, so hiring that worker raises profit and the firm should hire more workers.\nShut down, because the wage and MRP differ. is incorrect: Confuses a hiring adjustment with a shutdown decision.\nKeep employment unchanged, because both the wage and the marginal revenue product are positive. is incorrect: Positive values do not imply that profit is maximized; MRP must equal the wage.\nHire fewer workers, because total revenue exceeds total cost. is incorrect: Compares totals instead of the marginal hiring rule.\nHire more workers, because the average product of labor is above the wage. is incorrect: Compares average product, not marginal revenue product, with the wage.","explanation_en":"The correct answer is Hire more workers, because the marginal revenue product of the next worker exceeds the wage.. A competitive firm hires labor until MRP equals the wage; the next worker would add \\$30 of revenue but cost only the \\$26 wage, so hiring that worker raises profit and the firm should hire more workers.\nShut down, because the wage and MRP differ. is incorrect: Confuses a hiring adjustment with a shutdown decision.\nKeep employment unchanged, because both the wage and the marginal revenue product are positive. is incorrect: Positive values do not imply that profit is maximized; MRP must equal the wage.\nHire fewer workers, because total revenue exceeds total cost. is incorrect: Compares totals instead of the marginal hiring rule.\nHire more workers, because the average product of labor is above the wage. is incorrect: Compares average product, not marginal revenue product, with the wage."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-15T15:30:39.080Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R6-MICRO1-MC53","fromVersionId":"6d71984f-e767-417c-be55-b36d09f01ba4","fromVersionNo":2},"ap":{"candidate_key":"micro-p2-b:ap_microeconomics-m08-k0","item_index":0}},{"patch_id":"R6-MACRO1-MC28","problem_id":"5885525e-029f-4d59-b3fd-f248ba74dbd6","from_version_id":"3eb07db5-fee0-4bab-86b8-78dab5c4349a","expect":{"question":"A household moves $1,000 from a checking account to a savings account. What happens to M1 and M2?","explanation":"The correct answer is M1 decreases by \\$1,000 and M2 does not change.. Savings deposits are in M2 but not M1, so the move lowers M1 and leaves M2 unchanged.\nBoth M1 and M2 decrease by \\$1,000. is incorrect: M2 includes savings deposits, so it does not fall.\nM1 does not change and M2 decreases by \\$1,000. is incorrect: M2 includes both checking and savings deposits, so the total does not change.\nBoth M1 and M2 increase by \\$1,000. is incorrect: Moving funds between accounts does not create money.\nM1 increases by \\$1,000 and M2 does not change. is incorrect: Reverses the effect on M1; checking deposits are part of M1 and savings deposits are not.","explanation_en":"The correct answer is M1 decreases by \\$1,000 and M2 does not change.. Savings deposits are in M2 but not M1, so the move lowers M1 and leaves M2 unchanged.\nBoth M1 and M2 decrease by \\$1,000. is incorrect: M2 includes savings deposits, so it does not fall.\nM1 does not change and M2 decreases by \\$1,000. is incorrect: M2 includes both checking and savings deposits, so the total does not change.\nBoth M1 and M2 increase by \\$1,000. is incorrect: Moving funds between accounts does not create money.\nM1 increases by \\$1,000 and M2 does not change. is incorrect: Reverses the effect on M1; checking deposits are part of M1 and savings deposits are not."},"set":{"question":"Use these definitions: M1 is currency in circulation plus checkable (demand) deposits, and M2 is M1 plus savings deposits, small time deposits, and retail money market funds. A household moves \\$1,000 from a checking account to a savings account. What happens to M1 and M2?","explanation":"The correct answer is M1 decreases by \\$1,000 and M2 does not change.. Under the definitions given, savings deposits are in M2 but not in M1, so the move lowers M1 and leaves M2 unchanged.\nBoth M1 and M2 decrease by \\$1,000. is incorrect: M2 includes savings deposits, so it does not fall.\nM1 does not change and M2 decreases by \\$1,000. is incorrect: M2 includes both checking and savings deposits, so the total does not change.\nBoth M1 and M2 increase by \\$1,000. is incorrect: Moving funds between accounts does not create money.\nM1 increases by \\$1,000 and M2 does not change. is incorrect: Reverses the effect on M1; checking deposits are part of M1 and savings deposits are not.","explanation_en":"The correct answer is M1 decreases by \\$1,000 and M2 does not change.. Under the definitions given, savings deposits are in M2 but not in M1, so the move lowers M1 and leaves M2 unchanged.\nBoth M1 and M2 decrease by \\$1,000. is incorrect: M2 includes savings deposits, so it does not fall.\nM1 does not change and M2 decreases by \\$1,000. is incorrect: M2 includes both checking and savings deposits, so the total does not change.\nBoth M1 and M2 increase by \\$1,000. is incorrect: Moving funds between accounts does not create money.\nM1 increases by \\$1,000 and M2 does not change. is incorrect: Reverses the effect on M1; checking deposits are part of M1 and savings deposits are not."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-15T15:30:39.080Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R6-MACRO1-MC28","fromVersionId":"3eb07db5-fee0-4bab-86b8-78dab5c4349a","fromVersionNo":1},"ap":{"candidate_key":"macro-p2-a:ap_macroeconomics-m33-k0","item_index":0}},{"patch_id":"R6-CHEM2-MC29","problem_id":"e45902d7-9d9c-4c98-a72c-dcd21a7000b0","from_version_id":"53c23a6d-bf6c-4e03-9da7-21c8f2210893","expect":{"question":"A student titrates three identical samples of an acid with NaOH and records the buret readings (table). Which value is the best estimate of the volume of NaOH needed to reach the endpoint, in mL?","explanation":"The correct answer is $21.43\\text{ mL}$. The volume delivered in each trial is final − initial: 21.45 mL, 20.35 mL, 21.40 mL. Trial 2 is far from the other two, so the best estimate is the average of the closest pair, (21.45 + 21.40)/2 = 21.43 mL.\n$21.30\\text{ mL}$ is incorrect: Averages the three final readings.\n$21.07\\text{ mL}$ is incorrect: Averages all three trials, including the trial that is far from the others.\n$21.65\\text{ mL}$ is incorrect: Uses only the final buret reading of the first trial.","explanation_en":"The correct answer is $21.43\\text{ mL}$. The volume delivered in each trial is final − initial: 21.45 mL, 20.35 mL, 21.40 mL. Trial 2 is far from the other two, so the best estimate is the average of the closest pair, (21.45 + 21.40)/2 = 21.43 mL.\n$21.30\\text{ mL}$ is incorrect: Averages the three final readings.\n$21.07\\text{ mL}$ is incorrect: Averages all three trials, including the trial that is far from the others.\n$21.65\\text{ mL}$ is incorrect: Uses only the final buret reading of the first trial."},"set":{"question":"A student titrates three identical samples of an acid with NaOH and records the buret readings (table). Treat any trial whose delivered volume differs from the other two by more than $0.50\\text{ mL}$ as an outlier and leave it out, then average the remaining trials. Which value is the best estimate of the volume of NaOH needed to reach the endpoint, in mL?","explanation":"The correct answer is $21.43\\text{ mL}$. The volume delivered in each trial is final − initial: 21.45 mL, 20.35 mL, 21.40 mL. Trial 2 differs from the other two by more than 0.50 mL, so by the stated rule it is left out, and the best estimate is the average of the other two trials, (21.45 + 21.40)/2 = 21.43 mL.\n$21.30\\text{ mL}$ is incorrect: Averages the three final readings.\n$21.07\\text{ mL}$ is incorrect: Averages all three trials, including the trial that is far from the others.\n$21.65\\text{ mL}$ is incorrect: Uses only the final buret reading of the first trial.","explanation_en":"The correct answer is $21.43\\text{ mL}$. The volume delivered in each trial is final − initial: 21.45 mL, 20.35 mL, 21.40 mL. Trial 2 differs from the other two by more than 0.50 mL, so by the stated rule it is left out, and the best estimate is the average of the other two trials, (21.45 + 21.40)/2 = 21.43 mL.\n$21.30\\text{ mL}$ is incorrect: Averages the three final readings.\n$21.07\\text{ mL}$ is incorrect: Averages all three trials, including the trial that is far from the others.\n$21.65\\text{ mL}$ is incorrect: Uses only the final buret reading of the first trial."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-15T15:30:39.082Z","issues":[],"alt":"Table with columns Trial, Initial reading (mL), Final reading (mL) and 3 rows."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R6-CHEM2-MC29","fromVersionId":"53c23a6d-bf6c-4e03-9da7-21c8f2210893","fromVersionNo":1},"ap":{"candidate_key":"chem-s2-m-b:ap_chemistry-m03-k2","item_index":0}},{"patch_id":"R6-PSY2-MC58","problem_id":"6e7e25f1-b0f0-4b2a-9d48-d60aa25e3498","from_version_id":"b896829d-a032-4c42-8a2d-697d2afe302d","expect":{"figure":{"columns":["Condition","Number of students","Percentage who sought help"],"rows":[["1 (only other listener)","30","85%"],["2 (two other listeners)","30","62%"],["3 (four other listeners)","30","40%"]],"type":"ap_table"}},"set":{"figure":{"columns":["Condition","Number of students","Percentage who sought help"],"rows":[["1 (only other listener)","30","83%"],["2 (two other listeners)","30","63%"],["3 (four other listeners)","30","40%"]],"type":"ap_table","notes":["Percentages are rounded to the nearest whole percent (25, 19, and 12 of the 30 students in each condition)."]},"figure_checked":false},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-15T15:30:39.082Z","issues":[],"alt":"Table with columns Condition, Number of students, Percentage who sought help and 3 rows."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R6-PSY2-MC58","fromVersionId":"b896829d-a032-4c42-8a2d-697d2afe302d","fromVersionNo":1},"ap":{"candidate_key":"psych-p3-mc:ap_psychology-m05-k0","item_ind