sought help decreased.. The percentages fall from 85% to 62% to 40%.\nThe number of other listeners had no effect on helping. is incorrect: The percentages differ across conditions.\nStudents never helped when anyone else could hear. is incorrect: Between 40% and 85% of students sought help.\nAs the number of other listeners increased, the percentage of students who sought help increased. is incorrect: The percentages fall rather than rise.","figure":{"columns":["Condition","Number of students","Percentage who sought help"],"rows":[["1 (only other listener)","30","85%"],["2 (two other listeners)","30","62%"],["3 (four other listeners)","30","40%"]],"type":"ap_table"}},"set":{"explanation":"The correct answer is As the number of other listeners increased, the percentage of students who sought help decreased.. The percentages fall from 83% to 63% to 40%.\nThe number of other listeners had no effect on helping. is incorrect: The percentages differ across conditions.\nStudents never helped when anyone else could hear. is incorrect: Between 40% and 83% of students sought help.\nAs the number of other listeners increased, the percentage of students who sought help increased. is incorrect: The percentages fall rather than rise.","explanation_en":"The correct answer is As the number of other listeners increased, the percentage of students who sought help decreased.. The percentages fall from 83% to 63% to 40%.\nThe number of other listeners had no effect on helping. is incorrect: The percentages differ across conditions.\nStudents never helped when anyone else could hear. is incorrect: Between 40% and 83% of students sought help.\nAs the number of other listeners increased, the percentage of students who sought help increased. is incorrect: The percentages fall rather than rise.","figure":{"columns":["Condition","Number of students","Percentage who sought help"],"rows":[["1 (only other listener)","30","83%"],["2 (two other listeners)","30","63%"],["3 (four other listeners)","30","40%"]],"type":"ap_table","notes":["Percentages are rounded to the nearest whole percent (25, 19, and 12 of the 30 students in each condition)."]},"figure_checked":false},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-15T15:30:39.082Z","issues":[],"alt":"Table with columns Condition, Number of students, Percentage who sought help and 3 rows."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R6-PSY2-MC60","fromVersionId":"d12043f2-e762-42d1-8bb2-62aa524d64cb","fromVersionNo":1},"ap":{"candidate_key":"psych-p3-mc:ap_psychology-m05-k0","item_index":2}},{"patch_id":"R6-STAT2-FRQ1C","problem_id":"58571074-1835-4031-a86b-91d697e02e1a","from_version_id":"fb34f3a0-5b5c-4287-915a-d2b3a71195c0","expect":{"explanation":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [2 pt] Explanatory variable: whether a student exercises before school. Response variable: the number of minutes spent on homework the previous night.\n   - 1 pt: Identifies the explanatory variable as whether a student exercises before school\n   - 1 pt: Identifies the response variable as the number of minutes spent on homework the previous night\n(B) [2 pt] (i) IQR = 64 - 55 = 9. (ii) The upper boundary is 64 + 1.5(9) = 77.5; since 74 is not greater than 77.5, 74 is not an outlier.\n   - 1 pt: Calculates the interquartile range (answer 9)\n   - 1 pt: Compares 74 with the boundary Q3 + 1.5 IQR = 77.5 and states that it is not an outlier\n(C) [2 pt] (i) Number the volunteers, use a random number generator (or draw names from a hat) to choose half of them for the treatment group; the rest form the other group. (ii) Random assignment tends to balance other variables across the groups, so a difference in the response is attributable to the treatment.\n   - 1 pt: Describes a chance method (random numbers, names from a hat) that puts half of the volunteers in each group\n   - 1 pt: Explains that random assignment balances other (lurking) variables between the groups so cause and effect can be concluded\n(D) [1 pt] No. This is an observational study; the groups chose themselves, so other variables (confounders) may explain the difference and a cause-and-effect conclusion is not justified.\n   - 1 pt: States that a cause-and-effect conclusion is not justified because the individuals chose their own groups (observational study, confounding possible)\n(E) [3 pt] For example: What is the typical number of hours of sleep that students at the school get on a school night?\n   - 1 pt: Names the population of interest (all students at the school)\n   - 1 pt: Names the variable that would be measured (the number of hours of sleep that students get on a school night)\n   - 1 pt: Poses a question that anticipates variability among individuals and can be answered with data (not a single fact or an opinion)","explanation_en":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [2 pt] Explanatory variable: whether a student exercises before school. Response variable: the number of minutes spent on homework the previous night.\n   - 1 pt: Identifies the explanatory variable as whether a student exercises before school\n   - 1 pt: Identifies the response variable as the number of minutes spent on homework the previous night\n(B) [2 pt] (i) IQR = 64 - 55 = 9. (ii) The upper boundary is 64 + 1.5(9) = 77.5; since 74 is not greater than 77.5, 74 is not an outlier.\n   - 1 pt: Calculates the interquartile range (answer 9)\n   - 1 pt: Compares 74 with the boundary Q3 + 1.5 IQR = 77.5 and states that it is not an outlier\n(C) [2 pt] (i) Number the volunteers, use a random number generator (or draw names from a hat) to choose half of them for the treatment group; the rest form the other group. (ii) Random assignment tends to balance other variables across the groups, so a difference in the response is attributable to the treatment.\n   - 1 pt: Describes a chance method (random numbers, names from a hat) that puts half of the volunteers in each group\n   - 1 pt: Explains that random assignment balances other (lurking) variables between the groups so cause and effect can be concluded\n(D) [1 pt] No. This is an observational study; the groups chose themselves, so other variables (confounders) may explain the difference and a cause-and-effect conclusion is not justified.\n   - 1 pt: States that a cause-and-effect conclusion is not justified because the individuals chose their own groups (observational study, confounding possible)\n(E) [3 pt] For example: What is the typical number of hours of sleep that students at the school get on a school night?\n   - 1 pt: Names the population of interest (all students at the school)\n   - 1 pt: Names the variable that would be measured (the number of hours of sleep that students get on a school night)\n   - 1 pt: Poses a question that anticipates variability among individuals and can be answered with data (not a single fact or an opinion)","statements":[{"label":"A","mode":"explain","points":2,"prompt":"A researcher plans a study of students at a large high school. For each person the researcher records whether a student exercises before school and the number of minutes spent on homework the previous night. Identify the explanatory variable and the response variable in this study."},{"label":"B","mode":"calculate","points":2,"prompt":"The table shown gives the five-number summary of the number of minutes spent on homework the previous night for the sample of students at a large high school in the study. (i) Calculate the interquartile range. (ii) Is a value of $74$ minutes an outlier according to the $1.5\\times\\mathrm{IQR}$ rule? Justify your answer with a calculation."},{"label":"C","mode":"explain","points":2,"prompt":"The researcher is considering an experiment with $80$ volunteers from all students at the school. (i) Describe a method to assign each volunteer at random to one of the two groups so that the groups are the same size. (ii) Explain why random assignment makes it reasonable to conclude that a difference in the number of minutes spent on homework the previous night was caused by the treatment."},{"label":"D","mode":"explain","points":1,"prompt":"Suppose that instead the researcher simply compares the students at a large high school in the study who already exercise before school with those who do not exercise before school. Can the researcher conclude that whether a student exercises before school causes a change in the number of minutes spent on homework the previous night? Justify your answer."},{"label":"E","mode":"explain","points":3,"prompt":"The researcher also wants to study the number of hours of sleep that students get on a school night among all students at the school. State a valid investigative question about this topic that requires a statistical investigation. Your question should name the population of interest, name the variable that would be measured, and be answerable by collecting and analyzing data that vary from individual to individual."}]},"set":{"explanation":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [2 pt] Explanatory variable: whether a student exercises before school. Response variable: the number of minutes spent on homework the previous night.\n   - 1 pt: Identifies the explanatory variable as whether a student exercises before school\n   - 1 pt: Identifies the response variable as the number of minutes spent on homework the previous night\n(B) [2 pt] (i) IQR = 64 - 55 = 9. (ii) The upper boundary is 64 + 1.5(9) = 77.5; since 74 is not greater than 77.5, 74 is not an outlier.\n   - 1 pt: Calculates the interquartile range (answer 9)\n   - 1 pt: Compares 74 with the boundary Q3 + 1.5 IQR = 77.5 and states that it is not an outlier\n(C) [2 pt] (i) Number the volunteers, use a random number generator (or draw names from a hat) to choose half of them for the treatment group; the rest form the other group. (ii) Because the routine is assigned and followed for weeks before the night whose homework time is recorded, and random assignment tends to balance other variables across the groups, a difference in the response is attributable to the treatment.\n   - 1 pt: Describes a chance method (random numbers, names from a hat) that puts half of the volunteers in each group\n   - 1 pt: Explains that random assignment (with the routine assigned weeks before the homework time is measured) balances other (lurking) variables between the groups so cause and effect can be concluded\n(D) [1 pt] No. This is an observational study; the groups chose themselves, so other variables (confounders) may explain the difference and a cause-and-effect conclusion is not justified.\n   - 1 pt: States that a cause-and-effect conclusion is not justified because the individuals chose their own groups (observational study, confounding possible)\n(E) [3 pt] For example: What is the typical number of hours of sleep that students at the school get on a school night?\n   - 1 pt: Names the population of interest (all students at the school)\n   - 1 pt: Names the variable that would be measured (the number of hours of sleep that students get on a school night)\n   - 1 pt: Poses a question that anticipates variability among individuals and can be answered with data (not a single fact or an opinion)","explanation_en":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [2 pt] Explanatory variable: whether a student exercises before school. Response variable: the number of minutes spent on homework the previous night.\n   - 1 pt: Identifies the explanatory variable as whether a student exercises before school\n   - 1 pt: Identifies the response variable as the number of minutes spent on homework the previous night\n(B) [2 pt] (i) IQR = 64 - 55 = 9. (ii) The upper boundary is 64 + 1.5(9) = 77.5; since 74 is not greater than 77.5, 74 is not an outlier.\n   - 1 pt: Calculates the interquartile range (answer 9)\n   - 1 pt: Compares 74 with the boundary Q3 + 1.5 IQR = 77.5 and states that it is not an outlier\n(C) [2 pt] (i) Number the volunteers, use a random number generator (or draw names from a hat) to choose half of them for the treatment group; the rest form the other group. (ii) Because the routine is assigned and followed for weeks before the night whose homework time is recorded, and random assignment tends to balance other variables across the groups, a difference in the response is attributable to the treatment.\n   - 1 pt: Describes a chance method (random numbers, names from a hat) that puts half of the volunteers in each group\n   - 1 pt: Explains that random assignment (with the routine assigned weeks before the homework time is measured) balances other (lurking) variables between the groups so cause and effect can be concluded\n(D) [1 pt] No. This is an observational study; the groups chose themselves, so other variables (confounders) may explain the difference and a cause-and-effect conclusion is not justified.\n   - 1 pt: States that a cause-and-effect conclusion is not justified because the individuals chose their own groups (observational study, confounding possible)\n(E) [3 pt] For example: What is the typical number of hours of sleep that students at the school get on a school night?\n   - 1 pt: Names the population of interest (all students at the school)\n   - 1 pt: Names the variable that would be measured (the number of hours of sleep that students get on a school night)\n   - 1 pt: Poses a question that anticipates variability among individuals and can be answered with data (not a single fact or an opinion)","statements":[{"label":"A","mode":"explain","points":2,"prompt":"A researcher plans a study of students at a large high school. For each person the researcher records whether a student exercises before school and the number of minutes spent on homework the previous night. Identify the explanatory variable and the response variable in this study."},{"label":"B","mode":"calculate","points":2,"prompt":"The table shown gives the five-number summary of the number of minutes spent on homework the previous night for the sample of students at a large high school in the study. (i) Calculate the interquartile range. (ii) Is a value of $74$ minutes an outlier according to the $1.5\\times\\mathrm{IQR}$ rule? Justify your answer with a calculation."},{"label":"C","mode":"explain","points":2,"prompt":"The researcher is considering an experiment with $80$ volunteers from all students at the school. Each volunteer would be assigned to follow, or not to follow, a morning exercise routine before school for several weeks. At the end of that period the researcher would ask each volunteer how many minutes he or she spent on homework the previous night. (i) Describe a method to assign each volunteer at random to one of the two groups so that the groups are the same size. (ii) Explain why random assignment makes it reasonable to conclude that a difference in the number of minutes spent on homework the previous night was caused by the treatment."},{"label":"D","mode":"explain","points":1,"prompt":"Suppose that instead the researcher simply compares the students at a large high school in the study who already exercise before school with those who do not exercise before school. Can the researcher conclude that whether a student exercises before school causes a change in the number of minutes spent on homework the previous night? Justify your answer."},{"label":"E","mode":"explain","points":3,"prompt":"The researcher also wants to study the number of hours of sleep that students get on a school night among all students at the school. State a valid investigative question about this topic that requires a statistical investigation. Your question should name the population of interest, name the variable that would be measured, and be answerable by collecting and analyzing data that vary from individual to individual."}]},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-15T15:30:39.083Z","issues":[],"alt":"Table with columns Statistic, Value (minutes) and 5 rows."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R6-STAT2-FRQ1C","fromVersionId":"fb34f3a0-5b5c-4287-915a-d2b3a71195c0","fromVersionNo":1},"ap":{"candidate_key":"stats-s3b-c:ap_statistics-f01-k1","item_index":0}},{"patch_id":"R6-PHYS2-FRQ1D","problem_id":"c76a314a-f22b-4bb2-a1c2-6f17148d1333","from_version_id":"c2c74137-3083-4d5d-a112-ac453793f120","expect":{"passage":"A block of mass $m=0.5\\text{ kg}$ is released from rest at a height $h=1.5\\text{ m}$ above a horizontal floor on a smooth, frictionless curved track. At the bottom of the track the block slides onto a rough horizontal section whose coefficient of kinetic friction is $\\mu=0.25$. The rough section is long enough that the block comes to rest on it. Blank energy bar chart templates (one per state, with a common scale) are provided on the answer sheet.","statements":[{"label":"A","mode":"diagram","points":3,"prompt":"In the energy bar charts below, for each of three states (the block at the release point, the block at the bottom of the track just before the rough section, and the block at rest on the rough section), draw bars for the kinetic energy $K$, the gravitational potential energy $U_g$ (zero at the floor), and the energy dissipated by friction as thermal energy $E_{th}$. Use the same scale in each chart."},{"label":"B","mode":"derive","points":3,"prompt":"Derive an expression for the distance $d$ the block slides on the rough section before it stops in terms of $h$, $\\mu$, and fundamental constants."},{"label":"C","mode":"calculate","points":2,"prompt":"Calculate the numerical value of $d$ for $h=1.5\\text{ m}$ and $\\mu=0.25$."},{"label":"D","mode":"explain","points":2,"prompt":"The block is released from twice the height $2h$ on the same track and the same rough section. By what factor does the distance the block slides on the rough section change? Justify your answer."}]},"set":{"passage":"A block of mass $m=0.5\\text{ kg}$ is released from rest at a height $h=1.5\\text{ m}$ above a horizontal floor on a smooth, frictionless curved track. At the bottom of the track the block slides onto a rough horizontal section whose coefficient of kinetic friction is $\\mu=0.25$. The rough section is long enough that the block comes to rest on it, including in the second release from height $2h$ described in part (D). Blank energy bar chart templates (one per state, with a common scale) are provided on the answer sheet.","statements":[{"label":"A","mode":"diagram","points":3,"prompt":"In the energy bar charts below, for each of three states (the block at the release point, the block at the bottom of the track just before the rough section, and the block at rest on the rough section), draw bars for the kinetic energy $K$, the gravitational potential energy $U_g$ (zero at the floor), and the energy dissipated by friction as thermal energy $E_{th}$. Use the same scale in each chart."},{"label":"B","mode":"derive","points":3,"prompt":"Derive an expression for the distance $d$ the block slides on the rough section before it stops in terms of $h$, $\\mu$, and fundamental constants."},{"label":"C","mode":"calculate","points":2,"prompt":"Calculate the numerical value of $d$ for $h=1.5\\text{ m}$ and $\\mu=0.25$."},{"label":"D","mode":"explain","points":2,"prompt":"The block is released from twice the height $2h$ on the same track and the same rough section, which is long enough for the block to stop on it in this release too. By what factor does the distance the block slides on the rough section change? Justify your answer."}]},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-15T15:30:39.086Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R6-PHYS2-FRQ1D","fromVersionId":"c2c74137-3083-4d5d-a112-ac453793f120","fromVersionNo":1},"ap":{"candidate_key":"phys-s4-a:ap_physics_1-f02-k1","item_index":0}},{"patch_id":"R6-PHYS1-FRQ1D","problem_id":"4fbf6e9e-b653-41ee-892d-1cb5cefe0403","from_version_id":"ed6381a0-0f30-42df-bfcc-326e2d5efd19","expect":{"statements":[{"label":"A","mode":"diagram","points":3,"prompt":"On the dot below, which represents the block of mass $m_1$, draw and label the forces (not components) that act on it while it slides. Each force must be represented by a distinct arrow starting on the dot."},{"label":"B","mode":"derive","points":3,"prompt":"Derive an expression for the magnitude of the acceleration of the blocks in terms of $m_1$, $m_2$, $\\mu$, and fundamental constants."},{"label":"C","mode":"calculate","points":2,"prompt":"Calculate the numerical value of the acceleration for $m_1=1\\text{ kg}$, $m_2=3\\text{ kg}$, $\\mu=0.15$."},{"label":"D","mode":"explain","points":2,"prompt":"The tabletop is replaced by a surface with a larger coefficient of kinetic friction. Does the tension in the string increase, decrease, or stay the same? Justify your answer."}]},"set":{"statements":[{"label":"A","mode":"diagram","points":3,"prompt":"On the dot below, which represents the block of mass $m_1$, draw and label the forces (not components) that act on it while it slides. Each force must be represented by a distinct arrow starting on the dot."},{"label":"B","mode":"derive","points":3,"prompt":"Derive an expression for the magnitude of the acceleration of the blocks in terms of $m_1$, $m_2$, $\\mu$, and fundamental constants."},{"label":"C","mode":"calculate","points":2,"prompt":"Calculate the numerical value of the acceleration for $m_1=1\\text{ kg}$, $m_2=3\\text{ kg}$, $\\mu=0.15$."},{"label":"D","mode":"explain","points":2,"prompt":"The tabletop is replaced by a surface with a larger coefficient of kinetic friction, small enough that the blocks, released from rest, still accelerate. Does the tension in the string increase, decrease, or stay the same? Justify your answer."}]},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-15T15:30:39.088Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R6-PHYS1-FRQ1D","fromVersionId":"ed6381a0-0f30-42df-bfcc-326e2d5efd19","fromVersionNo":1},"ap":{"candidate_key":"phys-s2-e:ap_physics_1-f01-k1","item_index":0}},{"patch_id":"R6-MICRO2-FRQ1E","problem_id":"41e1e9a4-0c6b-411b-b96b-eb3bc09c718f","from_version_id":"63791449-827d-461a-8aed-ed9c57ef0f53","expect":{"explanation":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [3 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. Market graph: downward-sloping demand and upward-sloping supply meeting at \\$18 and Q_M. Firm graph: U-shaped ATC with MC crossing it at its minimum, a horizontal price line at \\$18, and Q_F where the price line meets MC.\n   - 1 pt: Market graph labeled correctly\n   - 1 pt: Firm graph curves correct\n   - 1 pt: Quantities labeled consistently\n(B) [2 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. Marginal cost of the 4th ton is \\$24, below \\$25, and the next ton costs \\$28, above it, so the firm produces 4 tons. Profit = \\$25 x 4 - \\$80 = \\$20.\n   - 1 pt: Profit-maximizing quantity (answer)\n   - 1 pt: Economic profit (answer)\n(C) [2 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. Market demand shifts right and the equilibrium price rises to the new price. On the firm graph the new horizontal price line meets MC at Q_F2, and the rectangle between the price and ATC up to Q_F2 is shaded.\n   - 1 pt: Market effect shown\n   - 1 pt: Firm profit shown\n(D) [2 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. Firms are earning positive economic profit, so new firms enter; entry shifts market supply to the right, which lowers the price until economic profit returns to zero.\n   - 1 pt: Entry because of profit\n   - 1 pt: Mechanism of adjustment\n(E) [1 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. Greater than Q_M, because the price returns to its original level while demand is higher, so the market quantity is larger.\n   - 1 pt: States the long-run market quantity and the reason","explanation_en":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [3 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. Market graph: downward-sloping demand and upward-sloping supply meeting at \\$18 and Q_M. Firm graph: U-shaped ATC with MC crossing it at its minimum, a horizontal price line at \\$18, and Q_F where the price line meets MC.\n   - 1 pt: Market graph labeled correctly\n   - 1 pt: Firm graph curves correct\n   - 1 pt: Quantities labeled consistently\n(B) [2 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. Marginal cost of the 4th ton is \\$24, below \\$25, and the next ton costs \\$28, above it, so the firm produces 4 tons. Profit = \\$25 x 4 - \\$80 = \\$20.\n   - 1 pt: Profit-maximizing quantity (answer)\n   - 1 pt: Economic profit (answer)\n(C) [2 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. Market demand shifts right and the equilibrium price rises to the new price. On the firm graph the new horizontal price line meets MC at Q_F2, and the rectangle between the price and ATC up to Q_F2 is shaded.\n   - 1 pt: Market effect shown\n   - 1 pt: Firm profit shown\n(D) [2 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. Firms are earning positive economic profit, so new firms enter; entry shifts market supply to the right, which lowers the price until economic profit returns to zero.\n   - 1 pt: Entry because of profit\n   - 1 pt: Mechanism of adjustment\n(E) [1 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. Greater than Q_M, because the price returns to its original level while demand is higher, so the market quantity is larger.\n   - 1 pt: States the long-run market quantity and the reason","statements":[{"label":"A","mode":"draw","points":3,"prompt":"Draw correctly labeled side-by-side graphs for the market for cabbage and for a typical firm, both in long-run equilibrium at a price of $18 per ton. On your graphs, label the market equilibrium price and quantity ($18 and Q_M), the firm's quantity (Q_F), and the firm's MC and ATC curves."},{"label":"B","mode":"calculate","points":2,"prompt":"Suppose market demand for cabbage increases and the market price rises to $25 per ton. Using the table, calculate the firm's profit-maximizing quantity and its economic profit at that price."},{"label":"C","mode":"draw","points":2,"prompt":"On your graphs from part (A), show the short-run effect of the increase in demand: label the new market price ($25) on the market graph and, on the firm graph, label the firm's new quantity (Q_F2) and shade the firm's economic profit."},{"label":"D","mode":"explain","points":2,"prompt":"Explain what happens to the number of firms in the industry in the long run and why."},{"label":"E","mode":"select","points":1,"prompt":"In the new long-run equilibrium, will the market quantity of the good be greater than, less than, or equal to Q_M? Identify and give a reason."}]},"set":{"explanation":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [3 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. Market graph: downward-sloping demand and upward-sloping supply meeting at \\$18 and Q_M. Firm graph: U-shaped ATC with MC crossing it at its minimum, a horizontal price line at 