 your graphs from part (A), show the short-run effect of the increase in demand: label the new market price ($25) on the market graph and, on the firm graph, label the firm's new quantity (Q_F2) and shade the firm's economic profit."},{"label":"D","mode":"explain","points":2,"prompt":"Explain what happens to the number of firms in the industry in the long run and why."},{"label":"E","mode":"select","points":1,"prompt":"Assume cabbage is a constant-cost industry (input prices and each firm's cost curves do not change when firms enter or exit). In the new long-run equilibrium, will the market quantity of the good be greater than, less than, or equal to Q_M? Identify and give a reason."}]},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-15T15:30:39.088Z","issues":[],"alt":"Table with columns Quantity (tons), Total cost (dollars) and 9 rows."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R6-MICRO2-FRQ1E","fromVersionId":"63791449-827d-461a-8aed-ed9c57ef0f53","fromVersionNo":2},"ap":{"candidate_key":"micro-p1-b:ap_microeconomics-f01-k0","item_index":0}},{"patch_id":"R6-MICRO2-FRQ3C","problem_id":"07516ae5-0353-4b3f-976c-ec50de46dd9e","from_version_id":"b53daa40-6e68-43d2-9ef6-7b3c9e12a288","expect":{"explanation":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [2 pt] Yes: low fare. Whatever Airline Y chooses, Airline X earns more with low fare (for example 61 vs 49 if Airline Y chooses high fare, and 37 vs 32 if Airline Y chooses low fare).\n   - 1 pt: Identifies the dominant strategy\n   - 1 pt: Justifies by showing that the strategy pays more against each of the rival's choices\n(B) [1 pt] (Airline X: low fare, Airline Y: low fare).\n   - 1 pt: States the equilibrium strategy pair for both firms\n(C) [1 pt] \\$37 million for Airline X and \\$43 million for Airline Y.\n   - 1 pt: Reads both equilibrium payoffs from the matrix (answer)\n(D) [1 pt] At least one firm earns more by switching to its best response, so the outcome is not stable without enforcement.\n   - 1 pt: Explains that at least one firm has an incentive to deviate from that outcome (a better response exists), so it is not stable","explanation_en":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [2 pt] Yes: low fare. Whatever Airline Y chooses, Airline X earns more with low fare (for example 61 vs 49 if Airline Y chooses high fare, and 37 vs 32 if Airline Y chooses low fare).\n   - 1 pt: Identifies the dominant strategy\n   - 1 pt: Justifies by showing that the strategy pays more against each of the rival's choices\n(B) [1 pt] (Airline X: low fare, Airline Y: low fare).\n   - 1 pt: States the equilibrium strategy pair for both firms\n(C) [1 pt] \\$37 million for Airline X and \\$43 million for Airline Y.\n   - 1 pt: Reads both equilibrium payoffs from the matrix (answer)\n(D) [1 pt] At least one firm earns more by switching to its best response, so the outcome is not stable without enforcement.\n   - 1 pt: Explains that at least one firm has an incentive to deviate from that outcome (a better response exists), so it is not stable"},"set":{"explanation":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [2 pt] Yes: low fare. Whatever Airline Y chooses, Airline X earns more with low fare (for example 61 vs 49 if Airline Y chooses high fare, and 37 vs 32 if Airline Y chooses low fare).\n   - 1 pt: Identifies the dominant strategy\n   - 1 pt: Justifies by showing that the strategy pays more against each of the rival's choices\n(B) [1 pt] (Airline X: low fare, Airline Y: low fare).\n   - 1 pt: States the equilibrium strategy pair for both firms\n(C) [1 pt] 37 for Airline X and 43 for Airline Y (in the units used by the payoff matrix).\n   - 1 pt: Reads both equilibrium payoffs from the matrix (answer)\n(D) [1 pt] At least one firm earns more by switching to its best response, so the outcome is not stable without enforcement.\n   - 1 pt: Explains that at least one firm has an incentive to deviate from that outcome (a better response exists), so it is not stable","explanation_en":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [2 pt] Yes: low fare. Whatever Airline Y chooses, Airline X earns more with low fare (for example 61 vs 49 if Airline Y chooses high fare, and 37 vs 32 if Airline Y chooses low fare).\n   - 1 pt: Identifies the dominant strategy\n   - 1 pt: Justifies by showing that the strategy pays more against each of the rival's choices\n(B) [1 pt] (Airline X: low fare, Airline Y: low fare).\n   - 1 pt: States the equilibrium strategy pair for both firms\n(C) [1 pt] 37 for Airline X and 43 for Airline Y (in the units used by the payoff matrix).\n   - 1 pt: Reads both equilibrium payoffs from the matrix (answer)\n(D) [1 pt] At least one firm earns more by switching to its best response, so the outcome is not stable without enforcement.\n   - 1 pt: Explains that at least one firm has an incentive to deviate from that outcome (a better response exists), so it is not stable"},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-15T15:30:39.088Z","issues":[],"alt":"Payoff matrix: Airline X (rows) and Airline Y (columns). Table with columns Airline X \\ Airline Y, high fare, low fare and 2 rows."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R6-MICRO2-FRQ3C","fromVersionId":"b53daa40-6e68-43d2-9ef6-7b3c9e12a288","fromVersionNo":2},"ap":{"candidate_key":"micro-p2-f:ap_microeconomics-f05-k1","item_index":0}},{"patch_id":"R6-STAT1-FRQ4D","problem_id":"b597391d-c790-4fc0-8a88-c6ef3ff3c592","from_version_id":"64f5c007-00e4-4e88-a739-078e530aed4f","expect":{"explanation":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [2 pt] $P = 76/181 \\approx 0.420$.\n   - 1 pt: Restricts to the group total (181) as the denominator\n   - 1 pt: Calculates the conditional probability (answer 0.420)\n(B) [2 pt] No. The conditional probability $0.420$ is not equal to the overall probability $0.453$, so the events are not independent.\n   - 1 pt: Compares the conditional probability (0.420) with the overall probability (0.453)\n   - 1 pt: Concludes the events are not independent because the two probabilities are not equal\n(C) [2 pt] $X$ is binomial with $n=6$ and $p=0.45$. $P(X \\ge 2) = 1 - P(0) - P(1) \\approx 0.836$.\n   - 1 pt: Identifies a binomial distribution with n = 6 and p = 0.45\n   - 1 pt: Calculates the probability of at least 2 (answer 0.836)\n(D) [2 pt] $\\hat p = 76/181 = 0.420$; interval: $0.420 \\pm 1.96\\sqrt{0.420(1-0.420)/181}$ = $(0.348, 0.492)$.\n   - 1 pt: Identifies a one-sample z-interval for a proportion with the sample proportion in the center and the standard error with the sample proportion\n   - 1 pt: Calculates the interval (answer (0.348, 0.492))\n(E) [2 pt] We are $95\\%$ confident that the interval from 0.348 to 0.492 captures the true proportion of all freshmen who play a sport. The method captures the true proportion in about $95\\%$ of all random samples.\n   - 1 pt: Interprets the interval as capturing the population proportion for the group in context\n   - 1 pt: Explains the confidence level as the long-run success rate of the method (about 95% of random samples)","explanation_en":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [2 pt] $P = 76/181 \\approx 0.420$.\n   - 1 pt: Restricts to the group total (181) as the denominator\n   - 1 pt: Calculates the conditional probability (answer 0.420)\n(B) [2 pt] No. The conditional probability $0.420$ is not equal to the overall probability $0.453$, so the events are not independent.\n   - 1 pt: Compares the conditional probability (0.420) with the overall probability (0.453)\n   - 1 pt: Concludes the events are not independent because the two probabilities are not equal\n(C) [2 pt] $X$ is binomial with $n=6$ and $p=0.45$. $P(X \\ge 2) = 1 - P(0) - P(1) \\approx 0.836$.\n   - 1 pt: Identifies a binomial distribution with n = 6 and p = 0.45\n   - 1 pt: Calculates the probability of at least 2 (answer 0.836)\n(D) [2 pt] $\\hat p = 76/181 = 0.420$; interval: $0.420 \\pm 1.96\\sqrt{0.420(1-0.420)/181}$ = $(0.348, 0.492)$.\n   - 1 pt: Identifies a one-sample z-interval for a proportion with the sample proportion in the center and the standard error with the sample proportion\n   - 1 pt: Calculates the interval (answer (0.348, 0.492))\n(E) [2 pt] We are $95\\%$ confident that the interval from 0.348 to 0.492 captures the true proportion of all freshmen who play a sport. The method captures the true proportion in about $95\\%$ of all random samples.\n   - 1 pt: Interprets the interval as capturing the population proportion for the group in context\n   - 1 pt: Explains the confidence level as the long-run success rate of the method (about 95% of random samples)","statements":[{"label":"A","mode":"calculate","points":2,"prompt":"The two-way display shown summarizes a survey of $278$ students. One student is selected at random. Given that the selected student is a freshman, what is the probability that the student plays a sport? Round to three decimal places."},{"label":"B","mode":"explain","points":2,"prompt":"Overall, the proportion of the $278$ students who play a sport is $0.453$. Are the events 'the student is a freshman' and 'the student plays a sport' independent? Justify your answer using probabilities from the display."},{"label":"C","mode":"calculate","points":2,"prompt":"Suppose $6$ students are selected at random with replacement. Let $X$ be the number of them who play a sport, and use $p = 0.45$. Identify the distribution of $X$ and calculate the probability that at least $2$ of the $6$ students play a sport."},{"label":"D","mode":"calculate","points":2,"prompt":"Treat the survey data for the freshmen as a random sample from all freshmen. Calculate a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all freshmen who play a sport. Round the endpoints to three decimal places."},{"label":"E","mode":"explain","points":2,"prompt":"Interpret the interval from part (D) in the context of the survey, including the meaning of the confidence level."}]},"set":{"explanation":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [2 pt] $P = 76/181 \\approx 0.420$.\n   - 1 pt: Restricts to the group total (181) as the denominator\n   - 1 pt: Calculates the conditional probability (answer 0.420)\n(B) [2 pt] No. The conditional probability $0.420$ is not equal to the overall probability $0.453$, so the events are not independent.\n   - 1 pt: Compares the conditional probability (0.420) with the overall probability (0.453)\n   - 1 pt: Concludes the events are not independent because the two probabilities are not equal\n(C) [2 pt] $X$ is binomial with $n=6$ and $p=0.45$. $P(X \\ge 2) = 1 - P(0) - P(1) \\approx 0.836$.\n   - 1 pt: Identifies a binomial distribution with n = 6 and p = 0.45\n   - 1 pt: Calculates the probability of at least 2 (answer 0.836)\n(D) [2 pt] Conditions: the sample is random, the population of freshmen is more than 10 times the sample size of 181 (stated), and both $76$ and $105$ are at least 10. $\\hat p = 76/181 = 0.420$; interval: $0.420 \\pm 1.96\\sqrt{0.420(1-0.420)/181}$ = $(0.348, 0.492)$.\n   - 1 pt: Identifies a one-sample z-interval for a proportion with the sample proportion in the center and the standard error with the sample proportion\n   - 1 pt: Calculates the interval (answer (0.348, 0.492))\n(E) [2 pt] We are $95\\%$ confident that the interval from 0.348 to 0.492 captures the true proportion of all freshmen who play a sport. The method captures the true proportion in about $95\\%$ of all random samples.\n   - 1 pt: Interprets the interval as capturing the population proportion for the group in context\n   - 1 pt: Explains the confidence level as the long-run success rate of the method (about 95% of random samples)","explanation_en":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [2 pt] $P = 76/181 \\approx 0.420$.\n   - 1 pt: Restricts to the group total (181) as the denominator\n   - 1 pt: Calculates the conditional probability (answer 0.420)\n(B) [2 pt] No. The conditional probability $0.420$ is not equal to the overall probability $0.453$, so the events are not independent.\n   - 1 pt: Compares the conditional probability (0.420) with the overall probability (0.453)\n   - 1 pt: Concludes the events are not independent because the two probabilities are not equal\n(C) [2 pt] $X$ is binomial with $n=6$ and $p=0.45$. $P(X \\ge 2) = 1 - P(0) - P(1) \\approx 0.836$.\n   - 1 pt: Identifies a binomial distribution with n = 6 and p = 0.45\n   - 1 pt: Calculates the probability of at least 2 (answer 0.836)\n(D) [2 pt] Conditions: the sample is random, the population of freshmen is more than 10 times the sample size of 181 (stated), and both $76$ and $105$ are at least 10. $\\hat p = 76/181 = 0.420$; interval: $0.420 \\pm 1.96\\sqrt{0.420(1-0.420)/181}$ = $(0.348, 0.492)$.\n   - 1 pt: Identifies a one-sample z-interval for a proportion with the sample proportion in the center and the standard error with the sample proportion\n   - 1 pt: Calculates the interval (answer (0.348, 0.492))\n(E) [2 pt] We are $95\\%$ confident that the interval from 0.348 to 0.492 captures the true proportion of all freshmen who play a sport. The method captures the true proportion in about $95\\%$ of all random samples.\n   - 1 pt: Interprets the interval as capturing the population proportion for the group in context\n   - 1 pt: Explains the confidence level as the long-run success rate of the method (about 95% of random samples)","statements":[{"label":"A","mode":"calculate","points":2,"prompt":"The two-way display shown summarizes a survey of $278$ students. One student is selected at random. Given that the selected student is a freshman, what is the probability that the student plays a sport? Round to three decimal places."},{"label":"B","mode":"explain","points":2,"prompt":"Overall, the proportion of the $278$ students who play a sport is $0.453$. Are the events 'the student is a freshman' and 'the student plays a sport' independent? Justify your answer using probabilities from the display."},{"label":"C","mode":"calculate","points":2,"prompt":"Suppose $6$ students are selected at random with replacement. Let $X$ be the number of them who play a sport, and use $p = 0.45$. Identify the distribution of $X$ and calculate the probability that at least $2$ of the $6$ students play a sport."},{"label":"D","mode":"calculate","points":2,"prompt":"Treat the survey data for the freshmen as a random sample from all freshmen, and assume there are more than $1810$ freshmen at the school. Calculate a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all freshmen who play a sport. Round the endpoints to three decimal places."},{"label":"E","mode":"explain","points":2,"prompt":"Interpret the interval from part (D) in the context of the survey, including the meaning of the confidence level."}]},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-15T15:30:39.089Z","issues":[],"alt":"Survey results. Two-way table with row variable Group. Freshmen: Plays a sport 76, Does not play a sport 105; Seniors: Plays a sport 50, Does not play a sport 47."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R6-STAT1-FRQ4D","fromVersionId":"64f5c007-00e4-4e88-a739-078e530aed4f","fromVersionNo":1},"ap":{"candidate_key":"stats-s2b-c:ap_statistics-f03-k0","item_index":0}},{"patch_id":"R6-MACRO1-FRQ1DE","problem_id":"92765650-9861-477c-9737-7d1412949a13","from_version_id":"a47e8a84-03e3-4995-b9d5-02b2cfab64c5","expect":{"explanation":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [3 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. AD slopes down, SRAS slopes up, LRAS is vertical to the right of Y1 because actual unemployment exceeds the natural rate; the AD-SRAS intersection is labeled Y1 and PL1 and the LRAS meets the axis at YF.\n   - 1 pt: AD, SRAS, and LRAS graph labeled\n   - 1 pt: Short-run equilibrium labeled\n   - 1 pt: Full-employment output labeled\n(B) [1 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. With unemployment above the natural rate, nominal wages and input prices fall, which lowers costs and shifts short-run aggregate supply to the right until output equals YF.\n   - 1 pt: Gives the reason: wage adjustment shifts SRAS right\n(C) [1 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. Increase government purchases, increase transfer payments, or cut taxes (any one expansionary fiscal action).\n   - 1 pt: Expansionary fiscal action\n(D) [1 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. The gap is 80 billion and the multiplier is 1/(1 - 0.6) = 2.5, so government purchases must rise by 80/2.5 = 32 billion.\n   - 1 pt: Required increase in purchases (answer)\n(E) [2 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. AD shifts right to AD2, intersecting SRAS at full-employment output, with PL2 above PL1.\n   - 1 pt: AD shifted right and labeled\n   - 1 pt: New price level higher\n(F) [2 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. Government borrowing increases the demand for loanable funds, which raises the real interest rate; the higher real interest rate reduces private investment spending (crowding out).\n   - 1 pt: Gives the reason: real interest rate rises\n   - 1 pt: Gives the reason: private investment falls","explanation_en":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [3 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. AD slopes down, SRAS slopes up, LRAS is vertical to the right of Y1 because actual unemployment exceeds the natural rate; the AD-SRAS intersection is labeled Y1 and PL1 and the LRAS meets the axis at YF.\n   - 1 pt: AD, SRAS, and LRAS graph labeled\n   - 1 pt: Short-run equilibrium labeled\n   - 1 pt: Full-employment output labeled\n(B) [1 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. With unemployment above the natural rate, nominal wages and input prices fall, which lowers costs and shifts short-run aggregate supply to the right until output equals YF.\n   - 1 pt: Gives the reason: wage adjustment shifts SRAS right\n(C) [1 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. Increase government purchases, increase transfer payments, or cut taxes (any one expansionary fiscal action).\n   - 1 pt: Expansionary fiscal action\n(D) [1 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. The gap is 80 billion and the multiplier is 1/(1 - 0.6) = 2.5, so government purchases must rise by 80/2.5 = 32 billion.\n   - 1 pt: Required increase in purchases (answer)\n(E) [2 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. AD shifts right to AD2, intersecting SRAS at full-employment output, with PL2 above PL1.\n   - 1 pt: AD shifted right and labeled\n   - 1 pt: New price level higher\n(F) [2 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. Government borrowing increases the demand for loanable funds, which raises the real interest rate; the higher real interest rate reduces private investment spending (crowding out).\n   - 1 pt: Gives the reason: real interest rate rises\n   - 1 pt: Gives the reason: private investment falls"},"set":{"explanation":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [3 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. AD slopes down, SRAS slopes up, LRAS is vertical to the right of Y1 because actual unemployment exceeds the natural rate; the AD-SRAS intersection is labeled Y1 and PL1 and the LRAS meets the axis at YF.\n   - 1 pt: AD, SRAS, and LRAS graph labeled\n   - 1 pt: Short-run equilibrium labeled\n   - 1 pt: Full-employment output labeled\n(B) [1 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. With unemployment above the natural rate, nominal wages and input prices fall, which lowers costs and shifts short-run aggregate supply to the right until output equals YF.\n   - 1 pt: Gives the reason: wage adjustment shifts SRAS right\n(C) [1 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. Increase government purchases, increase transfer payments, or cut taxes (any one expansionary fiscal action).\n   - 1 pt: Expansionary fiscal action\n(D) [1 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. The gap is 80 billion and the simple multiplier (which holds the price level constant) is 1/(1 - 0.6) = 2.5, so government purchases must rise by 80/2.5 = 32 billion.\n   - 1 pt: Required increase in purchases (answer)\n(E) [2 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. AD shifts right to AD2 (the shift of 80 at the original price level that the 32 billion produces under the constant-price simplification), so equilibrium output rises toward full-employment output YF and the price level rises to PL2, above PL1. Because the price level rises along the upward-sloping SRAS, the actual increase in output is smaller than the horizontal shift of AD, so reaching YF exactly would take a larger shift.\n   - 1 pt: AD shifted right and labeled\n   - 1 pt: New price level higher\n(F) [2 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. Government borrowing increases the demand for loanable funds, which raises the real interest rate; the higher real interest rate reduces private investment spending (crowding out).\n   - 1 pt: Gives the reason: real interest rate rises\n   - 1 pt: Gives the reason: private investment falls","explanation_en":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [3 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. AD slopes down, SRAS slopes up, LRAS is vertical to the right of Y1 because actual unemployment exceeds the natural rate; the AD-SRAS intersection is labeled Y1 and PL1 and the LRAS meets the axis at YF.\n   - 1 pt: AD, SRAS, and LRAS graph labeled\n   - 1 pt: Short-run equilibrium labeled\n   - 1 pt: Full-employment output labeled\n(B) [1 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. With unemployment above the natural rate, nominal wages and input prices fall, which lowers costs and shifts short-run aggregate supply to the right until output equals YF.\n   - 1 pt: Gives the reason: wage adjustment shifts SRAS right\n(C) [1 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. Increase government purchases, increase transfer payments, or cut taxes (any one expansionary fiscal action).\n   - 1 pt: Expansionary fiscal action\n(D) [1 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. The gap is 80 billion and the simple multiplier (which holds the price level constant) is 1/(1 - 0.6) = 2.5, so government purchases must rise by 80/2.5 = 32 billion.\n   - 1 pt: Required increase in purchases (answer)\n(E) [2 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. AD shifts right to AD2 (the shift of 80 at the original price level that the 32 billion produces under the constant-price simplification), so equilibrium output rises toward full-employment output YF and the price level rises to PL2, above PL1. Because the price level rises along the upward-sloping SRAS, the actual increase in output is smaller than the horizontal shift of AD, so reaching YF exactly would take a larger shift.\n   - 1 pt: AD shifted right and labeled\n   - 1 pt: New price level higher\n(F) [2 pt] ALTON reference answer for self-review — not official AP scoring. Government borrowing increases the demand for loanable funds, which raises the real interest rate; the higher real interest rate reduces private investment spending (crowding out).\n   - 1 pt: Gives the reason: real interest rate rises\n   - 1 pt: Gives the reason: private investment falls"},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-15T15:30:39.090Z","issues":[],"alt":"Table with columns Item, Value and 5 rows."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R6-MACRO1-FRQ1DE","fromVersionId":"a47e8a84-03e3-4995-b9d5-02b2cfab64c5","fromVersionNo":1},"ap":{"candidate_key":"macro-p2-f:ap_macroeconomics-f01-k0","item_index":0}},{"patch_id":"R6-ELA2-FRQ1","problem_id":"8a522f7d-2718-4820-8d8f-eaba2e0161e9","from_version_id":"8f799222-22a8-4988-b943-683c8057726e","expect":{"explanation":"Scoring guide (ALTON practice rubric, not official College Board scoring). Each essay is worth 6 points.\nThesis (0-1): A conditional position counts as defensible only if it names the condition. 'Towns should consolidate only if they fund a way for people without cars to reach the hub' earns the point; 'Towns should consolidate if it makes sense for them' does not. A thesis that picks a stance but mislabels it (calling Source D an opponent, for instance) still earns the point; source accuracy is scored in Row B.\n   - 1: A thesis that takes a defensible position on whether small towns should replace several small libraries with one regional hub. The position may be a flat yes or no, a conditional yes (only with transit and local reading rooms, for example), or a limited position (hub for towns within a short distance but not for distant ones). The thesis may appear anywhere in the essay and may be more than one sentence.\n   - 0: No thesis, or a thesis that only restates the prompt, only summarizes what the sources say, or states that the question has two sides without choosing one. Statements like 'There are many factors a town should consider before closing libraries' earn 0.\nEvidence and Commentary (0-4): Sources A, E and F are informational and can support any stance; using them does not by itself show synthesis unless the essay explains what the number means for its argument. Counting the number of sources cited is not enough for 3 or 4: an essay that cites five sources in one sentence each with no commentary stays at 2. Correct arithmetic drawn from the sources (for example, that the current system's overall cost per visit, 1.9 million dollars over 177,000 visits, is actually lower than the hub's projected 11.40) counts as strong evidence use. Misreading a source in a way that reverses its meaning (claiming Source D opposes the hub outright, or that Source E shows Dreyfield residents rarely use their branch) caps the row at 2 unless the misreading is minor and isolated.\n   - 4: Uses specific, well-chosen evidence from at least three sources, consistently explains how each piece supports the line of reasoning, and builds the paragraphs into a developed argument rather than a list. The essay engages with evidence that complicates its position (for example, a pro-hub essay that confronts the survey drop-off in Source E, or an anti-hub essay that confronts the hours and staffing gap in Sources A and B) and explains why its position still holds.\n   - 3: Uses specific evidence from at least three sources to support a line of reasoning, and explains for most of that evidence how it supports the claim. The essay usually points to a particular figure, example or quotation (the 14 percent expected hub use in Dreyfield, the 21.50 dollar cost per visit, Marrow's three-fifteen scene) and says what it shows. Some commentary may still be generic or some evidence may be dropped in without explanation.\n   - 2: Uses evidence from at least three sources and connects at least some of it to the thesis, but the reasoning is thin or uneven: the essay may lean on one source and mention the others briefly, make general claims ('distance is a big problem') without pointing to the specific figures or examples that show it, or leave the reader to infer why a cited fact matters.\n   - 1: Uses evidence from at least two sources but mostly summarizes them in turn ('Source A says... Source B says...') with little or no explanation of how the evidence supports a position. Commentary, where present, repeats the evidence in other words.\n   - 0: Restates the thesis, offers no evidence from the sources, or uses evidence from fewer than two sources with no connection to any claim.\nSophistication (0-1): A strong candidate for this point is an essay that identifies that consolidation shifts who bears the cost (from taxpayers in dollars to outlying residents in distance) and argues from that insight. Another is an essay that uses Source D's conditions to test whether a 'both' solution is really a compromise or simply a smaller version of the status quo. Vocabulary alone, a vivid opening, or a closing call to 'remember what libraries are for' does not earn the point.\n   - 1: The essay does at least one of the following in a sustained way, not in a single sentence: explains why the tension exists (for example, that per-visit cost and per-person access measure different things, so Sources B and C are not actually disagreeing about facts); situates the argument in a wider context, such as what a public institution owes to the residents least able to travel; examines the limits of the evidence, such as noting that the hub visit projection is only a projection and the 'expect to visit' survey column records intention rather than behavior; or writes with a controlled, precise style that makes the reasoning clearer.\n   - 0: The argument is straightforward and does not complicate its own claims, treats the issue as a simple contest between savings and sentiment, or relies on sweeping statements and ornamental prose instead of precise thought. Merely mentioning 'the other side' before dismissing it does not earn the point.\nNote: Score the three rows independently; an essay can earn Row C without earning a 4 in Row B, and vice versa.\nNote: Essays that cite the sources by author name (Kavale, Marrow) instead of letter are acceptable.\nNote: An essay that uses only two sources cannot score above 1 in Row B regardless of quality.\nNote: Numbers in the sources are consistent with one another; an essay that notices and uses a relationship between two sources (Source B's cost figures with Source F, Source C's percentages with Source E) is doing synthesis and should be credited for it.\nNote: Do not penalize an essay for taking a position the grader finds unpersuasive; penalize only unsupported or self-contradicting reasoning.\nNote: Personal experience with a local library counts as evidence only when it is tied to the argument; it does not substitute for the three-source minimum.\n\nSample response, high score (6/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 4, Sophistication 1):\nWhen people argue about the Harlow Valley libraries, they usually talk as if the only question is money versus memory. The sources show something more uncomfortable: consolidation does not get rid of a cost, it moves it. The four towns should build the regional hub, but only if they fund Source D's shuttle and reading rooms in the same vote. Without those, the \"savings\" just get paid by the residents of Dreyfield and Lowen Mill in miles instead of by taxpayers in dollars.\n\nThe case for the hub is real, and Desmond Kavale makes it better than anyone because he actually ran one of the branches. His point is that the current system is already unequal. Pennick has \"the only children's librarian in the valley,\" while Dreyfield's building \"has two outlets and closes at four\" (Source B). Source A backs this up: Dreyfield is open twenty hours a week, and the hub would be open sixty-six. A teenager who has to borrow a car to take an online exam in Pennick is not being served by her local branch. So opponents who picture four thriving libraries are defending something that doesn't exist.\n\nBut Kavale's best number is also misleading. He says each Dreyfield visit costs 21.50 dollars and each Pennick visit costs 7.50 dollars, and Source F charts the hub at 11.40 dollars. That looks like an obvious win until you do the math for the whole system. The four branches cost 1.9 million dollars for 177,000 visits (Source A), which comes to about 10.73 dollars a visit. That is actually cheaper than the hub. The hub lowers the total bill, but it expects 37,000 fewer visits, so its cost per visit goes up rather than down. In other words, the savings come with fewer people using the library, not with more efficient service.\n\nWho are those missing people? Source E answers that. In Dreyfield, 33 percent of residents visit their branch monthly, but only 14 percent expect to visit the hub that often, and 18 percent of households there have no car. Meanwhile Pennick's number goes up, from 41 to 44. Ilse Marrow's scene at three-fifteen puts faces on these percentages: the middle schoolers, the retired men, and the woman with \"no computer and no car\" who fills out forms on the one public computer (Source C). Kavale and Marrow aren't really disagreeing about facts. Kavale measures cost per visit, and Marrow measures who can get in the door. Divide dollars by visits and the number always looks better once the hardest people to serve stop showing up.\n\nThis is why Source D's conditions are not a watered-down compromise but the actual plan. The shuttle (95,000 dollars) and reading rooms (85,000 dollars) shrink the savings from 300,000 to 120,000, but they keep a public computer and a children's shelf within walking distance of Marrow's kids while also giving them a ride to a homework center their town could never afford. Marrow is right that \"a schedule is a reason not to go,\" which is why the reading room matters more than the shuttle. It keeps the walk-in version of the library alive. Someone could say this is just a smaller copy of the four-branch system. It isn't, though, because a twenty-hour room in a town hall doesn't need its own roof and furnace, and those are exactly the costs Kavale blames.\n\nThe review matters too, because so much of the case rests on guesses. The 140,000 visits is a projection, and \"expect to visit\" in Source E records what people wrote on a survey, not what they will do. A plan built on predictions should come with a promise to check them, and Source D's automatic extension makes that promise binding instead of hopeful.\n\nThe grant deadline makes it tempting to vote yes and work out the details later. The valley should vote yes, but only on the version where the details are paid for up front. Otherwise the towns with the fewest alternatives end up paying for a library they mostly can't reach.\n   - \"The four towns should build the regional hub, but only if they fund Source D's shuttle and reading rooms in the same vote.\" - Earns the point. This is a conditional thesis that names its condition (funded transit and local reading rooms, approved at the same time as construction), so it's defensible and specific, not a vague 'if it makes sense.'\n   - \"the \"savings\" just get paid by the residents of Dreyfield and Lowen Mill in miles instead of by taxpayers in dollars\" - Sets up the line of reasoning the rest of the essay follows: the cost doesn't disappear, it moves from taxpayers to outlying residents. Each body paragraph tests part of this claim.\n   - \"So opponents who picture four thriving libraries are defending something that doesn't exist.\" - Takes on evidence that cuts against caution about the hub (Kavale's staffing and outlets details, plus the 20 vs. 66 hours from Source A) and says what it shows instead of just listing it. Combining B and A here is synthesis.\n   - \"which comes to about 10.73 dollars a visit. That is actually cheaper than the hub.\" - Correct arithmetic from Source A (1.9 million / 177,000) checked against Source F's 11.40 dollars. The rubric names this kind of evidence use as strong. The essay then explains what it means: the savings come with 37,000 fewer projected visits.\n   - \"In Dreyfield, 33 percent of residents visit their branch monthly, but only 14 percent expect to visit the hub that often, and 18 percent of households there have no car.\" - Uses specific figures from Source E accurately, ties them to Marrow's three-fifteen scene in Source C, and links both back to the 'missing visits' point. This is developed reasoning rather than a list of sources.\n   - \"Someone could say this is just a smaller copy of the four-branch system.\" - Brings up a real objection to its own conditional position and answers it with evidence: reading rooms avoid the fixed building costs Kavale points to in Source B. Taking on complicating evidence like this supports a 4.\n   - \"Kavale and Marrow aren't really disagreeing about facts. Kavale measures cost per visit, and Marrow measures who can get in the door.\" - Explains why the tension exists: the two sources use different measures (cost per visit vs. access per person). This is one of the sophistication moves the rubric names.\n   - \"\"expect to visit\" in Source E records what people wrote on a survey, not what they will do\" - Looks at the limits of the evidence (intention vs. behavior, a projection vs. actual numbers) and uses that to argue the review must be binding. Together with the cost-shift framing that runs through the essay, this makes the sophistication sustained rather than a one-sentence gesture.\n   - \"Otherwise the towns with the fewest alternatives end up paying for a library they mostly can't reach.\" - The conclusion goes back to the cost-shift insight instead of a generic 'remember what libraries are for' ending. The essay argues from that insight from start to finish.\n\nSample response, middle score (5/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 4, Sophistication 0):\nThe four towns of Harlow Valley should build the regional hub, but only if they also pay for a shuttle and keep small reading rooms open in the towns that lose their branches. Without those two things the hub would help Pennick and leave the other towns worse off than they are now.\n\nThe best argument for the hub is the one Desmond Kavale makes in Source B. He points out that the four branches are already unequal, since Pennick has the new books, the evening hours and the only children's librarian. Source F backs him up with numbers, because a visit to the Dreyfield branch costs 21.50 dollars and a visit to Pennick costs 7.50. The hub is projected to cost 11.40 per visit, which is a lot less than Dreyfield or Lowen Mill. Kavale also says that closing the branches would free up about 300,000 dollars a year for specialists. This shows that keeping four small buildings open means paying for roofs and furnaces instead of for staff and services, so the hub would be a better use of money for the valley.\n\nHowever, cost per visit does not tell the whole story. If you take the whole system, 1.9 million dollars divided by 177,000 visits is about 10.70 per visit, which is actually less than the hub's projected 11.40. So the hub is not clearly cheaper per visit, and its savings come mostly from having fewer buildings. More important, the people who would lose the most are the ones with the fewest options. Source E shows that only 14 percent of Dreyfield residents expect to use the hub monthly, while 33 percent use their branch that often now, and 18 percent of Dreyfield households have no car. Ilse Marrow in Source C puts a face on those numbers with the woman from the apartments who uses the one public computer because she has no car. Marrow says a shuttle is \"a schedule, and a schedule is a reason not to go,\" and that is a fair point, since Dreyfield is fourteen miles from the hub according to Source A.\n\nThat is why I think Source D has the right answer. The Office of Shared Services does not reject the hub, it says the hub can be recommended with three funded conditions, a shuttle, staffed reading rooms in Dreyfield and Lowen Mill, and a review after three years. These conditions cut the savings from 300,000 to about 120,000 dollars, but that is the price of making the hub serve everyone and not just Pennick. Marrow is right that a shuttle is not the same as a walk, but the reading rooms give people a place in town, which answers her concern better than a shuttle alone could.\n\nIn the end, the question is not only about money. Consolidation saves the taxpayers money, but it moves the cost onto people who have to travel, and that is only fair if the towns pay to reduce that burden. The hub with the conditions is worth building, and the hub without them is not.\n   - \"should build the regional hub, but only if they also pay for a shuttle and keep small reading rooms open in the towns that lose their branches\" - Clear conditional thesis that names its conditions (shuttle and local reading rooms), so it earns the thesis point.\n   - \"a visit to the Dreyfield branch costs 21.50 dollars and a visit to Pennick costs 7.50\" - Specific figures from Sources B and F, tied to the claim that small branches are inefficient. This is good evidence, though the commentary after it is fairly general.\n   - \"1.9 million dollars divided by 177,000 visits is about 10.70 per visit, which is actually less than the hub's projected 11.40\" - Correct arithmetic combining Sources A and F, and it complicates the pro-hub case. The rubric names this as strong evidence use.\n   - \"only 14 percent of Dreyfield residents expect to use the hub monthly, while 33 percent use their branch that often now\" - Uses Source E and the Marrow figures accurately to show the drop-off in Dreyfield. It supports the thesis, but the essay does not look at whether 'expect' is only an intention.\n   - \"the woman from the apartments who uses the one public computer because she has no car\" - Connects Marrow's example to the Source E car-ownership figure, but the explanation is brief and a little repetitive.\n   - \"Consolidation saves the taxpayers money, but it moves the cost onto people who have to travel\" - Names the shift of cost from taxpayers to outlying residents. It appears only in the conclusion and is not developed through the essay, so it is not sustained enough for the sophistication point.\n\nSample response, low score (2/6; Thesis 0, Evidence and commentary 2, Sophistication 0):\nLibraries are an important part of any town and the people of Harlow Valley have a hard choice to make. There are good points on both sides of whether to build one regional hub or keep the four small libraries. In my opinion the towns should think carefully about what is best for everyone before they decide.\n\nSource B says that the old system is not working very well. The writer ran the Oskar Bend branch for nineteen years and says that Dreyfield's building has only two outlets and closes at four. He also says it costs 21.50 dollars per visit in Dreyfield and only 7.50 in Pennick. This shows that the small libraries are expensive and the hub would save money. Source F also shows the costs, with the hub at 11.40 which is in the middle.\n\nOn the other hand Source C is about Dreyfield and it describes kids coming in at three-fifteen and a woman who has no car. The writer says a shuttle is a schedule and a schedule is a reason not to go. This is a good point because lots of people would not be able to get to the hub. Source E has a table that shows the percent of people who would use the hub is lower in Dreyfield, 14 percent, than in Pennick. So distance is a big problem for the hub.\n\nSource D is a memo that says the hub can be recommended but only with conditions like a shuttle and reading rooms. This is a kind of middle option and it costs some money but the saving is still there.\n\nOverall both sides have a point. The hub has longer hours and more staff but people far away might lose out. The towns should look at all of this and make the best choice for the valley.\n   - \"In my opinion the towns should think carefully about what is best for everyone before they decide.\" - No defensible position and no named condition. It says the question has two sides and tells towns to 'think carefully', which the rubric scores 0.\n   - \"There are good points on both sides of whether to build one regional hub or keep the four small libraries.\" - Restates the prompt and declines to choose a side, so the thesis point is not earned.\n   - \"This shows that the small libraries are expensive and the hub would save money.\" - Cites the 21.50 and 7.50 figures but the commentary is thin. It does not notice that the current system's overall cost per visit (1.9 million over 177,000) is lower than the hub's 11.40, so the 'save money' claim is not examined.\n   - \"So distance is a big problem for the hub.\" - Uses the 14 percent figure from Source E but the conclusion is a general claim. It does not connect to Source C's 33 percent monthly use or to car-free households.\n   - \"This is a kind of middle option and it costs some money but the saving is still there.\" - Source D is summarized with no figures (95,000, 85,000, 120,000 saving) and no explanation of how its conditions test the hub plan. The paragraph is a one-sentence summary, so evidence is mostly summarized source by source.\n   - \"Overall both sides have a point.\" - The conclusion repeats the 'two sides' framing. It does not explain why the tension exists, such as cost per visit versus access per person, or who bears the cost. No sophistication.","explanation_en":"Scoring guide (ALTON practice rubric, not official College Board scoring). Each essay is worth 6 points.\nThesis (0-1): A conditional position counts as defensible only if it names the condition. 'Towns should consolidate only if they fund a way for people without cars to reach the hub' earns the point; 'Towns should consolidate if it makes sense for them' does not. A thesis that picks a stance but mislabels it (calling Source D an opponent, for instance) still earns the point; source accuracy is scored in Row B.\n   - 1: A thesis that takes a defensible position on whether small towns should replace several small libraries with one regional hub. The position may be a flat yes or no, a conditional yes (only with transit and local reading rooms, for example), or a limited position (hub for towns within a short distance but not for distant ones). The thesis may appear anywhere in the essay and may be more than one sentence.\n   - 0: No thesis, or a thesis that only restates the prompt, only summarizes what the sources say, or states that the question has two sides without choosing one. Statements like 'There are many factors a town should consider before closing libraries' earn 0.\nEvidence and Commentary (0-4): Sources A, E and F are informational and can support any stance; using them does not by itself show synthesis unless the essay explains what the number means for its argument. Counting the number of sources cited is not enough for 3 or 4: an essay that cites five sources in one sentence each with no commentary stays at 2. Correct arithmetic drawn from the sources (for example, that the current system's overall cost per visit, 1.9 million dollars over 177,000 visits, is actually lower than the hub's projected 11.40) counts as strong evidence use. Misreading a source in a way that reverses its meaning (claiming Source D opposes the hub outright, or that Source E shows Dreyfield residents rarely use their branch) caps the row at 2 unless the misreading is minor and isolated.\n   - 4: Uses specific, well-chosen evidence from at least three sources, consistently explains how each piece supports the line of reasoning, and builds the paragraphs into a developed argument rather than a list. The essay engages with evidence that complicates its position (for example, a pro-hub essay that confronts the survey drop-off in Source E, or an anti-hub essay that confronts the hours and staffing gap in Sources A and B) and explains why its position still holds.\n   - 3: Uses specific evidence from at least three sources to support a line of reasoning, and explains for most of that evidence how it supports the claim. The essay usually points to a particular figure, example or quotation (the 14 percent expected hub use in Dreyfield, the 21.50 dollar cost per visit, Marrow's three-fifteen scene) and says what it shows. Some commentary may still be generic or some evidence may be dropped in without explanation.\n   - 2: Uses evidence from at least three sources and connects at least some of it to the thesis, but the reasoning is thin or uneven: the essay may lean on one source and mention the others briefly, make general claims ('distance is a big problem') without pointing to the specific figures or examples that show it, or leave the reader to infer why a cited fact matters.\n   - 1: Uses evidence from at least two sources but mostly summarizes them in turn ('Source A says... Source B says...') with little or no explanation of how the evidence supports a position. Commentary, where present, repeats the evidence in other words.\n   - 0: Restates the thesis, offers no evidence from the sources, or uses evidence from fewer than two sources with no connection to any claim.\nSophistication (0-1): A strong candidate for this point is an essay that identifies that consolidation shifts who bears the cost (from taxpayers in dollars to outlying residents in distance) and argues from that insight. Another is an essay that uses Source D's conditions to test whether a 'both' solution is really a compromise or simply a smaller version of the status quo. Vocabulary alone, a vivid opening, or a closing call to 'remember what libraries are for' does not earn the point.\n   - 1: The essay does at least one of the following in a sustained way, not in a single sentence: explains why the tension exists (for example, that per-visit cost and per-person access measure different things, so Sources B and C are not actually disagreeing about facts); situates the argument in a wider context, such as what a public institution owes to the residents least able to travel; examines the limits of the evidence, such as noting that the hub visit projection is only a projection and the 'expect to visit' survey column records intention rather than behavior; or writes with a controlled, precise style that makes the reasoning clearer.\n   - 0: The argument is straightforward and does not complicate its own claims, treats the issue as a simple contest between savings and sentiment, or relies on sweeping statements and ornamental prose instead of precise thought. Merely mentioning 'the other side' before dismissing it does not earn the point.\nNote: Score the three rows independently; an essay can earn Row C without earning a 4 in Row B, and vice versa.\nNote: Essays that cite the sources by author name (Kavale, Marrow) instead of letter are acceptable.\nNote: An essay that uses only two sources cannot score above 1 in Row B regardless of quality.\nNote: Numbers in the sources are consistent with one another; an essay that notices and uses a relationship between two sources (Source B's cost figures with Source F, Source C's percentages with Source E) is doing synthesis and should be credited for it.\nNote: Do not penalize an essay for taking a position the grader finds unpersuasive; penalize only unsupported or self-contradicting reasoning.\nNote: Personal experience with a local library counts as evidence only when it is tied to the argument; it does not substitute for the three-source minimum.\n\nSample response, high score (6/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 4, Sophistication 1):\nWhen people argue about the Harlow Valley libraries, they usually talk as if the only question is money versus memory. The sources show something more uncomfortable: consolidation does not get rid of a cost, it moves it. The four towns should build the regional hub, but only if they fund Source D's shuttle and reading rooms in the same vote. Without those, the \"savings\" just get paid by the residents of Dreyfield and Lowen Mill in miles instead of by taxpayers in dollars.\n\nThe case for the hub is real, and Desmond Kavale makes it better than anyone because he actually ran one of the branches. His point is that the current system is already unequal. Pennick has \"the only children's librarian in the valley,\" while Dreyfield's building \"has two outlets and closes at four\" (Source B). Source A backs this up: Dreyfield is open twenty hours a week, and the hub would be open sixty-six. A teenager who has to borrow a car to take an online exam in Pennick is not being served by her local branch. So opponents who picture four thriving libraries are defending something that doesn't exist.\n\nBut Kavale's best number is also misleading. He says each Dreyfield visit costs 21.50 dollars and each Pennick visit costs 7.50 dollars, and Source F charts the hub at 11.40 dollars. That looks like an obvious win until you do the math for the whole system. The four branches cost 1.9 million dollars for 177,000 visits (Source A), which comes to about 10.73 dollars a visit. That is actually cheaper than the hub. The hub lowers the total bill, but it expects 37,000 fewer visits, so its cost per visit goes up rather than down. In other words, the savings come with fewer people using the library, not with more efficient service.\n\nWho are those missing people? Source E answers that. In Dreyfield, 33 percent of residents visit their branch monthly, but only 14 percent expect to visit the hub that often, and 18 percent of households there have no car. Meanwhile Pennick's number goes up, from 41 to 44. Ilse Marrow's scene at three-fifteen puts faces on these percentages: the middle schoolers, the retired men, and the woman with \"no computer and no car\" who fills out forms on the one public computer (Source C). Kavale and Marrow aren't really disagreeing about facts. Kavale measures cost per visit, and Marrow measures who can get in the door. Divide dollars by visits and the number always looks better once the hardest people to serve stop showing up.\n\nThis is why Source D's conditions are not a watered-down compromise but the actual plan. The shuttle (95,000 dollars) and reading rooms (85,000 dollars) shrink the savings from 300,000 to 120,000, but they keep a public computer and a children's shelf within walking distance of Marrow's kids while also giving them a ride to a homework center their town could never afford. Marrow is right that \"a schedule is a reason not to go,\" which is why the reading room matters more than the shuttle. It keeps the walk-in version of the library alive. Someone could say this is just a smaller copy of the four-branch system. It isn't, though, because a twenty-hour room in a town hall doesn't need its own roof and furnace, and those are exactly the costs Kavale blames.\n\nThe review matters too, because so much of the case rests on guesses. The 140,000 visits is a projection, and \"expect to visit\" in Source E records what people wrote on a survey, not what they will do. A plan built on predictions should come with a promise to check them, and Source D's automatic extension makes that promise binding instead of hopeful.\n\nThe grant deadline makes it tempting to vote yes and work out the details later. The valley should vote yes, but only on the version where the details are paid for up front. Otherwise the towns with the fewest alternatives end up paying for a library they mostly can't reach.\n   - \"The four towns should build the regional hub, but only if they fund Source D's shuttle and reading rooms in the same vote.\" - Earns the point. This is a conditional thesis that names its condition (funded transit and local reading rooms, approved at the same time as construction), so it's defensible and specific, not a vague 'if it makes sense.'\n   - \"the \"savings\" just get paid by the residents of Dreyfield and Lowen Mill in miles instead of by taxpayers in dollars\" - Sets up the line of reasoning the rest of the essay follows: the cost doesn't disappear, it moves from taxpayers to outlying residents. Each body paragraph tests part of this claim.\n   - \"So opponents who picture four thriving libraries are defending something that doesn't exist.\" - Takes on evidence that cuts against caution about the hub (Kavale's staffing and outlets details, plus the 20 vs. 66 hours from Source A) and says what it shows instead of just listing it. Combining B and A here is synthesis.\n   - \"which comes to about 10.73 dollars a visit. That is actually cheaper than the hub.\" - Correct arithmetic from Source A (1.9 million / 177,000) checked against Source F's 11.40 dollars. The rubric names this kind of evidence use as strong. The essay then explains what it means: the savings come with 37,000 fewer projected visits.\n   - \"In Dreyfield, 33 percent of residents visit their branch monthly, but only 14 percent expect to visit the hub that often, and 18 percent of households there have no car.\" - Uses specific figures from Source E accurately, ties them to Marrow's three-fifteen scene in Source C, and links both back to the 'missing visits' point. This is developed reasoning rather than a list of sources.\n   - \"Someone could say this is just a smaller copy of the four-branch system.\" - Brings up a real objection to its own conditional position and answers it with evidence: reading rooms avoid the fixed building costs Kavale points to in Source B. Taking on complicating evidence like this supports a 4.\n   - \"Kavale and Marrow aren't really disagreeing about facts. Kavale measures cost per visit, and Marrow measures who can get in the door.\" - Explains why the tension exists: the two sources use different measures (cost per visit vs. access per person). This is one of the sophistication moves the rubric names.\n   - \"\"expect to visit\" in Source E records what people wrote on a survey, not what they will do\" - Looks at the limits of the evidence (intention vs. behavior, a projection vs. actual numbers) and uses that to argue the review must be binding. Together with the cost-shift framing that runs through the essay, this makes the sophistication sustained rather than a one-sentence gesture.\n   - \"Otherwise the towns with the fewest alternatives end up paying for a library they mostly can't reach.\" - The conclusion goes back to the cost-shift insight instead of a generic 'remember what libraries are for' ending. The essay argues from that insight from start to finish.\n\nSample response, middle score (5/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 4, Sophistication 0):\nThe four towns of Harlow Valley should build the regional hub, but only if they also pay for a shuttle and keep small reading rooms open in the towns that lose their branches. Without those two things the hub would help Pennick and leave the other towns worse off than they are now.\n\nThe best argument for the hub is the one Desmond Kavale makes in Source B. He points out that the four branches are already unequal, since Pennick has the new books, the evening hours and the only children's librarian. Source F backs him up with numbers, because a visit to the Dreyfield branch costs 21.50 dollars and a visit to Pennick costs 7.50. The hub is projected to cost 11.40 per visit, which is a lot less than Dreyfield or Lowen Mill. Kavale also says that closing the branches would free up about 300,000 dollars a year for specialists. This shows that keeping four small buildings open means paying for roofs and furnaces instead of for staff and services, so the hub would be a better use of money for the valley.\n\nHowever, cost per visit does not tell the whole story. If you take the whole system, 1.9 million dollars divided by 177,000 visits is about 10.70 per visit, which is actually less than the hub's projected 11.40. So the hub is not clearly cheaper per visit, and its savings come mostly from having fewer buildings. More important, the people who would lose the most are the ones with the fewest options. Source E shows that only 14 percent of Dreyfield residents expect to use the hub monthly, while 33 percent use their branch that often now, and 18 percent of Dreyfield households have no car. Ilse Marrow in Source C puts a face on those numbers with the woman from the apartments who uses the one public computer because she has no car. Marrow says a shuttle is \"a schedule, and a schedule is a reason not to go,\" and that is a fair point, since Dreyfield is fourteen miles from the hub according to Source A.\n\nThat is why I think Source D has the right answer. The Office of Shared Services does not reject the hub, it says the hub can be recommended with three funded conditions, a shuttle, staffed reading rooms in Dreyfield and Lowen Mill, and a review after three years. These conditions cut the savings from 300,000 to about 120,000 dollars, but that is the price of making the hub serve everyone and not just Pennick. Marrow is right that a shuttle is not the same as a walk, but the reading rooms give people a place in town, which answers her concern better than a shuttle alone could.\n\nIn the end, the question is not only about money. Consolidation saves the taxpayers money, but it moves the cost onto people who have to travel, and that is only fair if the towns pay to reduce that burden. The hub with the conditions is worth building, and the hub without them is not.\n   - \"should build the regional hub, but only if they also pay for a shuttle and keep small reading rooms open in the towns that lose their branches\" - Clear conditional thesis that names its conditions (shuttle and local reading rooms), so it earns the thesis point.\n   - \"a visit to the Dreyfield branch costs 21.50 dollars and a visit to Pennick costs 7.50\" - Specific figures from Sources B and F, tied to the claim that small branches are inefficient. This is good evidence, though the commentary after it is fairly general.\n   - \"1.9 million dollars divided by 177,000 visits is about 10.70 per visit, which is actually less than the hub's projected 11.40\" - Correct arithmetic combining Sources A and F, and it complicates the pro-hub case. The rubric names this as strong evidence use.\n   - \"only 14 percent of Dreyfield residents expect to use the hub monthly, while 33 percent use their branch that often now\" - Uses Source E and the Marrow figures accurately to show the drop-off in Dreyfield. It supports the thesis, but the essay does not look at whether 'expect' is only an intention.\n   - \"the woman from the apartments who uses the one public computer because she has no car\" - Connects Marrow's example to the Source E car-ownership figure, but the explanation is brief and a little repetitive.\n   - \"Consolidation saves the taxpayers money, but it moves the cost onto people who have to travel\" - Names the shift of cost from taxpayers to outlying residents. It appears only in the conclusion and is not developed through the essay, so it is not sustained enough for the sophistication point.\n\nSample response, low score (2/6; Thesis 0, Evidence and commentary 2, Sophistication 0):\nLibraries are an important part of any town and the people of Harlow Valley have a hard choice to make. There are good points on both sides of whether to build one regional hub or keep the four small libraries. In my opinion the towns should think carefully about what is best for everyone before they decide.\n\nSource B says that the old system is not working very well. The writer ran the Oskar Bend branch for nineteen years and says that Dreyfield's building has only two outlets and closes at four. He also says it costs 21.50 dollars per visit in Dreyfield and only 7.50 in Pennick. This shows that the small libraries are expensive and the hub would save money. Source F also shows the costs, with the hub at 11.40 which is in the middle.\n\nOn the other hand Source C is about Dreyfield and it describes kids coming in at three-fifteen and a woman who has no car. The writer says a shuttle is a schedule and a schedule is a reason not to go. This is a good point because lots of people would not be able to get to the hub. Source E has a table that shows the percent of people who would use the hub is lower in Dreyfield, 14 percent, than in Pennick. So distance is a big problem for the hub.\n\nSource D is a memo that says the hub can be recommended but only with conditions like a shuttle and reading rooms. This is a kind of middle option and it costs some money but the saving is still there.\n\nOverall both sides have a point. The hub has longer hours and more staff but people far away might lose out. The towns should look at all of this and make the best choice for the valley.\n   - \"In my opinion the towns should think carefully about what is best for everyone before they decide.\" - No defensible position and no named condition. It says the question has two sides and tells towns to 'think carefully', which the rubric scores 0.\n   - \"There are good points on both sides of whether to build one regional hub or keep the four small libraries.\" - Restates the prompt and declines to choose a side, so the thesis point is not earned.\n   - \"This shows that the small libraries are expensive and the hub would save money.\" - Cites the 21.50 and 7.50 figures but the commentary is thin. It does not notice that the current system's overall cost per visit (1.9 million over 177,000) is lower than the hub's 11.40, so the 'save money' claim is not examined.\n   - \"So distance is a big problem for the hub.\" - Uses the 14 percent figure from Source E but the conclusion is a general claim. It does not connect to Source C's 33 percent monthly use or to car-free households.\n   - \"This is a kind of middle option and it costs some money but the saving is still there.\" - Source D is summarized with no figures (95,000, 85,000, 120,000 saving) and no explanation of how its conditions test the hub plan. The paragraph is a one-sentence summary, so evidence is mostly summarized source by source.\n   - \"Overall both sides have a point.\" - The conclusion repeats the 'two sides' framing. It does not explain why the tension exists, such as cost per visit versus access per person, or who bears the cost. No sophistication."},"set":{"explanation":"Scoring guide (ALTON practice rubric, not official College Board scoring). Each essay is worth 6 points.\nThesis (0-1): A conditional position counts as defensible only if it names the condition. 'Towns should consolidate only if they fund a way for people without cars to reach the hub' earns the point; 'Towns should consolidate if it makes sense for them' does not. A thesis that picks a stance but mislabels it (calling Source D an opponent, for instance) still earns the point; source accuracy is scored in Row B.\n   - 1: A thesis that takes a defensible position on whether small towns should replace several small libraries with one regional hub. The position may be a flat yes or no, a conditional yes (only with transit and local reading rooms, for example), or a limited position (hub for towns within a short distance but not for distant ones). The thesis may appear anywhere in the essay and may be more than one sentence.\n   - 0: No thesis, or a thesis that only restates the prompt, only summarizes what the sources say, or states that the question has two sides without choosing one. Statements like 'There are many factors a town should consider before closing libraries' earn 0.\nEvidence and Commentary (0-4): Sources A, E and F are informational and can support any stance; using them does not by itself show synthesis unless the essay explains what the number means for its argument. Counting the number of sources cited is not enough for 3 or 4: an essay that cites five sources in one sentence each with no commentary stays at 2. Correct arithmetic drawn from the sources (for example, that the current system's overall cost per visit, 1.9 million dollars over 177,000 visits, is actually lower than the hub's projected 11.40) counts as strong evidence use. Misreading a source in a way that reverses its meaning (claiming Source D opposes the hub outright, or that Source E shows Dreyfield residents rarely use their branch) caps the row at 2 unless the misreading is minor and isolated.\n   - 4: Uses specific, well-chosen evidence from at least three sources, consistently explains how each piece supports the line of reasoning, and builds the paragraphs into a developed argument rather than a list. The essay engages with evidence that complicates its position (for example, a pro-hub essay that confronts the survey drop-off in Source E, or an anti-hub essay that confronts the hours and staffing gap in Sources A and B) and explains why its position still holds.\n   - 3: Uses specific evidence from at least three sources to support a line of reasoning, and explains for most of that evidence how it supports the claim. The essay usually points to a particular figure, example or quotation (the 14 percent expected hub use in Dreyfield, the 21.50 dollar cost per visit, Marrow's three-fifteen scene) and says what it shows. Some commentary may still be generic or some evidence may be dropped in without explanation.\n   - 2: Uses evidence from at least three sources and connects at least some of it to the thesis, but the reasoning is thin or uneven: the essay may lean on one source and mention the others briefly, make general claims ('distance is a big problem') without pointing to the specific figures or examples that show it, or leave the reader to infer why a cited fact matters.\n   - 1: Uses evidence from at least two sources but mostly summarizes them in turn ('Source A says... Source B says...') with little or no explanation of how the evidence supports a position. Commentary, where present, repeats the evidence in other words.\n   - 0: Restates the thesis, offers no evidence from the sources, or uses evidence from fewer than two sources with no connection to any claim.\nSophistication (0-1): A strong candidate for this point is an essay that identifies that consolidation shifts who bears the cost (from taxpayers in dollars to outlying residents in distance) and argues from that insight. Another is an essay that uses Source D's conditions to test whether a 'both' solution is really a compromise or simply a smaller version of the status quo. Vocabulary alone, a vivid opening, or a closing call to 'remember what libraries are for' does not earn the point.\n   - 1: The essay does at least one of the following in a sustained way, not in a single sentence: explains why the tension exists (for example, that per-visit cost and per-person access measure different things, so Sources B and C are not actually disagreeing about facts); situates the argument in a wider context, such as what a public institution owes to the residents least able to travel; examines the limits of the evidence, such as noting that the hub visit projection is only a projection and the 'expect to visit' survey column records intention rather than behavior; or writes with a controlled, precise style that makes the reasoning clearer.\n   - 0: The argument is straightforward and does not complicate its own claims, treats the issue as a simple contest between savings and sentiment, or relies on sweeping statements and ornamental prose instead of precise thought. Merely mentioning 'the other side' before dismissing it does not earn the point.\nNote: Score the three rows independently; an essay can earn Row C without earning a 4 in Row B, and vice versa.\nNote: Essays that cite the sources by author name (Kavale, Marrow) instead of letter are acceptable.\nNote: An essay that uses only two sources cannot score above 1 in Row B regardless of quality.\nNote: Numbers in the sources are consistent with one another; an essay that notices and uses a relationship between two sources (Source B's cost figures with Source F, Source C's percentages with Source E) is doing synthesis and should be credited for it.\nNote: Do not penalize an essay for taking a position the grader finds unpersuasive; penalize only unsupported or self-contradicting reasoning.\nNote: Personal experience with a local library counts as evidence only when it is tied to the argument; it does not substitute for the three-source minimum.\n\nSample response, high score (6/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 4, Sophistication 1):\nWhen people argue about the Harlow Valley libraries, they usually talk as if the only question is money versus memory. The sources show something more uncomfortable: consolidation does not get rid of a cost, it moves it. The four towns should build the regional hub, but only if they fund Source D's shuttle and reading rooms in the same vote. Without those, the \"savings\" just get paid by the residents of Dreyfield and Lowen Mill in miles instead of by taxpayers in dollars.\n\nThe case for the hub is real, and Desmond Kavale makes it better than anyone because he actually ran one of the branches. His point is that the current system is already unequal. Pennick has \"the only children's librarian in the valley,\" while Dreyfield's building \"has two outlets and closes at four\" (Source B). Source A backs this up: Dreyfield is open twenty hours a week, and the hub would be open sixty-six. A teenager who has to borrow a car to take an online exam in Pennick is not being served by her local branch. So opponents who picture four thriving libraries are defending something that doesn't exist.\n\nBut Kavale's best number is also misleading. He says each Dreyfield visit costs 21.50 dollars and each Pennick visit costs 7.50 dollars, and Source F charts the hub at 11.40 dollars. That looks like an obvious win until you do the math for the whole system. The four branches cost 1.9 million dollars for 177,000 visits (Source A), which comes to about 10.73 dollars a visit. That is actually cheaper than the hub. The hub lowers the total bill, but it expects 37,000 fewer visits, so its cost per visit goes up rather than down. In other words, the savings come with fewer people using the library, not with more efficient service.\n\nWho are those missing people? Source E answers that. In Dreyfield, 33 percent of residents visit their branch monthly, but only 14 percent expect to visit the hub that often, and 18 percent of households there have no car. Meanwhile Pennick's number goes up, from 41 to 44. Ilse Marrow's scene at three-fifteen puts faces on these percentages: the middle schoolers, the retired men, and the woman with \"no computer and no car\" who fills out forms on the one public computer (Source C). Kavale and Marrow aren't really disagreeing about facts. Kavale measures cost per visit, and Marrow measures who can get in the door. An average like cost per visit cannot register who has stopped coming, because the people who are hardest to serve are exactly the ones who disappear from the count.\n\nThis is why Source D's conditions are not a watered-down compromise but the actual plan. The shuttle (95,000 dollars) and reading rooms (85,000 dollars) shrink the savings from 300,000 to 120,000, but they keep a public computer and a children's shelf within walking distance of Marrow's kids while also giving them a ride to a homework center their town could never afford. Marrow is right that \"a schedule is a reason not to go,\" which is why the reading room matters more than the shuttle. It keeps the walk-in version of the library alive. Someone could say this is just a smaller copy of the four-branch system. It isn't, though, because a twenty-hour room in a town hall doesn't need its own roof and furnace, and those are exactly the costs Kavale blames.\n\nThe review matters too, because so much of the case rests on guesses. The 140,000 visits is a projection, and \"expect to visit\" in Source E records what people wrote on a survey, not what they will do. A plan built on predictions should come with a promise to check them, and Source D's automatic extension makes that promise binding instead of hopeful.\n\nThe grant deadline makes it tempting to vote yes and work out the details later. The valley should vote yes, but only on the version where the details are paid for up front. Otherwise the towns with the fewest alternatives end up paying for a library they mostly can't reach.\n   - \"The four towns should build the regional hub, but only if they fund Source D's shuttle and reading rooms in the same vote.\" - Earns the point. This is a conditional thesis that names its condition (funded transit and local reading rooms, approved at the same time as construction), so it's defensible and specific, not a vague 'if it makes sense.'\n   - \"the \"savings\" just get paid by the residents of Dreyfield and Lowen Mill in miles instead of by taxpayers in dollars\" - Sets up the line of reasoning the rest of the essay follows: the cost doesn't disappear, it moves from taxpayers to outlying residents. Each body paragraph tests part of this claim.\n   - \"So opponents who picture four thriving libraries are defending something that doesn't exist.\" - Takes on evidence that cuts against caution about the hub (Kavale's staffing and outlets details, plus the 20 vs. 66 hours from Source A) and says what it shows instead of just listing it. Combining B and A here is synthesis.\n   - \"which comes to about 10.73 dollars a visit. That is actually cheaper than the hub.\" - Correct arithmetic from Source A (1.9 million / 177,000) checked against Source F's 11.40 dollars. The rubric names this kind of evidence use as strong. The essay then explains what it means: the savings come with 37,000 fewer projected visits.\n   - \"In Dreyfield, 33 percent of residents visit their branch monthly, but only 14 percent expect to visit the hub that often, and 18 percent of households there have no car.\" - Uses specific figures from Source E accurately, ties them to Marrow's three-fifteen scene in Source C, and links both back to the 'missing visits' point. This is developed reasoning rather than a list of sources.\n   - \"Someone could say this is just a smaller copy of the four-branch system.\" - Brings up a real objection to its own conditional position and answers it with evidence: reading rooms avoid the fixed building costs Kavale points to in Source B. Taking on complicating evidence like this supports a 4.\n   - \"Kavale and Marrow aren't really disagreeing about facts. Kavale measures cost per visit, and Marrow measures who can get in the door.\" - Explains why the tension exists: the two sources use different measures (cost per visit vs. access per person). This is one of the sophistication moves the rubric names.\n   - \"\"expect to visit\" in Source E records what people wrote on a survey, not what they will do\" - Looks at the limits of the evidence (intention vs. behavior, a projection vs. actual numbers) and uses that to argue the review must be binding. Together with the cost-shift framing that runs through the essay, this makes the sophistication sustained rather than a one-sentence gesture.\n   - \"Otherwise the towns with the fewest alternatives end up paying for a library they mostly can't reach.\" - The conclusion goes back to the cost-shift insight instead of a generic 'remember what libraries are for' ending. The essay argues from that insight from start to finish.\n\nSample response, middle score (5/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 4, Sophistication 0):\nThe four towns of Harlow Valley should build the regional hub, but only if they also pay for a shuttle and keep small reading rooms open in the towns that lose their branches. Without those two things the hub would help Pennick and leave the other towns worse off than they are now.\n\nThe best argument for the hub is the one Desmond Kavale makes in Source B. He points out that the four branches are already unequal, since Pennick has the new books, the evening hours and the only children's librarian. Source F backs him up with numbers, because a visit to the Dreyfield branch costs 21.50 dollars and a visit to Pennick costs 7.50. The hub is projected to cost 11.40 per visit, which is a lot less than Dreyfield or Lowen Mill. Kavale also says that closing the branches would free up about 300,000 dollars a year for specialists. This shows that keeping four small buildings open means paying for roofs and furnaces instead of for staff and services, so the hub would be a better use of money for the valley.\n\nHowever, cost per visit does not tell the whole story. If you take the whole system, 1.9 million dollars divided by 177,000 visits is about 10.70 per visit, which is actually less than the hub's projected 11.40. So the hub is not clearly cheaper per visit, and its savings come mostly from having fewer buildings. More important, the people who would lose the most are the ones with the fewest options. Source E shows that only 14 percent of Dreyfield residents expect to use the hub monthly, while 33 percent use their branch that often now, and 18 percent of Dreyfield households have no car. Ilse Marrow in Source C puts a face on those numbers with the woman from the apartments who uses the one public computer because she has no car. Marrow says a shuttle is \"a schedule, and a schedule is a reason not to go,\" and that is a fair point, since Dreyfield is fourteen miles from the hub according to Source A.\n\nThat is why I think Source D has the right answer. The Office of Shared Services does not reject the hub, it says the hub can be recommended with three funded conditions, a shuttle, staffed reading rooms in Dreyfield and Lowen Mill, and a review after three years. These conditions cut the savings from 300,000 to about 120,000 dollars, but that is the price of making the hub serve everyone and not just Pennick. Marrow is right that a shuttle is not the same as a walk, but the reading rooms give people a place in town, which answers her concern better than a shuttle alone could.\n\nIn the end, the question is not only about money. Consolidation saves the taxpayers money, but it moves the cost onto people who have to travel, and that is only fair if the towns pay to reduce that burden. The hub with the conditions is worth building, and the hub without them is not.\n   - \"should build the regional hub, but only if they also pay for a shuttle and keep small reading rooms open in the towns that lose their branches\" - Clear conditional thesis that names its conditions (shuttle and local reading rooms), so it earns the thesis point.\n   - \"a visit to the Dreyfield branch costs 21.50 dollars and a visit to Pennick costs 7.50\" - Specific figures from Sources B and F, tied to the claim that small branches are inefficient. This is good evidence, though the commentary after it is fairly general.\n   - \"1.9 million dollars divided by 177,000 visits is about 10.70 per visit, which is actually less than the hub's projected 11.40\" - Correct arithmetic combining Sources A and F, and it complicates the pro-hub case. The rubric names this as strong evidence use.\n   - \"only 14 percent of Dreyfield residents expect to use the hub monthly, while 33 percent use their branch that often now\" - Uses Source E and the Marrow figures accurately to show the drop-off in Dreyfield. It supports the thesis, but the essay does not look at whether 'expect' is only an intention.\n   - \"the woman from the apartments who uses the one public computer because she has no car\" - Connects Marrow's example to the Source E car-ownership figure, but the explanation is brief and a little repetitive.\n   - \"Consolidation saves the taxpayers money, but it moves the cost onto people who have to travel\" - Names the shift of cost from taxpayers to outlying residents. It appears only in the conclusion and is not developed through the essay, so it is not sustained enough for the sophistication point.\n\nSample response, low score (2/6; Thesis 0, Evidence and commentary 2, Sophistication 0):\nLibraries are an important part of any town and the people of Harlow Valley have a hard choice to make. There are good points on both sides of whether to build one regional hub or keep the four small libraries. In my opinion the towns should think carefully about what is best for everyone before they decide.\n\nSource B says that the old system is not working very well. The writer ran the Oskar Bend branch for nineteen years and says that Dreyfield's building has only two outlets and closes at four. He also says it costs 21.50 dollars per visit in Dreyfield and only 7.50 in Pennick. This shows that the small libraries are expensive and the hub would save money. Source F also shows the costs, with the hub at 11.40 which is in the middle.\n\nOn the other hand Source C is about Dreyfield and it describes kids coming in at three-fifteen and a woman who has no car. The writer says a shuttle is a schedule and a schedule is a reason not to go. This is a good point because lots of people would not be able to get to the hub. Source E has a table that shows the percent of people who would use the hub is lower in Dreyfield, 14 percent, than in Pennick. So distance is a big problem for the hub.\n\nSource D is a memo that says the hub can be recommended but only with conditions like a shuttle and reading rooms. This is a kind of middle option and it costs some money but the saving is still there.\n\nOverall both sides have a point. The hub has longer hours and more staff but people far away might lose out. The towns should look at all of this and make the best choice for the valley.\n   - \"In my opinion the towns should think carefully about what is best for everyone before they decide.\" - No defensible position and no named condition. It says the question has two sides and tells towns to 'think carefully', which the rubric scores 0.\n   - \"There are good points on both sides of whether to build one regional hub or keep the four small libraries.\" - Restates the prompt and declines to choose a side, so the thesis point is not earned.\n   - \"This shows that the small libraries are expensive and the hub would save money.\" - Cites the 21.50 and 7.50 figures but the commentary is thin. It does not notice that the current system's overall cost per visit (1.9 million over 177,000) is lower than the hub's 11.40, so the 'save money' claim is not examined.\n   - \"So distance is a big problem for the hub.\" - Uses the 14 percent figure from Source E but the conclusion is a general claim. It does not connect to Source C's 33 percent monthly use or to car-free households.\n   - \"This is a kind of middle option and it costs some money but the saving is still there.\" - Source D is summarized with no figures (95,000, 85,000, 120,000 saving) and no explanation of how its conditions test the hub plan. The paragraph is a one-sentence summary, so evidence is mostly summarized source by source.\n   - \"Overall both sides have a point.\" - The conclusion repeats the 'two sides' framing. It does not explain why the tension exists, such as cost per visit versus access per person, or who bears the cost. No sophistication.","explanation_en":"Scoring guide (ALTON practice rubric, not official College Board scoring). Each essay is worth 6 points.\nThesis (0-1): A conditional position counts as defensible only if it names the condition. 'Towns should consolidate only if they fund a way for people without cars to reach the hub' earns the point; 'Towns should consolidate if it makes sense for them' does not. A thesis that picks a stance but mislabels it (calling Source D an opponent, for instance) still earns the point; source accuracy is scored in Row B.\n   - 1: A thesis that takes a defensible position on whether small towns should replace several small libraries with one regional hub. The position may be a flat yes or no, a conditional yes (only with transit and local reading rooms, for example), or a limited position (hub for towns within a short distance but not for distant ones). The thesis may appear anywhere in the essay and may be more than one sentence.\n   - 0: No thesis, or a thesis that only restates the prompt, only summarizes what the sources say, or states that the question has two sides without choosing one. Statements like 'There are many factors a town should consider before closing libraries' earn 0.\nEvidence and Commentary (0-4): Sources A, E and F are informational and can support any stance; using them does not by itself show synthesis unless the essay explains what the number means for its argument. Counting the number of sources cited is not enough for 3 or 4: an essay that cites five sources in one sentence each with no commentary stays at 2. Correct arithmetic drawn from the sources (for example, that the current system's overall cost per visit, 1.9 million dollars over 177,000 visits, is actually lower than the hub's projected 11.40) counts as strong evidence use. Misreading a source in a way that reverses its meaning (claiming Source D opposes the hub outright, or that Source E shows Dreyfield residents rarely use their branch) caps the row at 2 unless the misreading is minor and isolated.\n   - 4: Uses specific, well-chosen evidence from at least three sources, consistently explains how each piece supports the line of reasoning, and builds the paragraphs into a developed argument rather than a list. The essay engages with evidence that complicates its position (for example, a pro-hub essay that confronts the survey drop-off in Source E, or an anti-hub essay that confronts the hours and staffing gap in Sources A and B) and explains why its position still holds.\n   - 3: Uses specific evidence from at least three sources to support a line of reasoning, and explains for most of that evidence how it supports the claim. The essay usually points to a particular figure, example or quotation (the 14 percent expected hub use in Dreyfield, the 21.50 dollar cost per visit, Marrow's three-fifteen scene) and says what it shows. Some commentary may still be generic or some evidence may be dropped in without explanation.\n   - 2: Uses evidence from at least three sources and connects at least some of it to the thesis, but the reasoning is thin or uneven: the essay may lean on one source and mention the others briefly, make general claims ('distance is a big problem') without pointing to the specific figures or examples that show it, or leave the reader to infer why a cited fact matters.\n   - 1: Uses evidence from at least two sources but mostly summarizes them in turn ('Source A says... Source B says...') with little or no explanation of how the evidence supports a position. Commentary, where present, repeats the evidence in other words.\n   - 0: Restates the thesis, offers no evidence from the sources, or uses evidence from fewer than two sources with no connection to any claim.\nSophistication (0-1): A strong candidate for this point is an essay that identifies that consolidation shifts who bears the cost (from taxpayers in dollars to outlying residents in distance) and argues from that insight. Another is an essay that uses Source D's conditions to test whether a 'both' solution is really a compromise or simply a smaller version of the status quo. Vocabulary alone, a vivid opening, or a closing call to 'remember what libraries are for' does not earn the point.\n   - 1: The essay does at least one of the following in a sustained way, not in a single sentence: explains why the tension exists (for example, that per-visit cost and per-person access measure different things, so Sources B and C are not actually disagreeing about facts); situates the argument in a wider context, such as what a public institution owes to the residents least able to travel; examines the limits of the evidence, such as noting that the hub visit projection is only a projection and the 'expect to visit' survey column records intention rather than behavior; or writes with a controlled, precise style that makes the reasoning clearer.\n   - 0: The argument is straightforward and does not complicate its own claims, treats the issue as a simple contest between savings and sentiment, or relies on sweeping statements and ornamental prose instead of precise thought. Merely mentioning 'the other side' before dismissing it does not earn the point.\nNote: Score the three rows independently; an essay can earn Row C without earning a 4 in Row B, and vice versa.\nNote: Essays that cite the sources by author name (Kavale, Marrow) instead of letter are acceptable.\nNote: An essay that uses only two sources cannot score above 1 in Row B regardless of quality.\nNote: Numbers in the sources are consistent with one another; an essay that notices and uses a relationship between two sources (Source B's cost figures with Source F, Source C's percentages with Source E) is doing synthesis and should be credited for it.\nNote: Do not penalize an essay for taking a position the grader finds unpersuasive; penalize only unsupported or self-contradicting reasoning.\nNote: Personal experience with a local library counts as evidence only when it is tied to the argument; it does not substitute for the three-source minimum.\n\nSample response, high score (6/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 4, Sophistication 1):\nWhen people argue about the Harlow Valley libraries, they usually talk as if the only question is money versus memory. The sources show something more uncomfortable: consolidation does not get rid of a cost, it moves it. The four towns should build the regional hub, but only if they fund Source D's shuttle and reading rooms in the same vote. Without those, the \"savings\" just get paid by the residents of Dreyfield and Lowen Mill in miles instead of by taxpayers in dollars.\n\nThe case for the hub is real, and Desmond Kavale makes it better than anyone because he actually ran one of the branches. His point is that the current system is already unequal. Pennick has \"the only children's librarian in the valley,\" while Dreyfield's building \"has two outlets and closes at four\" (Source B). Source A backs this up: Dreyfield is open twenty hours a week, and the hub would be open sixty-six. A teenager who has to borrow a car to take an online exam in Pennick is not being served by her local branch. So opponents who picture four thriving libraries are defending something that doesn't exist.\n\nBut Kavale's best number is also misleading. He says each Dreyfield visit costs 21.50 dollars and each Pennick visit costs 7.50 dollars, and Source F charts the hub at 11.40 dollars. That looks like an obvious win until you do the math for the whole system. The four branches cost 1.9 million dollars for 177,000 visits (Source A), which comes to about 10.73 dollars a visit. That is actually cheaper than the hub. The hub lowers the total bill, but it expects 37,000 fewer visits, so its cost per visit goes up rather than down. In other words, the savings come with fewer people using the library, not with more efficient service.\n\nWho are those missing people? Source E answers that. In Dreyfield, 33 percent of residents visit their branch monthly, but only 14 percent expect to visit the hub that often, and 18 percent of households there have no car. Meanwhile Pennick's number goes up, from 41 to 44. Ilse Marrow's scene at three-fifteen puts faces on these percentages: the middle schoolers, the retired men, and the woman with \"no computer and no car\" who fills out forms on the one public computer (Source C). Kavale and Marrow aren't really disagreeing about facts. Kavale measures cost per visit, and Marrow measures who can get in the door. An average like cost per visit cannot register who has stopped coming, because the people who are hardest to serve are exactly the ones who disappear from the count.\n\nThis is why Source D's conditions are not a watered-down compromise but the actual plan. The shuttle (95,000 dollars) and reading rooms (85,000 dollars) shrink the savings from 300,000 to 120,000, but they keep a public computer and a children's shelf within walking distance of Marrow's kids while also giving them a ride to a homework center their town could never afford. Marrow is right that \"a schedule is a reason not to go,\" which is why the reading room matters more than the shuttle. It keeps the walk-in version of the library alive. Someone could say this is just a smaller copy of the four-branch system. It isn't, though, because a twenty-hour room in a town hall doesn't need its own roof and furnace, and those are exactly the costs Kavale blames.\n\nThe review matters too, because so much of the case rests on guesses. The 140,000 visits is a projection, and \"expect to visit\" in Source E records what people wrote on a survey, not what they will do. A plan built on predictions should come with a promise to check them, and Source D's automatic extension makes that promise binding instead of hopeful.\n\nThe grant deadline makes it tempting to vote yes and work out the details later. The valley should vote yes, but only on the version where the details are paid for up front. Otherwise the towns with the fewest alternatives end up paying for a library they mostly can't reach.\n   - \"The four towns should build the regional hub, but only if they fund Source D's shuttle and reading rooms in the same vote.\" - Earns the point. This is a conditional thesis that names its condition (funded transit and local reading rooms, approved at the same time as construction), so it's defensible and specific, not a vague 'if it makes sense.'\n   - \"the \"savings\" just get paid by the residents of Dreyfield and Lowen Mill in miles instead of by taxpayers in dollars\" - Sets up the line of reasoning the rest of the essay follows: the cost doesn't disappear, it moves from taxpayers to outlying residents. Each body paragraph tests part of this claim.\n   - \"So opponents who picture four thriving libraries are defending something that doesn't exist.\" - Takes on evidence that cuts against caution about the hub (Kavale's staffing and outlets details, plus the 20 vs. 66 hours from Source A) and says what it shows instead of just listing it. Combining B and A here is synthesis.\n   - \"which comes to about 10.73 dollars a visit. That is actually cheaper than the hub.\" - Correct arithmetic from Source A (1.9 million / 177,000) checked against Source F's 11.40 dollars. The rubric names this kind of evidence use as strong. The essay then explains what it means: the savings come with 37,000 fewer projected visits.\n   - \"In Dreyfield, 33 percent of residents visit their branch monthly, but only 14 percent expect to visit the hub that often, and 18 percent of households there have no car.\" - Uses specific figures from Source E accurately, ties them to Marrow's three-fifteen scene in Source C, and links both back to the 'missing visits' point. This is developed reasoning rather than a list of sources.\n   - \"Someone could say this is just a smaller copy of the four-branch system.\" - Brings up a real objection to its own conditional position and answers it with evidence: reading rooms avoid the fixed building costs Kavale points to in Source B. Taking on complicating evidence like this supports a 4.\n   - \"Kavale and Marrow aren't really disagreeing about facts. Kavale measures cost per visit, and Marrow measures who can get in the door.\" - Explains why the tension exists: the two sources use different measures (cost per visit vs. access per person). This is one of the sophistication moves the rubric names.\n   - \"\"expect to visit\" in Source E records what people wrote on a survey, not what they will do\" - Looks at the limits of the evidence (intention vs. behavior, a projection vs. actual numbers) and uses that to argue the review must be binding. Together with the cost-shift framing that runs through the essay, this makes the sophistication sustained rather than a one-sentence gesture.\n   - \"Otherwise the towns with the fewest alternatives end up paying for a library they mostly can't reach.\" - The conclusion goes back to the cost-shift insight instead of a generic 'remember what libraries are for' ending. The essay argues from that insight from start to finish.\n\nSample response, middle score (5/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 4, Sophistication 0):\nThe four towns of Harlow Valley should build the regional hub, but only if they also pay for a shuttle and keep small reading rooms open in the towns that lose their branches. Without those two things the hub would help Pennick and leave the other towns worse off than they are now.\n\nThe best argument for the hub is the one Desmond Kavale makes in Source B. He points out that the four branches are already unequal, since Pennick has the new books, the evening hours and the only children's librarian. Source F backs him up with numbers, because a visit to the Dreyfield branch costs 21.50 dollars and a visit to Pennick costs 7.50. The hub is projected to cost 11.40 per visit, which is a lot less than Dreyfield or Lowen Mill. Kavale also says that closing the branches would free up about 300,000 dollars a year for specialists. This shows that keeping four small buildings open means paying for roofs and furnaces instead of for staff and services, so the hub would be a better use of money for the valley.\n\nHowever, cost per visit does not tell the whole story. If you take the whole system, 1.9 million dollars divided by 177,000 visits is about 10.70 per visit, which is actually less than the hub's projected 11.40. So the hub is not clearly cheaper per visit, and its savings come mostly from having fewer buildings. More important, the people who would lose the most are the ones with the fewest options. Source E shows that only 14 percent of Dreyfield residents expect to use the hub monthly, while 33 percent use their branch that often now, and 18 percent of Dreyfield households have no car. Ilse Marrow in Source C puts a face on those numbers with the woman from the apartments who uses the one public computer because she has no car. Marrow says a shuttle is \"a schedule, and a schedule is a reason not to go,\" and that is a fair point, since Dreyfield is fourteen miles from the hub according to Source A.\n\nThat is why I think Source D has the right answer. The Office of Shared Services does not reject the hub, it says the hub can be recommended with three funded conditions, a shuttle, staffed reading rooms in Dreyfield and Lowen Mill, and a review after three years. These conditions cut the savings from 300,000 to about 120,000 dollars, but that is the price of making the hub serve everyone and not just Pennick. Marrow is right that a shuttle is not the same as a walk, but the reading rooms give people a place in town, which answers her concern better than a shuttle alone could.\n\nIn the end, the question is not only about money. Consolidation saves the taxpayers money, but it moves the cost onto people who have to travel, and that is only fair if the towns pay to reduce that burden. The hub with the conditions is worth building, and the hub without them is not.\n   - \"should build the regional hub, but only if they also pay for a shuttle and keep small reading rooms open in the towns that lose their branches\" - Clear conditional thesis that names its conditions (shuttle and local reading rooms), so it earns the thesis point.\n   - \"a visit to the Dreyfield branch costs 21.50 dollars and a visit to Pennick costs 7.50\" - Specific figures from Sources B and F, tied to the claim that small branches are inefficient. This is good evidence, though the commentary after it is fairly general.\n   - \"1.9 million dollars divided by 177,000 visits is about 10.70 per visit, which is actually less than the hub's projected 11.40\" - Correct arithmetic combining Sources A and F, and it complicates the pro-hub case. The rubric names this as strong evidence use.\n   - \"only 14 percent of Dreyfield residents expect to use the hub monthly, while 33 percent use their branch that often now\" - Uses Source E and the Marrow figures accurately to show the drop-off in Dreyfield. It supports the thesis, but the essay does not look at whether 'expect' is only an intention.\n   - \"the woman from the apartments who uses the one public computer because she has no car\" - Connects Marrow's example to the Source E car-ownership figure, but the explanation is brief and a little repetitive.\n   - \"Consolidation saves the taxpayers money, but it moves the cost onto people who have to travel\" - Names the shift of cost from taxpayers to outlying residents. It appears only in the conclusion and is not developed through the essay, so it is not sustained enough for the sophistication point.\n\nSample response, low score (2/6; Thesis 0, Evidence and commentary 2, Sophistication 0):\nLibraries are an important part of any town and the people of Harlow Valley have a hard choice to make. There are good points on both sides of whether to build one regional hub or keep the four small libraries. In my opinion the towns should think carefully about what is best for everyone before they decide.\n\nSource B says that the old system is not working very well. The writer ran the Oskar Bend branch for nineteen years and says that Dreyfield's building has only two outlets and closes at four. He also says it costs 21.50 dollars per visit in Dreyfield and only 7.50 in Pennick. This shows that the small libraries are expensive and the hub would save money. Source F also shows the costs, with the hub at 11.40 which is in the middle.\n\nOn the other hand Source C is about Dreyfield and it describes kids coming in at three-fifteen and a woman who has no car. The writer says a shuttle is a schedule and a schedule is a reason not to go. This is a good point because lots of people would not be able to get to the hub. Source E has a table that shows the percent of people who would use the hub is lower in Dreyfield, 14 percent, than in Pennick. So distance is a big problem for the hub.\n\nSource D is a memo that says the hub can be recommended but only with conditions like a shuttle and reading rooms. This is a kind of middle option and it costs some money but the saving is still there.\n\nOverall both sides have a point. The hub has longer hours and more staff but people far away might lose out. The towns should look at all of this and make the best choice for the valley.\n   - \"In my opinion the towns should think carefully about what is best for everyone before they decide.\" - No defensible position and no named condition. It says the question has two sides and tells towns to 'think carefully', which the rubric scores 0.\n   - \"There are good points on both sides of whether to build one regional hub or keep the four small libraries.\" - Restates the prompt and declines to choose a side, so the thesis point is not earned.\n   - \"This shows that the small libraries are expensive and the hub would save money.\" - Cites the 21.50 and 7.50 figures but the commentary is thin. It does not notice that the current system's overall cost per visit (1.9 million over 177,000) is lower than the hub's 11.40, so the 'save money' claim is not examined.\n   - \"So distance is a big problem for the hub.\" - Uses the 14 percent figure from Source E but the conclusion is a general claim. It does not connect to Source C's 33 percent monthly use or to car-free households.\n   - \"This is a kind of middle option and it costs some money but the saving is still there.\" - Source D is summarized with no figures (95,000, 85,000, 120,000 saving) and no explanation of how its conditions test the hub plan. The paragraph is a one-sentence summary, so evidence is mostly summarized source by source.\n   - \"Overall both sides have a point.\" - The conclusion repeats the 'two sides' framing. It does not explain why the tension exists, such as cost per visit versus access per person, or who bears the cost. No sophistication."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-15T15:30:39.091Z","issues":[],"alt":"Operating Cost per Visit, Current Branches and Proposed Hub. Bar chart. Horizontal axis: Library location. Vertical axis: Operating cost per visit (dollars). Pennick: 7.5, Oskar Bend: 12, Lowen Mill: 14, Dreyfield: 21.5, Proposed hub: 11.4."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R6-ELA2-FRQ1","fromVersionId":"8f799222-22a8-4988-b943-683c8057726e","fromVersionNo":1},"ap":{"candidate_key":"el-rv:ap_english_language-fa1-k0","item_index":0}},{"patch_id":"R6-ELA2-FRQ2","problem_id":"e5fb78ec-f380-4c98-9474-c3c81026102e","from_version_id":"d90bffc2-8f53-4f43-953b-33ae1613bf87","expect":{"explanation":"Scoring guide (ALTON practice rubric, not official College Board scoring). Each essay is worth 6 points.\nThesis (0-1): Acceptable theses identify at least one specific choice or pattern (the kitchen metaphor, the concession in paragraph 3, the Anna Belle anecdote, the shift from economic to moral framing, the piloting metaphor that frames the whole speech) and link it to the goal of defeating the rezoning. A thesis that lists devices with no claim about effect ('Osgood uses metaphor, statistics, and a story') is on the line; award the point only if the essay's thesis, wherever it appears, attaches those devices to a specific purpose; a generic purpose ('to persuade the town') does not count.\n   - 1: A defensible claim about how Osgood's choices work toward her purpose. The claim may be modest (for example, that she establishes herself as a practical insider and then redefines the wharf as infrastructure rather than a business) but it must name a rhetorical move or strategy and connect it to persuading the town to keep the wharf working. The thesis may appear anywhere in the essay and may be more than one sentence.\n   - 0: No thesis, or a thesis that only restates the prompt (for example, 'Osgood uses many rhetorical choices to persuade the town'), or a statement that merely summarizes what Osgood says about the wharf without making a claim about how she persuades. A thesis that takes a position on whether the wharf should be rezoned, rather than on Osgood's rhetoric, also earns 0.\nEvidence and Commentary (0-4): Choices that count as valid evidence include: the opening ethos and antithesis (sentences 1-4); the description of the brochure and the pointed absence (8-9); the concession and rebuttal (10-15); the kitchen metaphor and restaurant analogy (14-16); the enumeration of trades and the direct address 'half of you' (17); the cause-and-effect chain with 'Then' repetition and the two clipped sentences (19-20) before the longer sentence about the kids (21); the statistics and their scaling to a town of six thousand (22-23); the tone shift and anecdote (24-30), including the short sentences 'It cannot. Nobody is aboard.'; the rhetorical question and redefinition of the decision (31-33); the personification 'it answers back' and the parallel verbs (35-36); the closing metaphor of bringing a town in (38-39) and the conciliatory offer about the culvert (41). Essays need not use these labels; what matters is accurate description of the move and explanation of its effect. Merely noting that Osgood 'uses numbers' is weak; noting that she volunteers the unflattering numbers first (12) so the favorable ones (22) land as honest counts for more.\n   - 4: Specific, well-chosen evidence from across the passage supports a sustained line of reasoning. Commentary consistently explains how choices function for this audience: for example, why the concession about the ice house and fuel dock makes the later kitchen metaphor credible, why the Anna Belle story is placed after the numbers rather than before, or how the closing piloting metaphor returns to the ethos of the opening. The essay shows how choices work together rather than as a checklist.\n   - 3: Specific evidence from several parts of the passage, with commentary that explains how at least two or three choices serve the purpose in ways tied to this audience and occasion. The essay has a line of reasoning, but one or more body paragraphs may slip into summary, or the analysis may be uneven (strong on the anecdote, generic on the opening ethos).\n   - 2: Quotes or closely references specific moments and names the choices accurately, but commentary is thin or repetitive: it asserts that a choice 'makes the audience feel' or 'helps her argument' without explaining the mechanism. Typical 2 essays treat each device in isolation and do not connect them to the exigence (an imminent council vote on a developer's proposal).\n   - 1: Mostly summary or paraphrase of Osgood's points about the wharf with little attention to how she says them. Evidence, if quoted, is general and commentary consists of labels ('this is pathos') with no explanation of effect on the town-meeting audience.\n   - 0: Restates the thesis, summarizes the speech, or offers evidence that is not from the passage.\nSophistication (0-1): A strong candidate for this point notices that Osgood never attacks the developers, that she borrows the brochure's own silence (no forklifts) as her strongest exhibit, or that the Anna Belle story quietly reframes the question from money to safety and who is physically present on the water. Do not award the point for simply using the word 'juxtaposition' or for an eloquent conclusion attached to an otherwise formulaic essay.\n   - 1: The essay does one or more of the following convincingly and throughout rather than in a single sentence: explains how the structure of the speech works as a whole (the movement from credibility, to concession, to economics, to the rescue story, to redefinition, to the closing ask); examines tensions in Osgood's rhetoric, such as the risk that a retired pilot's nostalgia could read as self-interest and how she heads that off by conceding the fleet's decline and ending with an offer to solve the culvert problem; situates the speech in its rhetorical situation by considering what council members and residents who favor the tax revenue would need to hear; or writes with a consistently precise and controlled prose style.\n   - 0: Competent analysis that stays at the level of device plus effect, or an essay whose attempts at complexity are decorative (ornate vocabulary, sweeping claims about 'society') rather than analytical.\nNote: Score holistically within each row; an essay that earns 4 in Row B will almost always also earn the thesis point, but check that the claim is about rhetoric, not about the merits of rezoning.\nNote: Students may describe the choices without technical names. 'She stacks three short sentences so the loss sounds like dominoes falling' is full-credit commentary on sentences 19-21 even without the word 'repetition'.\nNote: Essays that argue Osgood is wrong or right about the wharf are off task; score them only on whatever rhetorical analysis they contain.\nNote: An essay built on one well-analyzed section (for example, only paragraph 5) can reach a 3 in Row B if the commentary is rich, but reaching 4 requires evidence from more than one part of the speech.\nNote: Misreadings that distort the passage (for example, claiming Osgood opposes any change or that she dismisses the town's money problems) cap Row B at 2 because the commentary rests on a false account of the text.\n\nSample response, high score (6/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 4, Sophistication 1):\nWhen Marguerite Osgood stood up at the Tillet's Reach town meeting, she was speaking to an audience that already knew the wharf was losing money. The developer's proposal had a simple pitch: handsome condos and cafes, and the tax money that comes with them. Osgood could not win by pretending those numbers away. Instead she builds her speech like one of her channel crossings, carefully and in order. First she earns trust as a practical insider who admits the wharf's weaknesses. Then she redefines the wharf from a failing business into infrastructure the town cannot function without. Finally she shifts the question from money to who is actually on the water when something goes wrong. By the end, a vote that looked like a budget decision feels like a decision about what kind of town Tillet's Reach wants to be.\n\nOsgood opens by shrinking her own authority in a way that actually increases it. \"I am not a planner, a developer, or an economist. I am a woman who knows where the rocks are.\" Listing what she is not lets her step out of the expert argument the council expects, and the plain image of the rocks suggests she knows dangers the planners don't. For an audience of neighbors, someone who climbed \"a rope ladder\" for thirty-one years is probably more trustworthy than a developer. Then, instead of mocking the brochure, she compliments it: \"It is handsome.\" She never attacks the developers at all. She uses their own drawing as evidence, pointing out that it shows \"not a single forklift, fuel barge, or crab pot.\" The brochure's silence becomes her strongest exhibit, because the audience can see for themselves what the plan leaves out.\n\nThe most important move comes in paragraph 3, where Osgood concedes the facts the other side would use against her. The ice house closed, the fuel dock \"loses money three months a year,\" and the town \"cannot fix the culvert on Pell Street.\" By saying these things first, she takes them away from her opponents and shows the council members who want the tax money that she is not just being nostalgic. That concession is what makes the kitchen metaphor work: \"It is not a gold mine. It is a kitchen.\" Nobody judges a kitchen by whether it turns a profit by itself; they judge it by whether the restaurant can run without it. The metaphor changes the standard the wharf is measured by. She then shows the consequences as a chain: \"Then the mechanic goes. Then the welder.\" The sentences get shorter as the town loses more, so the first losses fall like dominoes, and the longer final sentence about the kids who leave carries the damage past the shop floor. And because she already admitted the bad numbers, the good ones, \"four million dollars\" and \"one hundred and forty jobs,\" sound like honest counts and not a sales pitch.\n\nOsgood saves the Anna Belle story for after the economics, and the placement is deliberate. She even flags the shift, calling it \"something now that is harder to put in a brochure.\" Once the council has heard that the wharf is worth money, she shows that its real value can't be counted. Dale Cuttery reached the trawler in eleven minutes \"because they were already there.\" Then come two blunt sentences: \"It cannot. Nobody is aboard.\" The image of empty shrink-wrapped sailboats quietly turns the debate from profit to safety, and from what the waterfront looks like to who is physically present on it. It is hard to argue for a boardwalk after that without sounding like you'd trade a rescue for a view.\n\nFinally, Osgood returns to where she started. \"I have brought a great many ships safely in\" sets up her wish \"to help bring one town in too.\" The piloting metaphor ties the ethos of her opening to her request, so she sounds less like an opponent of progress and more like a guide through a dangerous channel. Even her last line, \"let us sit down and find another way to pay for the culvert,\" keeps the concession alive, so nobody in the room has to choose between the wharf and the culvert. Osgood persuades because she never asks the town to ignore its problems. She only asks it to see what the brochure left out.\n   - \"First she earns trust as a practical insider who admits the wharf's weaknesses. Then she redefines the wharf from a failing business into infrastructure the town cannot function without.\" - Row A: Defensible, specific thesis that names actual strategies (insider ethos, concession, redefinition as infrastructure, shift to safety) and links them to defeating the rezoning. The First/Then/Finally order also sets up the essay's line of reasoning.\n   - \"she builds her speech like one of her channel crossings, carefully and in order\" - Row C: Sets up the essay's lasting focus on how the whole speech is structured. The body paragraphs follow that sequence, from credibility to concession to economics to the rescue story to the closing ask, so the sophistication point rests on more than one line.\n   - \"Listing what she is not lets her step out of the expert argument the council expects\" - Row B: Explains how the antithesis in sentences 3-4 works on this particular audience instead of just labeling it as ethos.\n   - \"The brochure's silence becomes her strongest exhibit, because the audience can see for themselves what the plan leaves out.\" - Row C: Points out that Osgood turns the brochure's own omission into evidence and never attacks the developers. This is one of the insights the rubric names as a sign of sophistication.\n   - \"That concession is what makes the kitchen metaphor work\" - Row B: Shows how two choices work together. The concession in paragraph 3 makes the metaphor believable, so the essay goes beyond treating devices one by one like a checklist.\n   - \"The sentences get shorter as the town loses more, so the first losses fall like dominoes\" - Row B: Describes how sentence length and repetition create an effect in sentences 19-21. This is full-credit commentary even without technical terms.\n   - \"because she already admitted the bad numbers, the good ones, \"four million dollars\" and \"one hundred and forty jobs,\" sound like honest counts and not a sales pitch\" - Row B: Connects the order of the statistics (unflattering first, favorable later) to credibility. This is exactly the kind of mechanism the rubric rewards.\n   - \"Osgood saves the Anna Belle story for after the economics, and the placement is deliberate.\" - Row B: Explains why the story is placed where it is, which the rubric singles out for Row B commentary.\n   - \"quietly turns the debate from profit to safety, and from what the waterfront looks like to who is physically present on it\" - Row C: Recognizes the reframing from money to safety and to who is actually present on the water, which adds complexity to the reading.\n   - \"keeps the concession alive, so nobody in the room has to choose between the wharf and the culvert\" - Row C: Sustains the tension the essay has been tracing, which is how Osgood heads off the charge of nostalgia by taking the town's money problems seriously right through her closing offer.\n   - \"The piloting metaphor ties the ethos of her opening to her request\" - Row B: Links the closing metaphor back to the ethos of the opening, completing a sustained line of reasoning with evidence from across the passage.\n\nSample response, middle score (4/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 3, Sophistication 0):\nIn her speech to the town meeting, Marguerite Osgood tries to convince the council to vote down the rezoning by presenting herself as a practical person who knows the harbor, and then by redefining the north wharf as something the whole town needs instead of just a business that is losing money. She does this through a metaphor, a concession, and a story about a rescue, which together move the audience from thinking about money to thinking about what kind of town they want.\n\nOsgood begins by building her credibility. She says \"I am not a planner, a developer, or an economist. I am a woman who knows where the rocks are.\" By listing what she is not, she sounds humble and not like someone with an agenda, and the line about the rocks makes her sound like she has real experience. This matters because the audience is about to vote on a developer's plan, and she needs them to trust her over a glossy brochure. She also describes the brochure's pictures as having \"not a single forklift, fuel barge, or crab pot,\" which points out what the developer left out and makes the audience notice what would be lost.\n\nNext, Osgood makes a concession. She admits \"The fishing fleet is smaller than it was\" and that the ice house closed, and she says condominium taxes are \"real money.\" This helps her because the people who want the tax money will not feel attacked, and it makes her seem fair and honest. Then she uses her metaphor: \"It is not a gold mine. It is a kitchen.\" The kitchen idea works because everyone understands that a restaurant needs a kitchen even if the kitchen does not make money by itself. She then lists the mechanic, the welder, and the net patcher, and says \"half of you\" buy boots at the supply store, which speaks directly to the people in the room. The statistics about four million dollars and one hundred and forty jobs back this up and show the wharf is important to the economy.\n\nThe most powerful part is probably the Anna Belle story. Osgood changes her tone to say \"something now that is harder to put in a brochure,\" and tells how Dale Cuttery got to the trawler in eleven minutes. Her short sentences, \"It cannot. Nobody is aboard,\" make the point very clearly. This shows the wharf is also about safety and not only about money, which is something the condominiums can't replace.\n\nFinally, she asks \"what are we really deciding?\" and asks whether the waterfront \"belongs to the town or merely faces it.\" Her ending goes back to her piloting job by saying she wants to \"bring one town in too,\" and she offers to help find another way to pay for the culvert. This shows she is not just saying no but is willing to help. Overall, Osgood persuades by being trustworthy, fair, and by making the audience see the wharf differently.\n   - \"tries to convince the council to vote down the rezoning by presenting herself as a practical person who knows the harbor\" - Defensible claim: it names the strategies (practical credibility, then redefining the wharf as something the whole town needs) and ties them to defeating the rezoning, so the thesis point is earned.\n   - \"By listing what she is not, she sounds humble and not like someone with an agenda\" - Accurately names the move in sentences 3-4 and gives some effect, tying it to the vote on a developer's plan. The explanation stays fairly general and does not address the risk of seeming self-interested.\n   - \"which points out what the developer left out and makes the audience notice what would be lost\" - Notes the brochure's absence but stops short of explaining how Osgood turns the developer's own silence into her exhibit; this is a missed chance for deeper analysis.\n   - \"this helps her because the people who want the tax money will not feel attacked\" - Good link between the concession and the audience's situation. The commentary does not go on to explain how the concession makes the later kitchen metaphor and statistics more credible.\n   - \"The statistics about four million dollars and one hundred and forty jobs back this up and show the wharf is important to the economy.\" - Evidence is cited but commentary is thin; it says the numbers 'back this up' without noting that she gave the unflattering numbers first or scaled the jobs to a town of six thousand.\n   - \"This shows the wharf is also about safety and not only about money, which is something the condominiums can't replace.\" - Correctly identifies the shift from money to safety in the anecdote, but does not develop why the story comes after the economic argument or who is physically present on the water.\n   - \"Overall, Osgood persuades by being trustworthy, fair, and by making the audience see the wharf differently.\" - The conclusion repeats the thesis in general terms. The essay moves through devices in order and mostly treats them separately, so it does not show a sustained structural analysis that would earn sophistication.\n\nSample response, low score (2/6; Thesis 0, Evidence and commentary 2, Sophistication 0):\nMarguerite Osgood is a retired harbor pilot and she gives a speech to the town about the north wharf. She uses many rhetorical choices to persuade the town to not rezone the wharf and keep it a working waterfront.\n\nIn the first paragraph she tells about her job climbing a rope ladder for thirty-one years. This shows she has experience and the audience will trust her. She says \"I am a woman who knows where the rocks are.\" This makes her sound like she knows what she is talking about so people will listen to her.\n\nNext she talks about the brochure from the developer. She says it is handsome and the pictures show cafes and sailboats but no forklifts. This is to show that the developer is leaving out the real working part of the town. Then she says the working wharf \"is not a gold mine. It is a kitchen.\" This is a metaphor that compares the wharf to a kitchen in a restaurant. A kitchen doesn't make money by itself but the restaurant needs it. This helps her argument because it makes the audience see the wharf is important.\n\nShe also uses facts and numbers like the co-op landing almost four million dollars of catch and one hundred and forty jobs. This is logos and it makes her argument stronger because it proves the wharf matters to the town's money.\n\nThen she tells a story about the Anna Belle that was in a gale. Dale Cuttery and the deckhands saved them in eleven minutes because they were already there. This is pathos and makes the audience feel emotional about the wharf. She says \"Nobody is aboard\" about the marina boats.\n\nIn the end she says to vote no on the rezoning. She also says they can find another way to pay for the culvert. Overall Osgood uses ethos, logos, and pathos to persuade the town and it works because it makes the audience agree with her.\n   - \"She uses many rhetorical choices to persuade the town to not rezone the wharf and keep it a working waterfront.\" - Only restates the prompt. It names no specific strategy and makes no claim about how the choices work, so it earns no thesis point.\n   - \"Overall Osgood uses ethos, logos, and pathos to persuade the town and it works because it makes the audience agree with her.\" - The conclusion falls back on a generic list of appeals. It still offers no defensible claim about this speech in particular.\n   - \"This shows she has experience and the audience will trust her.\" - Names ethos accurately but gives only a general effect. It never explains why a pilot's credibility matters to a town weighing tax revenue, or how the line about 'not a planner' works.\n   - \"This is to show that the developer is leaving out the real working part of the town.\" - Notices the brochure's absence of forklifts, which is a good choice of evidence. But the commentary is thin and misses that Osgood turns the brochure's own silence into her exhibit.\n   - \"A kitchen doesn't make money by itself but the restaurant needs it. This helps her argument because it makes the audience see the wharf is important.\" - Explains the metaphor partly. The commentary stops at 'helps her argument' and ignores that her concession in paragraph 3 makes the metaphor believable.\n   - \"This is logos and it makes her argument stronger because it proves the wharf matters to the town's money.\" - Treats the statistics as a label. It does not notice that she gives the unflattering numbers first or scales the figures to a town of six thousand.\n   - \"This is pathos and makes the audience feel emotional about the wharf.\" - Asserts that the audience feels something without explaining the mechanism. It misses that the story shifts the question from money to safety, and that the short sentences carry the force.\n   - \"In the end she says to vote no on the rezoning. She also says they can find another way to pay for the culvert.\" - Summarizes the ending without analysis. It does not discuss how the offer on the culvert eases the pressure from council members who want the tax money.\n   - \"Osgood is a retired harbor pilot and she gives a speech to the town about the north wharf.\" - The essay is a device-by-device checklist. It never looks at how the speech is structured or at the rhetorical situation, so there is no sophistication.","explanation_en":"Scoring guide (ALTON practice rubric, not official College Board scoring). Each essay is worth 6 points.\nThesis (0-1): Acceptable theses identify at least one specific choice or pattern (the kitchen metaphor, the concession in paragraph 3, the Anna Belle anecdote, the shift from economic to moral framing, the piloting metaphor that frames the whole speech) and link it to the goal of defeating the rezoning. A thesis that lists devices with no claim about effect ('Osgood uses metaphor, statistics, and a story') is on the line; award the point only if the essay's thesis, wherever it appears, attaches those devices to a specific purpose; a generic purpose ('to persuade the town') does not count.\n   - 1: A defensible claim about how Osgood's choices work toward her purpose. The claim may be modest (for example, that she establishes herself as a practical insider and then redefines the wharf as infrastructure rather than a business) but it must name a rhetorical move or strategy and connect it to persuading the town to keep the wharf working. The thesis may appear anywhere in the essay and may be more than one sentence.\n   - 0: No thesis, or a thesis that only restates the prompt (for example, 'Osgood uses many rhetorical choices to persuade the town'), or a statement that merely summarizes what Osgood says about the wharf without making a claim about how she persuades. A thesis that takes a position on whether the wharf should be rezoned, rather than on Osgood's rhetoric, also earns 0.\nEvidence and Commentary (0-4): Choices that count as valid evidence include: the opening ethos and antithesis (sentences 1-4); the description of the brochure and the pointed absence (8-9); the concession and rebuttal (10-15); the kitchen metaphor and restaurant analogy (14-16); the enumeration of trades and the direct address 'half of you' (17); the cause-and-effect chain with 'Then' repetition and the two clipped sentences (19-20) before the longer sentence about the kids (21); the statistics and their scaling to a town of six thousand (22-23); the tone shift and anecdote (24-30), including the short sentences 'It cannot. Nobody is aboard.'; the rhetorical question and redefinition of the decision (31-33); the personification 'it answers back' and the parallel verbs (35-36); the closing metaphor of bringing a town in (38-39) and the conciliatory offer about the culvert (41). Essays need not use these labels; what matters is accurate description of the move and explanation of its effect. Merely noting that Osgood 'uses numbers' is weak; noting that she volunteers the unflattering numbers first (12) so the favorable ones (22) land as honest counts for more.\n   - 4: Specific, well-chosen evidence from across the passage supports a sustained line of reasoning. Commentary consistently explains how choices function for this audience: for example, why the concession about the ice house and fuel dock makes the later kitchen metaphor credible, why the Anna Belle story is placed after the numbers rather than before, or how the closing piloting metaphor returns to the ethos of the opening. The essay shows how choices work together rather than as a checklist.\n   - 3: Specific evidence from several parts of the passage, with commentary that explains how at least two or three choices serve the purpose in ways tied to this audience and occasion. The essay has a line of reasoning, but one or more body paragraphs may slip into summary, or the analysis may be uneven (strong on the anecdote, generic on the opening ethos).\n   - 2: Quotes or closely references specific moments and names the choices accurately, but commentary is thin or repetitive: it asserts that a choice 'makes the audience feel' or 'helps her argument' without explaining the mechanism. Typical 2 essays treat each device in isolation and do not connect them to the exigence (an imminent council vote on a developer's proposal).\n   - 1: Mostly summary or paraphrase of Osgood's points about the wharf with little attention to how she says them. Evidence, if quoted, is general and commentary consists of labels ('this is pathos') with no explanation of effect on the town-meeting audience.\n   - 0: Restates the thesis, summarizes the speech, or offers evidence that is not from the passage.\nSophistication (0-1): A strong candidate for this point notices that Osgood never attacks the developers, that she borrows the brochure's own silence (no forklifts) as her strongest exhibit, or that the Anna Belle story quietly reframes the question from money to safety and who is physically present on the water. Do not award the point for simply using the word 'juxtaposition' or for an eloquent conclusion attached to an otherwise formulaic essay.\n   - 1: The essay does one or more of the following convincingly and throughout rather than in a single sentence: explains how the structure of the speech works as a whole (the movement from credibility, to concession, to economics, to the rescue story, to redefinition, to the closing ask); examines tensions in Osgood's rhetoric, such as the risk that a retired pilot's nostalgia could read as self-interest and how she heads that off by conceding the fleet's decline and ending with an offer to solve the culvert problem; situates the speech in its rhetorical situation by considering what council members and residents who favor the tax revenue would need to hear; or writes with a consistently precise and controlled prose style.\n   - 0: Competent analysis that stays at the level of device plus effect, or an essay whose attempts at complexity are decorative (ornate vocabulary, sweeping claims about 'society') rather than analytical.\nNote: Score holistically within each row; an essay that earns 4 in Row B will almost always also earn the thesis point, but check that the claim is about rhetoric, not about the merits of rezoning.\nNote: Students may describe the choices without technical names. 'She stacks three short sentences so the loss sounds like dominoes falling' is full-credit commentary on sentences 19-21 even without the word 'repetition'.\nNote: Essays that argue Osgood is wrong or right about the wharf are off task; score them only on whatever rhetorical analysis they contain.\nNote: An essay built on one well-analyzed section (for example, only paragraph 5) can reach a 3 in Row B if the commentary is rich, but reaching 4 requires evidence from more than one part of the speech.\nNote: Misreadings that distort the passage (for example, claiming Osgood opposes any change or that she dismisses the town's money problems) cap Row B at 2 because the commentary rests on a false account of the text.\n\nSample response, high score (6/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 4, Sophistication 1):\nWhen Marguerite Osgood stood up at the Tillet's Reach town meeting, she was speaking to an audience that already knew the wharf was losing money. The developer's proposal had a simple pitch: handsome condos and cafes, and the tax money that comes with them. Osgood could not win by pretending those numbers away. Instead she builds her speech like one of her channel crossings, carefully and in order. First she earns trust as a practical insider who admits the wharf's weaknesses. Then she redefines the wharf from a failing business into infrastructure the town cannot function without. Finally she shifts the question from money to who is actually on the water when something goes wrong. By the end, a vote that looked like a budget decision feels like a decision about what kind of town Tillet's Reach wants to be.\n\nOsgood opens by shrinking her own authority in a way that actually increases it. \"I am not a planner, a developer, or an economist. I am a woman who knows where the rocks are.\" Listing what she is not lets her step out of the expert argument the council expects, and the plain image of the rocks suggests she knows dangers the planners don't. For an audience of neighbors, someone who climbed \"a rope ladder\" for thirty-one years is probably more trustworthy than a developer. Then, instead of mocking the brochure, she compliments it: \"It is handsome.\" She never attacks the developers at all. She uses their own drawing as evidence, pointing out that it shows \"not a single forklift, fuel barge, or crab pot.\" The brochure's silence becomes her strongest exhibit, because the audience can see for themselves what the plan leaves out.\n\nThe most important move comes in paragraph 3, where Osgood concedes the facts the other side would use against her. The ice house closed, the fuel dock \"loses money three months a year,\" and the town \"cannot fix the culvert on Pell Street.\" By saying these things first, she takes them away from her opponents and shows the council members who want the tax money that she is not just being nostalgic. That concession is what makes the kitchen metaphor work: \"It is not a gold mine. It is a kitchen.\" Nobody judges a kitchen by whether it turns a profit by itself; they judge it by whether the restaurant can run without it. The metaphor changes the standard the wharf is measured by. She then shows the consequences as a chain: \"Then the mechanic goes. Then the welder.\" The sentences get shorter as the town loses more, so the first losses fall like dominoes, and the longer final sentence about the kids who leave carries the damage past the shop floor. And because she already admitted the bad numbers, the good ones, \"four million dollars\" and \"one hundred and forty jobs,\" sound like honest counts and not a sales pitch.\n\nOsgood saves the Anna Belle story for after the economics, and the placement is deliberate. She even flags the shift, calling it \"something now that is harder to put in a brochure.\" Once the council has heard that the wharf is worth money, she shows that its real value can't be counted. Dale Cuttery reached the trawler in eleven minutes \"because they were already there.\" Then come two blunt sentences: \"It cannot. Nobody is aboard.\" The image of empty shrink-wrapped sailboats quietly turns the debate from profit to safety, and from what the waterfront looks like to who is physically present on it. It is hard to argue for a boardwalk after that without sounding like you'd trade a rescue for a view.\n\nFinally, Osgood returns to where she started. \"I have brought a great many ships safely in\" sets up her wish \"to help bring one town in too.\" The piloting metaphor ties the ethos of her opening to her request, so she sounds less like an opponent of progress and more like a guide through a dangerous channel. Even her last line, \"let us sit down and find another way to pay for the culvert,\" keeps the concession alive, so nobody in the room has to choose between the wharf and the culvert. Osgood persuades because she never asks the town to ignore its problems. She only asks it to see what the brochure left out.\n   - \"First she earns trust as a practical insider who admits the wharf's weaknesses. Then she redefines the wharf from a failing business into infrastructure the town cannot function without.\" - Row A: Defensible, specific thesis that names actual strategies (insider ethos, concession, redefinition as infrastructure, shift to safety) and links them to defeating the rezoning. The First/Then/Finally order also sets up the essay's line of reasoning.\n   - \"she builds her speech like one of her channel crossings, carefully and in order\" - Row C: Sets up the essay's lasting focus on how the whole speech is structured. The body paragraphs follow that sequence, from credibility to concession to economics to the rescue story to the closing ask, so the sophistication point rests on more than one line.\n   - \"Listing what she is not lets her step out of the expert argument the council expects\" - Row B: Explains how the antithesis in sentences 3-4 works on this particular audience instead of just labeling it as ethos.\n   - \"The brochure's silence becomes her strongest exhibit, because the audience can see for themselves what the plan leaves out.\" - Row C: Points out that Osgood turns the brochure's own omission into evidence and never attacks the developers. This is one of the insights the rubric names as a sign of sophistication.\n   - \"That concession is what makes the kitchen metaphor work\" - Row B: Shows how two choices work together. The concession in paragraph 3 makes the metaphor believable, so the essay goes beyond treating devices one by one like a checklist.\n   - \"The sentences get shorter as the town loses more, so the first losses fall like dominoes\" - Row B: Describes how sentence length and repetition create an effect in sentences 19-21. This is full-credit commentary even without technical terms.\n   - \"because she already admitted the bad numbers, the good ones, \"four million dollars\" and \"one hundred and forty jobs,\" sound like honest counts and not a sales pitch\" - Row B: Connects the order of the statistics (unflattering first, favorable later) to credibility. This is exactly the kind of mechanism the rubric rewards.\n   - \"Osgood saves the Anna Belle story for after the economics, and the placement is deliberate.\" - Row B: Explains why the story is placed where it is, which the rubric singles out for Row B commentary.\n   - \"quietly turns the debate from profit to safety, and from what the waterfront looks like to who is physically present on it\" - Row C: Recognizes the reframing from money to safety and to who is actually present on the water, which adds complexity to the reading.\n   - \"keeps the concession alive, so nobody in the room has to choose between the wharf and the culvert\" - Row C: Sustains the tension the essay has been tracing, which is how Osgood heads off the charge of nostalgia by taking the town's money problems seriously right through her closing offer.\n   - \"The piloting metaphor ties the ethos of her opening to her request\" - Row B: Links the closing metaphor back to the ethos of the opening, completing a sustained line of reasoning with evidence from across the passage.\n\nSample response, middle score (4/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 3, Sophistication 0):\nIn her speech to the town meeting, Marguerite Osgood tries to convince the council to vote down the rezoning by presenting herself as a practical person who knows the harbor, and then by redefining the north wharf as something the whole town needs instead of just a business that is losing money. She does this through a metaphor, a concession, and a story about a rescue, which together move the audience from thinking about money to thinking about what kind of town they want.\n\nOsgood begins by building her credibility. She says \"I am not a planner, a developer, or an economist. I am a woman who knows where the rocks are.\" By listing what she is not, she sounds humble and not like someone with an agenda, and the line about the rocks makes her sound like she has real experience. This matters because the audience is about to vote on a developer's plan, and she needs them to trust her over a glossy brochure. She also describes the brochure's pictures as having \"not a single forklift, fuel barge, or crab pot,\" which points out what the developer left out and makes the audience notice what would be lost.\n\nNext, Osgood makes a concession. She admits \"The fishing fleet is smaller than it was\" and that the ice house closed, and she says condominium taxes are \"real money.\" This helps her because the people who want the tax money will not feel attacked, and it makes her seem fair and honest. Then she uses her metaphor: \"It is not a gold mine. It is a kitchen.\" The kitchen idea works because everyone understands that a restaurant needs a kitchen even if the kitchen does not make money by itself. She then lists the mechanic, the welder, and the net patcher, and says \"half of you\" buy boots at the supply store, which speaks directly to the people in the room. The statistics about four million dollars and one hundred and forty jobs back this up and show the wharf is important to the economy.\n\nThe most powerful part is probably the Anna Belle story. Osgood changes her tone to say \"something now that is harder to put in a brochure,\" and tells how Dale Cuttery got to the trawler in eleven minutes. Her short sentences, \"It cannot. Nobody is aboard,\" make the point very clearly. This shows the wharf is also about safety and not only about money, which is something the condominiums can't replace.\n\nFinally, she asks \"what are we really deciding?\" and asks whether the waterfront \"belongs to the town or merely faces it.\" Her ending goes back to her piloting job by saying she wants to \"bring one town in too,\" and she offers to help find another way to pay for the culvert. This shows she is not just saying no but is willing to help. Overall, Osgood persuades by being trustworthy, fair, and by making the audience see the wharf differently.\n   - \"tries to convince the council to vote down the rezoning by presenting herself as a practical person who knows the harbor\" - Defensible claim: it names the strategies (practical credibility, then redefining the wharf as something the whole town needs) and ties them to defeating the rezoning, so the thesis point is earned.\n   - \"By listing what she is not, she sounds humble and not like someone with an agenda\" - Accurately names the move in sentences 3-4 and gives some effect, tying it to the vote on a developer's plan. The explanation stays fairly general and does not address the risk of seeming self-interested.\n   - \"which points out what the developer left out and makes the audience notice what would be lost\" - Notes the brochure's absence but stops short of explaining how Osgood turns the developer's own silence into her exhibit; this is a missed chance for deeper analysis.\n   - \"this helps her because the people who want the tax money will not feel attacked\" - Good link between the concession and the audience's situation. The commentary does not go on to explain how the concession makes the later kitchen metaphor and statistics more credible.\n   - \"The statistics about four million dollars and one hundred and forty jobs back this up and show the wharf is important to the economy.\" - Evidence is cited but commentary is thin; it says the numbers 'back this up' without noting that she gave the unflattering numbers first or scaled the jobs to a town of six thousand.\n   - \"This shows the wharf is also about safety and not only about money, which is something the condominiums can't replace.\" - Correctly identifies the shift from money to safety in the anecdote, but does not develop why the story comes after the economic argument or who is physically present on the water.\n   - \"Overall, Osgood persuades by being trustworthy, fair, and by making the audience see the wharf differently.\" - The conclusion repeats the thesis in general terms. The essay moves through devices in order and mostly treats them separately, so it does not show a sustained structural analysis that would earn sophistication.\n\nSample response, low score (2/6; Thesis 0, Evidence and commentary 2, Sophistication 0):\nMarguerite Osgood is a retired harbor pilot and she gives a speech to the town about the north wharf. She uses many rhetorical choices to persuade the town to not rezone the wharf and keep it a working waterfront.\n\nIn the first paragraph she tells about her job climbing a rope ladder for thirty-one years. This shows she has experience and the audience will trust her. She says \"I am a woman who knows where the rocks are.\" This makes her sound like she knows what she is talking about so people will listen to her.\n\nNext she talks about the brochure from the developer. She says it is handsome and the pictures show cafes and sailboats but no forklifts. This is to show that the developer is leaving out the real working part of the town. Then she says the working wharf \"is not a gold mine. It is a kitchen.\" This is a metaphor that compares the wharf to a kitchen in a restaurant. A kitchen doesn't make money by itself but the restaurant needs it. This helps her argument because it makes the audience see the wharf is important.\n\nShe also uses facts and numbers like the co-op landing almost four million dollars of catch and one hundred and forty jobs. This is logos and it makes her argument stronger because it proves the wharf matters to the town's money.\n\nThen she tells a story about the Anna Belle that was in a gale. Dale Cuttery and the deckhands saved them in eleven minutes because they were already there. This is pathos and makes the audience feel emotional about the wharf. She says \"Nobody is aboard\" about the marina boats.\n\nIn the end she says to vote no on the rezoning. She also says they can find another way to pay for the culvert. Overall Osgood uses ethos, logos, and pathos to persuade the town and it works because it makes the audience agree with her.\n   - \"She uses many rhetorical choices to persuade the town to not rezone the wharf and keep it a working waterfront.\" - Only restates the prompt. It names no specific strategy and makes no claim about how the choices work, so it earns no thesis point.\n   - \"Overall Osgood uses ethos, logos, and pathos to persuade the town and it works because it makes the audience agree with her.\" - The conclusion falls back on a generic list of appeals. It still offers no defensible claim about this speech in particular.\n   - \"This shows she has experience and the audience will trust her.\" - Names ethos accurately but gives only a general effect. It never explains why a pilot's credibility matters to a town weighing tax revenue, or how the line about 'not a planner' works.\n   - \"This is to show that the developer is leaving out the real working part of the town.\" - Notices the brochure's absence of forklifts, which is a good choice of evidence. But the commentary is thin and misses that Osgood turns the brochure's own silence into her exhibit.\n   - \"A kitchen doesn't make money by itself but the restaurant needs it. This helps her argument because it makes the audience see the wharf is important.\" - Explains the metaphor partly. The commentary stops at 'helps her argument' and ignores that her concession in paragraph 3 makes the metaphor believable.\n   - \"This is logos and it makes her argument stronger because it proves the wharf matters to the town's money.\" - Treats the statistics as a label. It does not notice that she gives the unflattering numbers first or scales the figures to a town of six thousand.\n   - \"This is pathos and makes the audience feel emotional about the wharf.\" - Asserts that the audience feels something without explaining the mechanism. It misses that the story shifts the question from money to safety, and that the short sentences carry the force.\n   - \"In the end she says to vote no on the rezoning. She also says they can find another way to pay for the culvert.\" - Summarizes the ending without analysis. It does not discuss how the offer on the culvert eases the pressure from council members who want the tax money.\n   - \"Osgood is a retired harbor pilot and she gives a speech to the town about the north wharf.\" - The essay is a device-by-device checklist. It never looks at how the speech is structured or at the rhetorical situation, so there is no sophistication."},"set":{"explanation":"Scoring guide (ALTON practice rubric, not official College Board scoring). Each essay is worth 6 points.\nThesis (0-1): Acceptable theses identify at least one specific choice or pattern (the kitchen metaphor, the concession in paragraph 3, the Anna Belle anecdote, the shift from economic to moral framing, the piloting metaphor that frames the whole speech) and link it to the goal of defeating the rezoning. A thesis that lists devices with no claim about effect ('Osgood uses metaphor, statistics, and a story') is on the line; award the point only if the essay's thesis, wherever it appears, attaches those devices to a specific purpose; a generic purpose ('to persuade the town') does not count.\n   - 1: A defensible claim about how Osgood's choices work toward her purpose. The claim may be modest (for example, that she establishes herself as a practical insider and then redefines the wharf as infrastructure rather than a business) but it must name a rhetorical move or strategy and connect it to persuading the town to keep the wharf working. The thesis may appear anywhere in the essay and may be more than one sentence.\n   - 0: No thesis, or a thesis that only restates the prompt (for example, 'Osgood uses many rhetorical choices to persuade the town'), or a statement that merely summarizes what Osgood says about the wharf without making a claim about how she persuades. A thesis that takes a position on whether the wharf should be rezoned, rather than on Osgood's rhetoric, also earns 0.\nEvidence and Commentary (0-4): Choices that count as valid evidence include: the opening ethos and antithesis (sentences 1-4); the description of the brochure and the pointed absence (8-9); the concession and rebuttal (10-15); the kitchen metaphor and restaurant analogy (14-16); the enumeration of trades and the direct address 'half of you' (17); the cause-and-effect chain with 'Then' repetition and the two clipped sentences (19-20) before the longer sentence about the kids (21); the statistics and their scaling to a town of six thousand (22-23); the tone shift and anecdote (24-30), including the short sentences 'It cannot. Nobody is aboard.'; the rhetorical question and redefinition of the decision (31-33); the personification 'it answers back' and the parallel verbs (35-36); the closing metaphor of bringing a town in (38-39) and the conciliatory offer about the culvert (41). Essays need not use these labels; what matters is accurate description of the move and explanation of its effect. Merely noting that Osgood 'uses numbers' is weak; noting that she volunteers the unflattering numbers first (12) so the favorable ones (22) land as honest counts for more.\n   - 4: Specific, well-chosen evidence from across the passage supports a sustained line of reasoning. Commentary consistently explains how choices function for this audience: for example, why the concession about the ice house and fuel dock makes the later kitchen metaphor credible, why the Anna Belle story is placed after the numbers rather than before, or how the closing piloting metaphor returns to the ethos of the opening. The essay shows how choices work together rather than as a checklist.\n   - 3: Specific evidence from several parts of the passage, with commentary that explains how at least two or three choices serve the purpose in ways tied to this audience and occasion. The essay has a line of reasoning, but one or more body paragraphs may slip into summary, or the analysis may be uneven (strong on the anecdote, generic on the opening ethos).\n   - 2: Quotes or closely references specific moments and names the choices accurately, but commentary is thin or repetitive: it asserts that a choice 'makes the audience feel' or 'helps her argument' without explaining the mechanism. Typical 2 essays treat each device in isolation and do not connect them to the exigence (an imminent council vote on a developer's proposal).\n   - 1: Mostly summary or paraphrase of Osgood's points about the wharf with little attention to how she says them. Evidence, if quoted, is general and commentary consists of labels ('this is pathos') with no explanation of effect on the town-meeting audience.\n   - 0: Restates the thesis, summarizes the speech, or offers evidence that is not from the passage.\nSophistication (0-1): A strong candidate for this point notices that Osgood never attacks the developers, that she borrows the brochure's own silence (no forklifts) as her strongest exhibit, or that the Anna Belle story quietly reframes the question from money to safety and who is physically present on the water. Do not award the point for simply using the word 'juxtaposition' or for an eloquent conclusion attached to an otherwise formulaic essay.\n   - 1: The essay does one or more of the following convincingly and throughout rather than in a single sentence: explains how the structure of the speech works as a whole (the movement from credibility, to concession, to economics, to the rescue story, to redefinition, to the closing ask); examines tensions in Osgood's rhetoric, such as the risk that a retired pilot's nostalgia could read as self-interest and how she heads that off by conceding the fleet's decline and ending with an offer to solve the culvert problem; situates the speech in its rhetorical situation by considering what council members and residents who favor the tax revenue would need to hear; or writes with a consistently precise and controlled prose style.\n   - 0: Competent analysis that stays at the level of device plus effect, or an essay whose attempts at complexity are decorative (ornate vocabulary, sweeping claims about 'society') rather than analytical.\nNote: Score holistically within each row; an essay that earns 4 in Row B will almost always also earn the thesis point, but check that the claim is about rhetoric, not about the merits of rezoning.\nNote: Students may describe the choices without technical names. 'She stacks three short sentences so the loss sounds like dominoes falling' is full-credit commentary on sentences 19-21 even without the word 'repetition'.\nNote: Essays that argue Osgood is wrong or right about the wharf are off task; score them only on whatever rhetorical analysis they contain.\nNote: An essay built on one well-analyzed section (for example, only paragraph 5) can reach a 3 in Row B if the commentary is rich, but reaching 4 requires evidence from more than one part of the speech.\nNote: Misreadings that distort the passage (for example, claiming Osgood opposes any change or that she dismisses the town's money problems) cap Row B at 2 because the commentary rests on a false account of the text.\n\nSample response, high score (6/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 4, Sophistication 1):\nWhen Marguerite Osgood stood up at the Tillet's Reach town meeting, she was speaking to an audience that already knew the wharf was under economic pressure and its facilities were in decline. The developer's proposal had a simple pitch: handsome condos and cafes, and the tax money that comes with them. Osgood could not win by pretending those numbers away. Instead she builds her speech like one of her channel crossings, carefully and in order. First she earns trust as a practical insider who admits the wharf's weaknesses. Then she redefines the wharf from a business under economic pressure into infrastructure the town cannot function without. Finally she shifts the question from money to who is actually on the water when something goes wrong. By the end, a vote that looked like a budget decision feels like a decision about what kind of town Tillet's Reach wants to be.\n\nOsgood opens by shrinking her own authority in a way that actually increases it. \"I am not a planner, a developer, or an economist. I am a woman who knows where the rocks are.\" Listing what she is not lets her step out of the expert argument the council expects, and the plain image of the rocks suggests she knows dangers the planners don't. For an audience of neighbors, someone who climbed \"a rope ladder\" for thirty-one years is probably more trustworthy than a developer. Then, instead of mocking the brochure, she compliments it: \"It is handsome.\" She never attacks the developers at all. She uses their own drawing as evidence, pointing out that it shows \"not a single forklift, fuel barge, or crab pot.\" The brochure's silence becomes her strongest exhibit, because the audience can see for themselves what the plan leaves out.\n\nThe most important move comes in paragraph 3, where Osgood concedes the facts the other side would use against her. The ice house closed, the fuel dock \"loses money three months a year,\" and the town \"cannot fix the culvert on Pell Street.\" By saying these things first, she takes them away from her opponents and shows the council members who want the tax money that she is not just being nostalgic. That concession is what makes the kitchen metaphor work: \"It is not a gold mine. It is a kitchen.\" Nobody judges a kitchen by whether it turns a profit by itself; they judge it by whether the restaurant can run without it. The metaphor changes the standard the wharf is measured by. She then shows the consequences as a chain: \"Then the mechanic goes. Then the welder.\" The sentences get shorter as the town loses more, so the first losses fall like dominoes, and the longer final sentence about the kids who leave carries the damage past the shop floor. And because she already admitted the bad numbers, the good ones, \"four million dollars\" and \"one hundred and forty jobs,\" sound like honest counts and not a sales pitch.\n\nOsgood saves the Anna Belle story for after the economics, and the placement is deliberate. She even flags the shift, calling it \"something now that is harder to put in a brochure.\" Once the council has heard that the wharf is worth money, she shows that its real value can't be counted. Dale Cuttery reached the trawler in eleven minutes \"because they were already there.\" Then come two blunt sentences: \"It cannot. Nobody is aboard.\" The image of empty shrink-wrapped sailboats quietly turns the debate from profit to safety, and from what the waterfront looks like to who is physically present on it. It is hard to argue for a boardwalk after that without sounding like you'd trade a rescue for a view.\n\nFinally, Osgood returns to where she started. \"I have brought a great many ships safely in\" sets up her wish \"to help bring one town in too.\" The piloting metaphor ties the ethos of her opening to her request, so she sounds less like an opponent of progress and more like a guide through a dangerous channel. Even her last line, \"let us sit down and find another way to pay for the culvert,\" keeps the concession alive, so nobody in the room has to choose between the wharf and the culvert. Osgood persuades because she never asks the town to ignore its problems. She only asks it to see what the brochure left out.\n   - \"First she earns trust as a practical insider who admits the wharf's weaknesses. Then she redefines the wharf from a business under economic pressure into infrastructure the town cannot function without.\" - Row A: Defensible, specific thesis that names actual strategies (insider ethos, concession, redefinition as infrastructure, shift to safety) and links them to defeating the rezoning. The First/Then/Finally order also sets up the essay's line of reasoning.\n   - \"she builds her speech like one of her channel crossings, carefully and in order\" - Row C: Sets up the essay's lasting focus on how the whole speech is structured. The body paragraphs follow that sequence, from credibility to concession to economics to the rescue story to the closing ask, so the sophistication point rests on more than one line.\n   - \"Listing what she is not lets her step out of the expert argument the council expects\" - Row B: Explains how the antithesis in sentences 3-4 works on this particular audience instead of just labeling it as ethos.\n   - \"The brochure's silence becomes her strongest exhibit, because the audience can see for themselves what the plan leaves out.\" - Row C: Points out that Osgood turns the brochure's own omission into evidence and never attacks the developers. This is one of the insights the rubric names as a sign of sophistication.\n   - \"That concession is what makes the kitchen metaphor work\" - Row B: Shows how two choices work together. The concession in paragraph 3 makes the metaphor believable, so the essay goes beyond treating devices one by one like a checklist.\n   - \"The sentences get shorter as the town loses more, so the first losses fall like dominoes\" - Row B: Describes how sentence length and repetition create an effect in sentences 19-21. This is full-credit commentary even without technical terms.\n   - \"because she already admitted the bad numbers, the good ones, \"four million dollars\" and \"one hundred and forty jobs,\" sound like honest counts and not a sales pitch\" - Row B: Connects the order of the statistics (unflattering first, favorable later) to credibility. This is exactly the kind of mechanism the rubric rewards.\n   - \"Osgood saves the Anna Belle story for after the economics, and the placement is deliberate.\" - Row B: Explains why the story is placed where it is, which the rubric singles out for Row B commentary.\n   - \"quietly turns the debate from profit to safety, and from what the waterfront looks like to who is physically present on it\" - Row C: Recognizes the reframing from money to safety and to who is actually present on the water, which adds complexity to the reading.\n   - \"keeps the concession alive, so nobody in the room has to choose between the wharf and the culvert\" - Row C: Sustains the tension the essay has been tracing, which is how Osgood heads off the charge of nostalgia by taking the town's money problems seriously right through her closing offer.\n   - \"The piloting metaphor ties the ethos of her opening to her request\" - Row B: Links the closing metaphor back to the ethos of the opening, completing a sustained line of reasoning with evidence from across the passage.\n\nSample response, middle score (4/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 3, Sophistication 0):\nIn her speech to the town meeting, Marguerite Osgood tries to convince the council to vote down the rezoning by presenting herself as a practical person who knows the harbor, and then by redefining the north wharf as something the whole town needs instead of just a business under economic pressure. She does this through a metaphor, a concession, and a story about a rescue, which together move the audience from thinking about money to thinking about what kind of town they want.\n\nOsgood begins by building her credibility. She says \"I am not a planner, a developer, or an economist. I am a woman who knows where the rocks are.\" By listing what she is not, she sounds humble and not like someone with an agenda, and the line about the rocks makes her sound like she has real experience. This matters because the audience is about to vote on a developer's plan, and she needs them to trust her over a glossy brochure. She also describes the brochure's pictures as having \"not a single forklift, fuel barge, or crab pot,\" which points out what the developer left out and makes the audience notice what would be lost.\n\nNext, Osgood makes a concession. She admits \"The fishing fleet is smaller than it was\" and that the ice house closed, and she says condominium taxes are \"real money.\" This helps her because the people who want the tax money will not feel attacked, and it makes her seem fair and honest. Then she uses her metaphor: \"It is not a gold mine. It is a kitchen.\" The kitchen idea works because everyone understands that a restaurant needs a kitchen even if the kitchen does not make money by itself. She then lists the mechanic, the welder, and the net patcher, and says \"half of you\" buy boots at the supply store, which speaks directly to the people in the room. The statistics about four million dollars and one hundred and forty jobs back this up and show the wharf is important to the economy.\n\nThe most powerful part is probably the Anna Belle story. Osgood changes her tone to say \"something now that is harder to put in a brochure,\" and tells how Dale Cuttery got to the trawler in eleven minutes. Her short sentences, \"It cannot. Nobody is aboard,\" make the point very clearly. This shows the wharf is also about safety and not only about money, which is something the condominiums can't replace.\n\nFinally, she asks \"what are we really deciding?\" and asks whether the waterfront \"belongs to the town or merely faces it.\" Her ending goes back to her piloting job by saying she wants to \"bring one town in too,\" and she offers to help find another way to pay for the culvert. This shows she is not just saying no but is willing to help. Overall, Osgood persuades by being trustworthy, fair, and by making the audience see the wharf differently.\n   - \"tries to convince the council to vote down the rezoning by presenting herself as a practical person who knows the harbor\" - Defensible claim: it names the strategies (practical credibility, then redefining the wharf as something the whole town needs) and ties them to defeating the rezoning, so the thesis point is earned.\n   - \"By listing what she is not, she sounds humble and not like someone with an agenda\" - Accurately names the move in sentences 3-4 and gives some effect, tying it to the vote on a developer's plan. The explanation stays fairly general and does not address the risk of seeming self-interested.\n   - \"which points out what the developer left out and makes the audience notice what would be lost\" - Notes the brochure's absence but stops short of explaining how Osgood turns the developer's own silence into her exhibit; this is a missed chance for deeper analysis.\n   - \"this helps her because the people who want the tax money will not feel attacked\" - Good link between the concession and the audience's situation. The commentary does not go on to explain how the concession makes the later kitchen metaphor and statistics more credible.\n   - \"The statistics about four million dollars and one hundred and forty jobs back this up and show the wharf is important to the economy.\" - Evidence is cited but commentary is thin; it says the numbers 'back this up' without noting that she gave the unflattering numbers first or scaled the jobs to a town of six thousand.\n   - \"This shows the wharf is also about safety and not only about money, which is something the condominiums can't replace.\" - Correctly identifies the shift from money to safety in the anecdote, but does not develop why the story comes after the economic argument or who is physically present on the water.\n   - \"Overall, Osgood persuades by being trustworthy, fair, and by making the audience see the wharf differently.\" - The conclusion repeats the thesis in general terms. The essay moves through devices in order and mostly treats them separately, so it does not show a sustained structural analysis that would earn sophistication.\n\nSample response, low score (2/6; Thesis 0, Evidence and commentary 2, Sophistication 0):\nMarguerite Osgood is a retired harbor pilot and she gives a speech to the town about the north wharf. She uses many rhetorical choices to persuade the town to not rezone the wharf and keep it a working waterfront.\n\nIn the first paragraph she tells about her job climbing a rope ladder for thirty-one years. This shows she has experience and the audience will trust her. She says \"I am a woman who knows where the rocks are.\" This makes her sound like she knows what she is talking about so people will listen to her.\n\nNext she talks about the brochure from the developer. She says it is handsome and the pictures show cafes and sailboats but no forklifts. This is to show that the developer is leaving out the real working part of the town. Then she says the working wharf \"is not a gold mine. It is a kitchen.\" This is a metaphor that compares the wharf to a kitchen in a restaurant. A kitchen doesn't make money by itself but the restaurant needs it. This helps her argument because it makes the audience see the wharf is important.\n\nShe also uses facts and numbers like the co-op landing almost four million dollars of catch and one hundred and forty jobs. This is logos and it makes her argument stronger because it proves the wharf matters to the town's money.\n\nThen she tells a story about the Anna Belle that was in a gale. Dale Cuttery and the deckhands saved them in eleven minutes because they were already there. This is pathos and makes the audience feel emotional about the wharf. She says \"Nobody is aboard\" about the marina boats.\n\nIn the end she says to vote no on the rezoning. She also says they can find another way to pay for the culvert. Overall Osgood uses ethos, logos, and pathos to persuade the town and it works because it makes the audience agree with her.\n   - \"She uses many rhetorical choices to persuade the town to not rezone the wharf and keep it a working waterfront.\" - Only restates the prompt. It names no specific strategy and makes no claim about how the choices work, so it earns no thesis point.\n   - \"Overall Osgood uses ethos, logos, and pathos to persuade the town and it works because it makes the audience agree with her.\" - The conclusion falls back on a generic list of appeals. It still offers no defensible claim about this speech in particular.\n   - \"This shows she has experience and the audience will trust her.\" - Names ethos accurately but gives only a general effect. It never explains why a pilot's credibility matters to a town weighing tax revenue, or how the line about 'not a planner' works.\n   - \"This is to show that the developer is leaving out the real working part of the town.\" - Notices the brochure's absence of forklifts, which is a good choice of evidence. But the commentary is thin and misses that Osgood turns the brochure's own silence into her exhibit.\n   - \"A kitchen doesn't make money by itself but the restaurant needs it. This helps her argument because it makes the audience see the wharf is important.\" - Explains the metaphor partly. The commentary stops at 'helps her argument' and ignores that her concession in paragraph 3 makes the metaphor believable.\n   - \"This is logos and it makes her argument stronger because it proves the wharf matters to the town's money.\" - Treats the statistics as a label. It does not notice that she gives the unflattering numbers first or scales the figures to a town of six thousand.\n   - \"This is pathos and makes the audience feel emotional about the wharf.\" - Asserts that the audience feels something without explaining the mechanism. It misses that the story shifts the question from money to safety, and that the short sentences carry the force.\n   - \"In the end she says to vote no on the rezoning. She also says they can find another way to pay for the culvert.\" - Summarizes the ending without analysis. It does not discuss how the offer on the culvert eases the pressure from council members who want the tax money.\n   - \"Osgood is a retired harbor pilot and she gives a speech to the town about the north wharf.\" - The essay is a device-by-device checklist. It never looks at how the speech is structured or at the rhetorical situation, so there is no sophistication.","explanation_en":"Scoring guide (ALTON practice rubric, not official College Board scoring). Each essay is worth 6 points.\nThesis (0-1): Acceptable theses identify at least one specific choice or pattern (the kitchen metaphor, the concession in paragraph 3, the Anna Belle anecdote, the shift from economic to moral framing, the piloting metaphor that frames the whole speech) and link it to the goal of defeating the rezoning. A thesis that lists devices with no claim about effect ('Osgood uses metaphor, statistics, and a story') is on the line; award the point only if the essay's thesis, wherever it appears, attaches those devices to a specific purpose; a generic purpose ('to persuade the town') does not count.\n   - 1: A defensible claim about how Osgood's choices work toward her purpose. The claim may be modest (for example, that she establishes herself as a practical insider and then redefines the wharf as infrastructure rather than a business) but it must name a rhetorical move or strategy and connect it to persuading the town to keep the wharf working. The thesis may appear anywhere in the essay and may be more than one sentence.\n   - 0: No thesis, or a thesis that only restates the prompt (for example, 'Osgood uses many rhetorical choices to persuade the town'), or a statement that merely summarizes what Osgood says about the wharf without making a claim about how she persuades. A thesis that takes a position on whether the wharf should be rezoned, rather than on Osgood's rhetoric, also earns 0.\nEvidence and Commentary (0-4): Choices that count as valid evidence include: the opening ethos and antithesis (sentences 1-4); the description of the brochure and the pointed absence (8-9); the concession and rebuttal (10-15); the kitchen metaphor and restaurant analogy (14-16); the enumeration of trades and the direct address 'half of you' (17); the cause-and-effect chain with 'Then' repetition and the two clipped sentences (19-20) before the longer sentence about the kids (21); the statistics and their scaling to a town of six thousand (22-23); the tone shift and anecdote (24-30), including the short sentences 'It cannot. Nobody is aboard.'; the rhetorical question and redefinition of the decision (31-33); the personification 'it answers back' and the parallel verbs (35-36); the closing metaphor of bringing a town in (38-39) and the conciliatory offer about the culvert (41). Essays need not use these labels; what matters is accurate description of the move and explanation of its effect. Merely noting that Osgood 'uses numbers' is weak; noting that she volunteers the unflattering numbers first (12) so the favorable ones (22) land as honest counts for more.\n   - 4: Specific, well-chosen evidence from across the passage supports a sustained line of reasoning. Commentary consistently explains how choices function for this audience: for example, why the concession about the ice house and fuel dock makes the later kitchen metaphor credible, why the Anna Belle story is placed after the numbers rather than before, or how the closing piloting metaphor returns to the ethos of the opening. The essay shows how choices work together rather than as a checklist.\n   - 3: Specific evidence from several parts of the passage, with commentary that explains how at least two or three choices serve the purpose in ways tied to this audience and occasion. The essay has a line of reasoning, but one or more body paragraphs may slip into summary, or the analysis may be uneven (strong on the anecdote, generic on the opening ethos).\n   - 2: Quotes or closely references specific moments and names the choices accurately, but commentary is thin or repetitive: it asserts that a choice 'makes the audience feel' or 'helps her argument' without explaining the mechanism. Typical 2 essays treat each device in isolation and do not connect them to the exigence (an imminent council vote on a developer's proposal).\n   - 1: Mostly summary or paraphrase of Osgood's points about the wharf with little attention to how she says them. Evidence, if quoted, is general and commentary consists of labels ('this is pathos') with no explanation of effect on the town-meeting audience.\n   - 0: Restates the thesis, summarizes the speech, or offers evidence that is not from the passage.\nSophistication (0-1): A strong candidate for this point notices that Osgood never attacks the developers, that she borrows the brochure's own silence (no forklifts) as her strongest exhibit, or that the Anna Belle story quietly reframes the question from money to safety and who is physically present on the water. Do not award the point for simply using the word 'juxtaposition' or for an eloquent conclusion attached to an otherwise formulaic essay.\n   - 1: The essay does one or more of the following convincingly and throughout rather than in a single sentence: explains how the structure of the speech works as a whole (the movement from credibility, to concession, to economics, to the rescue story, to redefinition, to the closing ask); examines tensions in Osgood's rhetoric, such as the risk that a retired pilot's nostalgia could read as self-interest and how she heads that off by conceding the fleet's decline and ending with an offer to solve the culvert problem; situates the speech in its rhetorical situation by considering what council members and residents who favor the tax revenue would need to hear; or writes with a consistently precise and controlled prose style.\n   - 0: Competent analysis that stays at the level of device plus effect, or an essay whose attempts at complexity are decorative (ornate vocabulary, sweeping claims about 'society') rather than analytical.\nNote: Score holistically within each row; an essay that earns 4 in Row B will almost always also earn the thesis point, but check that the claim is about rhetoric, not about the merits of rezoning.\nNote: Students may describe the choices without technical names. 'She stacks three short sentences so the loss sounds like dominoes falling' is full-credit commentary on sentences 19-21 even without the word 'repetition'.\nNote: Essays that argue Osgood is wrong or right about the wharf are off task; score them only on whatever rhetorical analysis they contain.\nNote: An essay built on one well-analyzed section (for example, only paragraph 5) can reach a 3 in Row B if the commentary is rich, but reaching 4 requires evidence from more than one part of the speech.\nNote: Misreadings that distort the passage (for example, claiming Osgood opposes any change or that she dismisses the town's money problems) cap Row B at 2 because the commentary rests on a false account of the text.\n\nSample response, high score (6/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 4, Sophistication 1):\nWhen Marguerite Osgood stood up at the Tillet's Reach town meeting, she was speaking to an audience that already knew the wharf was under economic pressure and its facilities were in decline. The developer's proposal had a simple pitch: handsome condos and cafes, and the tax money that comes with them. Osgood could not win by pretending those numbers away. Instead she builds her speech like one of her channel crossings, carefully and in order. First she earns trust as a practical insider who admits the wharf's weaknesses. Then she redefines the wharf from a business under economic pressure into infrastructure the town cannot function without. Finally she shifts the question from money to who is actually on the water when something goes wrong. By the end, a vote that looked like a budget decision feels like a decision about what kind of town Tillet's Reach wants to be.\n\nOsgood opens by shrinking her own authority in a way that actually increases it. \"I am not a planner, a developer, or an economist. I am a woman who knows where the rocks are.\" Listing what she is not lets her step out of the expert argument the council expects, and the plain image of the rocks suggests she knows dangers the planners don't. For an audience of neighbors, someone who climbed \"a rope ladder\" for thirty-one years is probably more trustworthy than a developer. Then, instead of mocking the brochure, she compliments it: \"It is handsome.\" She never attacks the developers at all. She uses their own drawing as evidence, pointing out that it shows \"not a single forklift, fuel barge, or crab pot.\" The brochure's silence becomes her strongest exhibit, because the audience can see for themselves what the plan leaves out.\n\nThe most important move comes in paragraph 3, where Osgood concedes the facts the other side would use against her. The ice house closed, the fuel dock \"loses money three months a year,\" and the town \"cannot fix the culvert on Pell Street.\" By saying these things first, she takes them away from her opponents and shows the council members who want the tax money that she is not just being nostalgic. That concession is what makes the kitchen metaphor work: \"It is not a gold mine. It is a kitchen.\" Nobody judges a kitchen by whether it turns a profit by itself; they judge it by whether the restaurant can run without it. The metaphor changes the standard the wharf is measured by. She then shows the consequences as a chain: \"Then the mechanic goes. Then the welder.\" The sentences get shorter as the town loses more, so the first losses fall like dominoes, and the longer final sentence about the kids who leave carries the damage past the shop floor. And because she already admitted the bad numbers, the good ones, \"four million dollars\" and \"one hundred and forty jobs,\" sound like honest counts and not a sales pitch.\n\nOsgood saves the Anna Belle story for after the economics, and the placement is deliberate. She even flags the shift, calling it \"something now that is harder to put in a brochure.\" Once the council has heard that the wharf is worth money, she shows that its real value can't be counted. Dale Cuttery reached the trawler in eleven minutes \"because they were already there.\" Then come two blunt sentences: \"It cannot. Nobody is aboard.\" The image of empty shrink-wrapped sailboats quietly turns the debate from profit to safety, and from what the waterfront looks like to who is physically present on it. It is hard to argue for a boardwalk after that without sounding like you'd trade a rescue for a view.\n\nFinally, Osgood returns to where she started. \"I have brought a great many ships safely in\" sets up her wish \"to help bring one town in too.\" The piloting metaphor ties the ethos of her opening to her request, so she sounds less like an opponent of progress and more like a guide through a dangerous channel. Even her last line, \"let us sit down and find another way to pay for the culvert,\" keeps the concession alive, so nobody in the room has to choose between the wharf and the culvert. Osgood persuades because she never asks the town to ignore its problems. She only asks it to see what the brochure left out.\n   - \"First she earns trust as a practical insider who admits the wharf's weaknesses. Then she redefines the wharf from a business under economic pressure into infrastructure the town cannot function without.\" - Row A: Defensible, specific thesis that names actual strategies (insider ethos, concession, redefinition as infrastructure, shift to safety) and links them to defeating the rezoning. The First/Then/Finally order also sets up the essay's line of reasoning.\n   - \"she builds her speech like one of her channel crossings, carefully and in order\" - Row C: Sets up the essay's lasting focus on how the whole speech is structured. The body paragraphs follow that sequence, from credibility to concession to economics to the rescue story to the closing ask, so the sophistication point rests on more than one line.\n   - \"Listing what she is not lets her step out of the expert argument the council expects\" - Row B: Explains how the antithesis in sentences 3-4 works on this particular audience instead of just labeling it as ethos.\n   - \"The brochure's silence becomes her strongest exhibit, because the audience can see for themselves what the plan leaves out.\" - Row C: Points out that Osgood turns the brochure's own omission into evidence and never attacks the developers. This is one of the insights the rubric names as a sign of sophistication.\n   - \"That concession is what makes the kitchen metaphor work\" - Row B: Shows how two choices work together. The concession in paragraph 3 makes the metaphor believable, so the essay goes beyond treating devices one by one like a checklist.\n   - \"The sentences get shorter as the town loses more, so the first losses fall like dominoes\" - Row B: Describes how sentence length and repetition create an effect in sentences 19-21. This is full-credit commentary even without technical terms.\n   - \"because she already admitted the bad numbers, the good ones, \"four million dollars\" and \"one hundred and forty jobs,\" sound like honest counts and not a sales pitch\" - Row B: Connects the order of the statistics (unflattering first, favorable later) to credibility. This is exactly the kind of mechanism the rubric rewards.\n   - \"Osgood saves the Anna Belle story for after the economics, and the placement is deliberate.\" - Row B: Explains why the story is placed where it is, which the rubric singles out for Row B commentary.\n   - \"quietly turns the debate from profit to safety, and from what the waterfront looks like to who is physically present on it\" - Row C: Recognizes the reframing from money to safety and to who is actually present on the water, which adds complexity to the reading.\n   - \"keeps the concession alive, so nobody in the room has to choose between the wharf and the culvert\" - Row C: Sustains the tension the essay has been tracing, which is how Osgood heads off the charge of nostalgia by taking the town's money problems seriously right through her closing offer.\n   - \"The piloting metaphor ties the ethos of her opening to her request\" - Row B: Links the closing metaphor back to the ethos of the opening, completing a sustained line of reasoning with evidence from across the passage.\n\nSample response, middle score (4/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 3, Sophistication 0):\nIn her speech to the town meeting, Marguerite Osgood tries to convince the council to vote down the rezoning by presenting herself as a practical person who knows the harbor, and then by redefining the north wharf as something the whole town needs instead of just a business under economic pressure. She does this through a metaphor, a concession, and a story about a rescue, which together move the audience from thinking about money to thinking about what kind of town they want.\n\nOsgood begins by building her credibility. She says \"I am not a planner, a developer, or an economist. I am a woman who knows where the rocks are.\" By listing what she is not, she sounds humble and not like someone with an agenda, and the line about the rocks makes her sound like she has real experience. This matters because the audience is about to vote on a developer's plan, and she needs them to trust her over a glossy brochure. She also describes the brochure's pictures as having \"not a single forklift, fuel barge, or crab pot,\" which points out what the developer left out and makes the audience notice what would be lost.\n\nNext, Osgood makes a concession. She admits \"The fishing fleet is smaller than it was\" and that the ice house closed, and she says condominium taxes are \"real money.\" This helps her because the people who want the tax money will not feel attacked, and it makes her seem fair and honest. Then she uses her metaphor: \"It is not a gold mine. It is a kitchen.\" The kitchen idea works because everyone understands that a restaurant needs a kitchen even if the kitchen does not make money by itself. She then lists the mechanic, the welder, and the net patcher, and says \"half of you\" buy boots at the supply store, which speaks directly to the people in the room. The statistics about four million dollars and one hundred and forty jobs back this up and show the wharf is important to the economy.\n\nThe most powerful part is probably the Anna Belle story. Osgood changes her tone to say \"something now that is harder to put in a brochure,\" and tells how Dale Cuttery got to the trawler in eleven minutes. Her short sentences, \"It cannot. Nobody is aboard,\" make the point very clearly. This shows the wharf is also about safety and not only about money, which is something the condominiums can't replace.\n\nFinally, she asks \"what are we really deciding?\" and asks whether the waterfront \"belongs to the town or merely faces it.\" Her ending goes back to her piloting job by saying she wants to \"bring one town in too,\" and she offers to help find another way to pay for the culvert. This shows she is not just saying no but is willing to help. Overall, Osgood persuades by being trustworthy, fair, and by making the audience see the wharf differently.\n   - \"tries to convince the council to vote down the rezoning by presenting herself as a practical person who knows the harbor\" - Defensible claim: it names the strategies (practical credibility, then redefining the wharf as something the whole town needs) and ties them to defeating the rezoning, so the thesis point is earned.\n   - \"By listing what she is not, she sounds humble and not like someone with an agenda\" - Accurately names the move in sentences 3-4 and gives some effect, tying it to the vote on a developer's plan. The explanation stays fairly general and does not address the risk of seeming self-interested.\n   - \"which points out what the developer left out and makes the audience notice what would be lost\" - Notes the brochure's absence but stops short of explaining how Osgood turns the developer's own silence into her exhibit; this is a missed chance for deeper analysis.\n   - \"this helps her because the people who want the tax money will not feel attacked\" - Good link between the concession and the audience's situation. The commentary does not go on to explain how the concession makes the later kitchen metaphor and statistics more credible.\n   - \"The statistics about four million dollars and one hundred and forty jobs back this up and show the wharf is important to the economy.\" - Evidence is cited but commentary is thin; it says the numbers 'back this up' without noting that she gave the unflattering numbers first or scaled the jobs to a town of six thousand.\n   - \"This shows the wharf is also about safety and not only about money, which is something the condominiums can't replace.\" - Correctly identifies the shift from money to safety in the anecdote, but does not develop why the story comes after the economic argument or who is physically present on the water.\n   - \"Overall, Osgood persuades by being trustworthy, fair, and by making the audience see the wharf differently.\" - The conclusion repeats the thesis in general terms. The essay moves through devices in order and mostly treats them separately, so it does not show a sustained structural analysis that would earn sophistication.\n\nSample response, low score (2/6; Thesis 0, Evidence and commentary 2, Sophistication 0):\nMarguerite Osgood is a retired harbor pilot and she gives a speech to the town about the north wharf. She uses many rhetorical choices to persuade the town to not rezone the wharf and keep it a working waterfront.\n\nIn the first paragraph she tells about her job climbing a rope ladder for thirty-one years. This shows she has experience and the audience will trust her. She says \"I am a woman who knows where the rocks are.\" This makes her sound like she knows what she is talking about so people will listen to her.\n\nNext she talks about the brochure from the developer. She says it is handsome and the pictures show cafes and sailboats but no forklifts. This is to show that the developer is leaving out the real working part of the town. Then she says the working wharf \"is not a gold mine. It is a kitchen.\" This is a metaphor that compares the wharf to a kitchen in a restaurant. A kitchen doesn't make money by itself but the restaurant needs it. This helps her argument because it makes the audience see the wharf is important.\n\nShe also uses facts and numbers like the co-op landing almost four million dollars of catch and one hundred and forty jobs. This is logos and it makes her argument stronger because it proves the wharf matters to the town's money.\n\nThen she tells a story about the Anna Belle that was in a gale. Dale Cuttery and the deckhands saved them in eleven minutes because they were already there. This is pathos and makes the audience feel emotional about the wharf. She says \"Nobody is aboard\" about the marina boats.\n\nIn the end she says to vote no on the rezoning. She also says they can find another way to pay for the culvert. Overall Osgood uses ethos, logos, and pathos to persuade the town and it works because it makes the audience agree with her.\n   - \"She uses many rhetorical choices to persuade the town to not rezone the wharf and keep it a working waterfront.\" - Only restates the prompt. It names no specific strategy and makes no claim about how the choices work, so it earns no thesis point.\n   - \"Overall Osgood uses ethos, logos, and pathos to persuade the town and it works because it makes the audience agree with her.\" - The conclusion falls back on a generic list of appeals. It still offers no defensible claim about this speech in particular.\n   - \"This shows she has experience and the audience will trust her.\" - Names ethos accurately but gives only a general effect. It never explains why a pilot's credibility matters to a town weighing tax revenue, or how the line about 'not a planner' works.\n   - \"This is to show that the developer is leaving out the real working part of the town.\" - Notices the brochure's absence of forklifts, which is a good choice of evidence. But the commentary is thin and misses that Osgood turns the brochure's own silence into her exhibit.\n   - \"A kitchen doesn't make money by itself but the restaurant needs it. This helps her argument because it makes the audience see the wharf is important.\" - Explains the metaphor partly. The commentary stops at 'helps her argument' and ignores that her concession in paragraph 3 makes the metaphor believable.\n   - \"This is logos and it makes her argument stronger because it proves the wharf matters to the town's money.\" - Treats the statistics as a label. It does not notice that she gives the unflattering numbers first or scales the figures to a town of six thousand.\n   - \"This is pathos and makes the audience feel emotional about the wharf.\" - Asserts that the audience feels something without explaining the mechanism. It misses that the story shifts the question from money to safety, and that the short sentences carry the force.\n   - \"In the end she says to vote no on the rezoning. She also says they can find another way to pay for the culvert.\" - Summarizes the ending without analysis. It does not discuss how the offer on the culvert eases the pressure from council members who want the tax money.\n   - \"Osgood is a retired harbor pilot and she gives a speech to the town about the north wharf.\" - The essay is a device-by-device checklist. It never looks at how the speech is structured or at the rhetorical situation, so there is no sophistication."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-15T15:30:39.091Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R6-ELA2-FRQ2","fromVersionId":"d90bffc2-8f53-4f43-953b-33ae1613bf87","fromVersionNo":1},"ap":{"candidate_key":"el-rv:ap_english_language-fa2-k0","item_index":0}},{"patch_id":"R6-ELA1-FRQ1","problem_id":"a0d8b10e-5ed9-4740-994c-48fa1f29e1ee","from_version_id":"e9a902d1-3a57-4fa7-8128-c927bafc38a4","expect":{"explanation":"Scoring guide (ALTON practice rubric, not official College Board scoring). Each essay is worth 6 points.\nThesis (0-1): A conditional thesis earns the point only if the conditions are named, not merely gestured at ('it depends on the district'). A thesis that says the schedule 'has pros and cons' without resolving them does not earn the point.\n   - 1: States a defensible position on whether, or under what conditions, a district should move to a four-day week. The position may be unconditional (adopt / do not adopt), conditional (adopt only if instructional hours are preserved and a fifth-day option is funded), or alternative (reject the schedule and address teacher shortages another way). A thesis may appear anywhere in the essay and may be more than one sentence, but it must commit to a stance the rest of the essay can defend.\n   - 0: No thesis; a thesis that only restates the prompt (for example, 'There are many opinions about the four-day week'); a thesis that merely lists what the sources say without taking a position; or a thesis that answers a different question (such as whether teachers deserve raises) without connecting it to whether a district should adopt the schedule.\nEvidence and commentary (0-4): Count as 'using' a source only when the writer draws a specific claim, figure, or example from it and attributes it (Source A through Source F). Numbers from Source E or Source F count as evidence when the writer interprets them (for example, that employers oppose the schedule while teachers favor it, and why that gap matters), not when they are simply reproduced. Source C and Source D can be used for more than one stance; credit depends on the writer's explanation, not on which stance the writer takes.\n   - 4: Uses at least three sources with well-chosen, specific evidence throughout and consistently explains how each piece advances the argument. Sources are synthesized rather than stacked: the writer notices, for example, that Source A's reduction in vacancies, Source C's eleven applicants, and Source D's 2.4 multiplier describe the same phenomenon at three scales, or that Source E's split among elementary parents matches the grade-level findings in Source D. The essay engages with evidence that complicates its position and explains why the position still holds.\n   - 3: Uses at least three sources with specific, accurate evidence and explains how most of it supports a clear line of reasoning. The writer may put two sources in conversation (for instance, Source B's cost-transfer argument against Source F's breakdown of savings) but one or two pieces of evidence may be underexplained, or the essay may not account for the strongest counter-evidence.\n   - 2: Uses at least three sources but commentary is thin or repetitive: the writer quotes or paraphrases a figure (such as the 410,000 dollar savings or the 41 percent childcare figure) and then asserts that it proves the point without explaining why. Evidence may be loosely connected to the thesis, or the essay may treat each source in isolation without connecting them.\n   - 1: Mostly summary or paraphrase of fewer than three sources, or of three sources with no explanation of how any of them supports the writer's position. Sources may be mentioned in a list ('Sources A, C, and D all discuss savings') without being put to use.\n   - 0: Restates the thesis, offers only personal opinion with no source material, or uses sources in a way that shows misreading (for example, claiming Source D proves four-day weeks raise test scores).\nSophistication (0-1): Sophistication is not earned by taking the middle stance automatically. A conditional thesis with vague conditions is no more sophisticated than a one-sided essay; a one-sided essay that honestly confronts Source C's caveats or Source B's cost-transfer logic can earn the point.\n   - 1: Demonstrates a complex understanding of the issue through one or more of the following, carried through the essay rather than appearing in a single sentence: explaining why the same evidence (such as the modest 2 percent savings) can support opposing conclusions; identifying who gains and who pays and treating that distribution as central to the decision; recognizing that Harrow Fork and Pinemont Valley are different districts and reasoning about whether one's experience transfers to another; distinguishing between short-run effects (first-year reading dips) and long-run effects (closed gaps after repair work); or making a conditional argument whose conditions are drawn from and tested against the sources.\n   - 0: Argument stays at a general level ('the district should weigh costs and benefits'), relies on vague praise or blame, uses ornamental but empty language, or acknowledges a counterargument only to drop it.\nNote: Scores are additive: Row A (0-1) plus Row B (0-4) plus Row C (0-1), maximum 6.\nNote: An essay that earns 0 on Row A can still earn up to 2 on Row B if it uses sources, but cannot earn 3 or 4 because there is no position for the evidence to support.\nNote: Misstating a figure from a source (for example, calling the savings 5 percent) counts against accuracy on Row B; one small slip does not by itself lower the score, but a pattern does.\nNote: Writers who cite Source E or Source F must interpret the numbers; an essay that reproduces the table as a sentence earns no more credit than one that mentions the source by letter.\nNote: Essays that reject the four-day week and propose a different remedy for teacher shortages (such as Source B's levy proposal) are fully defensible, provided the writer still engages with the sources' evidence on the schedule itself.\n\nSample response, high score (6/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 4, Sophistication 1):\nWhen people argue about the four-day school week, they usually argue about Fridays. Teachers and teenagers picture a three-day weekend, and working parents picture a day-care bill. But the real question for a district isn't whether Fridays off are good or bad. It's who ends up paying for them. A district should move to a four-day week only if it will do two things before the first Thursday dismissal: lengthen the remaining days enough to keep total instructional hours the same, and pay for a Friday option for children under twelve. If it can't afford both, it should find another way to fix its teacher shortage.\n\nThe case for the schedule is not really about money, even though money is what gets announced. Harrow Fork saved about 410,000 dollars, \"close to 2 percent of its operating budget\" (Source A), and Source D found that savings across 23 districts topped out at 2.5 percent, with none reaching the 5 percent some had promised. Source F shows why. The biggest savings come from parking buses (165,000 dollars) and paying for fewer utilities, and there is no bar for teacher salaries at all, because teachers get paid the same whether school meets four days or five. A district hoping to balance its budget this way will be disappointed. What it can realistically buy is teachers. Harrow Fork went from 14 unfilled positions to 5 (Source A), Pinemont Valley's science posting went from two applications to eleven (Source C), and across the Meridian Basin applicants per vacancy rose 2.4 times (Source D). That is the same pattern at the scale of one job, one district, and a whole region, which makes it hard to dismiss as luck. For a rural district that can't find a science teacher, this is a serious argument.\n\nThe problem is that the hiring benefit goes to the district while the cost goes somewhere else. Marisol Ettinger, a store owner with two kids in elementary school, pays 76 dollars a week, over 2,700 dollars a year, for Friday care, and she argues that \"a saving that moves from the district's ledger to mine is not a saving; it is a transfer\" (Source B). It would be easy to write her off as one frustrated parent, except that Source D found 41 percent of parents of children under 12 reported new childcare costs and 22 percent cut their own work hours. Source E shows the same split: 84 percent of teachers want to keep the calendar, but elementary parents are divided 48 to 42, and local employers, probably the people covering for those parents' missing hours, oppose it 49 to 37. The groups who like the schedule most are the ones it was designed to help, and the people closest to the youngest children are the ones paying for it.\n\nThat is why the conditions matter. Source D's achievement findings suggest the four-day schedule isn't the problem; lost hours are. Districts that kept total hours saw growth \"statistically indistinguishable\" from five-day districts, while those that cut hours fell 3 percentile points behind in math, mostly in grades 2 through 5. Pinemont Valley's third and fourth graders slipped in reading the first year, and the gap only closed after the district moved reading to the morning and paid for Friday tutoring (Source C). So a first-year dip doesn't prove the schedule fails, but a closed gap doesn't prove it works by itself either. Castellane's own summary, \"We got lucky, and then we worked,\" reads less like a success story than a warning.\n\nEven Pinemont hasn't solved childcare. Its enrichment day serves about 140 children at two of five buildings, paid for mostly by a county grant, and Castellane tells critics to \"ask to see the waiting list.\" A district without that grant is not Pinemont. Harrow Fork has a library turning children away on Fridays and day-cares adding new rates, which suggests the town absorbed the cost instead of the district planning for it. Before a board copies Pinemont's results, it should ask whether it can copy Pinemont's repair work.\n\nThe four-day week is not a budget fix, and it is not a gift. It's a trade: teachers in exchange for a Friday that someone has to cover. A district that pays for that Friday itself has made a fair trade. A district that can't should take Source B's advice, raise the levy and pay teachers directly, because at least that puts the cost where everyone can see it.\n   - \"A district should move to a four-day week only if it will do two things before the first Thursday dismissal: lengthen the remaining days enough to keep total instructional hours the same, and pay for a Friday option for children under twelve.\" - This conditional thesis earns the point because it names specific conditions instead of saying 'it depends.' It also sets up the line of reasoning the essay follows: what the district gains, who pays for it, and why each condition is needed.\n   - \"If it can't afford both, it should find another way to fix its teacher shortage.\" - The thesis also says what happens if the conditions aren't met, so it commits to a stance. The conclusion picks this up again with the levy alternative.\n   - \"Source F shows why. The biggest savings come from parking buses (165,000 dollars) and paying for fewer utilities, and there is no bar for teacher salaries at all\" - This interprets the chart instead of just repeating it. The missing salary bar explains why savings stay near 2 percent, and the writer connects Source F to the figures in Sources A and D.\n   - \"That is the same pattern at the scale of one job, one district, and a whole region, which makes it hard to dismiss as luck.\" - This is synthesis, not stacking: Sources A, C, and D are read as the same effect at three scales. That lets the writer say the hiring benefit is the real case for the schedule.\n   - \"It would be easy to write her off as one frustrated parent, except that Source D found 41 percent of parents of children under 12 reported new childcare costs\" - The writer uses Source D's survey data to show that Source B's single story reflects a wider pattern. Two sources are put in conversation here.\n   - \"local employers, probably the people covering for those parents' missing hours, oppose it 49 to 37\" - The Source E numbers are interpreted, not just reproduced. The writer offers a reason the employer row differs from the teacher row and ties the gap to the essay's point about who pays.\n   - \"Source D's achievement findings suggest the four-day schedule isn't the problem; lost hours are.\" - This reads Source D accurately: it does not claim four-day weeks raise scores. It then uses Source C's reading data to support the first condition in the thesis, which addresses evidence that cuts against adopting the schedule.\n   - \"So a first-year dip doesn't prove the schedule fails, but a closed gap doesn't prove it works by itself either.\" - The essay separates short-run effects from long-run ones and explains why neither settles the question alone. This is the kind of complexity the sophistication point rewards.\n   - \"A district without that grant is not Pinemont.\" - The writer asks whether one district's results transfer to another and builds on that question through the next sentences about Harrow Fork. Treating Pinemont and Harrow Fork as different districts keeps that line of thinking going.\n   - \"It's a trade: teachers in exchange for a Friday that someone has to cover.\" - The question of who gains and who pays runs from the opening paragraph to this conclusion and drives the whole decision. That is why the sophistication is sustained, not a single sentence. The plain, concrete style (for example, 'a day-care bill') also strengthens the argument.\n\nSample response, middle score (4/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 3, Sophistication 0):\nA school district should only move to a four-day week if it is willing to keep the same number of instructional hours and provide care for families on Fridays. Without those two things, the schedule saves a little money but costs students and parents too much.\n\nThe main reason districts do this is money and teachers, and the sources show both benefits are real but small or uneven. In Source A, Harrow Fork saved about 410,000 dollars, which is close to 2 percent of its budget. Source F shows that most of this came from transportation and utilities, and none of it came from teacher salaries. That means the savings are not very big, since salaries are the biggest cost. Source D agrees, saying no district reached the 5 percent that some expected. So if a district is only doing this to save money, it is probably not worth it.\n\nThe better argument for the four-day week is hiring teachers. Source A says Harrow Fork went from 14 unfilled positions to 5, and Source C says Pinemont Valley got eleven applicants for a job that used to get two. Source D also found that four-day districts had 2.4 times as many applicants. These all show that teachers like the schedule, which is also shown in Source E where 84 percent of teachers want to keep it. This is a strong point in favor of the schedule, because a district with empty classrooms has a serious problem.\n\nHowever, the schedule has real downsides for younger kids and for families. Source D says districts that cut total hours had math scores that fell behind by 3 percentile points, especially in grades 2 through 5. Source C backs this up because third and fourth graders lost ground in reading the first year. The Pinemont superintendent fixed it with tutoring, but that took extra effort and money. This is why I think a district must keep its hours up. Families also pay. Source B describes a parent paying 76 dollars a week for day care, and Source D says 41 percent of parents of young children had new childcare costs. Source E shows elementary parents are split, 48 percent to 42 percent, while local employers lean toward going back, which shows that the people who feel the cost are not happy.\n\nSome people might say the teacher hiring is more important than the childcare costs. That is a fair point, but a savings that just moves the cost to parents, as Source B says, is not really a savings. Pinemont Valley also says they \"got lucky, and then we worked,\" so other districts should not assume it will go the same way for them.\n\nIn conclusion, a district should adopt the four-day week only with preserved hours, a funded Friday option for young children, and a plan to check the results. If it cannot do those things, it should keep five days and find another way to pay teachers.\n   - \"A school district should only move to a four-day week if it is willing to keep the same number of instructional hours and provide care for families on Fridays.\" - Clear conditional thesis with two named conditions (preserved hours, Friday care), so it earns the thesis point. The conditions are stated plainly and the essay stays consistent with them.\n   - \"Source F shows that most of this came from transportation and utilities, and none of it came from teacher salaries.\" - Accurate and interpreted. The writer connects the chart to why savings are limited, and ties it to Source D. This is one of the stronger moments of synthesis.\n   - \"These all show that teachers like the schedule, which is also shown in Source E where 84 percent of teachers want to keep it.\" - Source A, C and D are grouped as describing hiring, but the commentary slips to 'teachers like the schedule' and does not explain that these are the same phenomenon at different scales. Source E's figure is mostly reproduced.\n   - \"Source E shows elementary parents are split, 48 percent to 42 percent, while local employers lean toward going back, which shows that the people who feel the cost are not happy.\" - Interprets Source E somewhat, but the essay does not tie the split to Source D's findings about younger children, and the commentary stays general.\n   - \"Source B describes a parent paying 76 dollars a week for day care, and Source D says 41 percent of parents of young children had new childcare costs.\" - Accurate figures from two sources, but they are placed side by side with little explanation of how the anecdote and the survey strengthen each other.\n   - \"Pinemont Valley also says they \"got lucky, and then we worked,\" so other districts should not assume it will go the same way for them.\" - Gestures toward whether one district's experience transfers to another, but it appears in one sentence and is not carried through the essay, so sophistication is not earned.\n\nSample response, low score (2/6; Thesis 0, Evidence and commentary 2, Sophistication 0):\nThe four day school week is a big decision for any district and there are a lot of opinions about it. Some people like it and some people do not. In this essay I will look at both sides and talk about what the sources say.\n\nSource A is about Harrow Fork which switched to four days. The district saved about 410,000 dollars and went from 14 unfilled teaching jobs to 5. That is a lot of money and it shows the four day week works for the budget. But the same article says day care centers added a Friday rate so not everyone is happy.\n\nSource B is written by a mom who owns a hardware store. She says she has to pay 38 dollars a child for Friday day care which is 76 dollars a week. This shows that the four day week costs families money. She also says younger kids get tired by two o'clock. I think this is a good point because little kids cannot focus that long.\n\nSource D is a research brief that looked at 23 districts. It says savings were between 1.2 and 2.5 percent and that 41 percent of parents had new childcare costs. It also says math scores went down in some districts. So the four day week has good things and bad things.\n\nIn conclusion, the four day week has pros and cons. Districts should think carefully about the money and the students and the families before they decide. If they do that they can make the right choice for their district.\n   - \"The four day school week is a big decision for any district and there are a lot of opinions about it.\" - Restates the prompt's premise and takes no position, so no thesis point is earned.\n   - \"Districts should think carefully about the money and the students and the families before they decide.\" - Conclusion only says 'weigh pros and cons'; no named conditions and no resolution, so it still fails the thesis row.\n   - \"That is a lot of money and it shows the four day week works for the budget.\" - Asserts the figure proves the point without noting it is only about 2 percent of the budget or that Source F shows where it came from; thin commentary.\n   - \"This shows that the four day week costs families money.\" - Uses Source B's figures accurately but does not connect them to Source A or D's cost data or to the idea of costs shifting from district to families.\n   - \"So the four day week has good things and bad things.\" - Source D is mostly summarized; the writer skips its key finding that outcomes depend on preserving instructional hours. Sources are stacked one by one, not synthesized.\n   - \"So the four day week has good things and bad things.\" - General level argument with no complex understanding of who gains, who pays, or whether Harrow Fork transfers to other districts.","explanation_en":"Scoring guide (ALTON practice rubric, not official College Board scoring). Each essay is worth 6 points.\nThesis (0-1): A conditional thesis earns the point only if the conditions are named, not merely gestured at ('it depends on the district'). A thesis that says the schedule 'has pros and cons' without resolving them does not earn the point.\n   - 1: States a defensible position on whether, or under what conditions, a district should move to a four-day week. The position may be unconditional (adopt / do not adopt), conditional (adopt only if instructional hours are preserved and a fifth-day option is funded), or alternative (reject the schedule and address teacher shortages another way). A thesis may appear anywhere in the essay and may be more than one sentence, but it must commit to a stance the rest of the essay can defend.\n   - 0: No thesis; a thesis that only restates the prompt (for example, 'There are many opinions about the four-day week'); a thesis that merely lists what the sources say without taking a position; or a thesis that answers a different question (such as whether teachers deserve raises) without connecting it to whether a district should adopt the schedule.\nEvidence and commentary (0-4): Count as 'using' a source only when the writer draws a specific claim, figure, or example from it and attributes it (Source A through Source F). Numbers from Source E or Source F count as evidence when the writer interprets them (for example, that employers oppose the schedule while teachers favor it, and why that gap matters), not when they are simply reproduced. Source C and Source D can be used for more than one stance; credit depends on the writer's explanation, not on which stance the writer takes.\n   - 4: Uses at least three sources with well-chosen, specific evidence throughout and consistently explains how each piece advances the argument. Sources are synthesized rather than stacked: the writer notices, for example, that Source A's reduction in vacancies, Source C's eleven applicants, and Source D's 2.4 multiplier describe the same phenomenon at three scales, or that Source E's split among elementary parents matches the grade-level findings in Source D. The essay engages with evidence that complicates its position and explains why the position still holds.\n   - 3: Uses at least three sources with specific, accurate evidence and explains how most of it supports a clear line of reasoning. The writer may put two sources in conversation (for instance, Source B's cost-transfer argument against Source F's breakdown of savings) but one or two pieces of evidence may be underexplained, or the essay may not account for the strongest counter-evidence.\n   - 2: Uses at least three sources but commentary is thin or repetitive: the writer quotes or paraphrases a figure (such as the 410,000 dollar savings or the 41 percent childcare figure) and then asserts that it proves the point without explaining why. Evidence may be loosely connected to the thesis, or the essay may treat each source in isolation without connecting them.\n   - 1: Mostly summary or paraphrase of fewer than three sources, or of three sources with no explanation of how any of them supports the writer's position. Sources may be mentioned in a list ('Sources A, C, and D all discuss savings') without being put to use.\n   - 0: Restates the thesis, offers only personal opinion with no source material, or uses sources in a way that shows misreading (for example, claiming Source D proves four-day weeks raise test scores).\nSophistication (0-1): Sophistication is not earned by taking the middle stance automatically. A conditional thesis with vague conditions is no more sophisticated than a one-sided essay; a one-sided essay that honestly confronts Source C's caveats or Source B's cost-transfer logic can earn the point.\n   - 1: Demonstrates a complex understanding of the issue through one or more of the following, carried through the essay rather than appearing in a single sentence: explaining why the same evidence (such as the modest 2 percent savings) can support opposing conclusions; identifying who gains and who pays and treating that distribution as central to the decision; recognizing that Harrow Fork and Pinemont Valley are different districts and reasoning about whether one's experience transfers to another; distinguishing between short-run effects (first-year reading dips) and long-run effects (closed gaps after repair work); or making a conditional argument whose conditions are drawn from and tested against the sources.\n   - 0: Argument stays at a general level ('the district should weigh costs and benefits'), relies on vague praise or blame, uses ornamental but empty language, or acknowledges a counterargument only to drop it.\nNote: Scores are additive: Row A (0-1) plus Row B (0-4) plus Row C (0-1), maximum 6.\nNote: An essay that earns 0 on Row A can still earn up to 2 on Row B if it uses sources, but cannot earn 3 or 4 because there is no position for the evidence to support.\nNote: Misstating a figure from a source (for example, calling the savings 5 percent) counts against accuracy on Row B; one small slip does not by itself lower the score, but a pattern does.\nNote: Writers who cite Source E or Source F must interpret the numbers; an essay that reproduces the table as a sentence earns no more credit than one that mentions the source by letter.\nNote: Essays that reject the four-day week and propose a different remedy for teacher shortages (such as Source B's levy proposal) are fully defensible, provided the writer still engages with the sources' evidence on the schedule itself.\n\nSample response, high score (6/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 4, Sophistication 1):\nWhen people argue about the four-day school week, they usually argue about Fridays. Teachers and teenagers picture a three-day weekend, and working parents picture a day-care bill. But the real question for a district isn't whether Fridays off are good or bad. It's who ends up paying for them. A district should move to a four-day week only if it will do two things before the first Thursday dismissal: lengthen the remaining days enough to keep total instructional hours the same, and pay for a Friday option for children under twelve. If it can't afford both, it should find another way to fix its teacher shortage.\n\nThe case for the schedule is not really about money, even though money is what gets announced. Harrow Fork saved about 410,000 dollars, \"close to 2 percent of its operating budget\" (Source A), and Source D found that savings across 23 districts topped out at 2.5 percent, with none reaching the 5 percent some had promised. Source F shows why. The biggest savings come from parking buses (165,000 dollars) and paying for fewer utilities, and there is no bar for teacher salaries at all, because teachers get paid the same whether school meets four days or five. A district hoping to balance its budget this way will be disappointed. What it can realistically buy is teachers. Harrow Fork went from 14 unfilled positions to 5 (Source A), Pinemont Valley's science posting went from two applications to eleven (Source C), and across the Meridian Basin applicants per vacancy rose 2.4 times (Source D). That is the same pattern at the scale of one job, one district, and a whole region, which makes it hard to dismiss as luck. For a rural district that can't find a science teacher, this is a serious argument.\n\nThe problem is that the hiring benefit goes to the district while the cost goes somewhere else. Marisol Ettinger, a store owner with two kids in elementary school, pays 76 dollars a week, over 2,700 dollars a year, for Friday care, and she argues that \"a saving that moves from the district's ledger to mine is not a saving; it is a transfer\" (Source B). It would be easy to write her off as one frustrated parent, except that Source D found 41 percent of parents of children under 12 reported new childcare costs and 22 percent cut their own work hours. Source E shows the same split: 84 percent of teachers want to keep the calendar, but elementary parents are divided 48 to 42, and local employers, probably the people covering for those parents' missing hours, oppose it 49 to 37. The groups who like the schedule most are the ones it was designed to help, and the people closest to the youngest children are the ones paying for it.\n\nThat is why the conditions matter. Source D's achievement findings suggest the four-day schedule isn't the problem; lost hours are. Districts that kept total hours saw growth \"statistically indistinguishable\" from five-day districts, while those that cut hours fell 3 percentile points behind in math, mostly in grades 2 through 5. Pinemont Valley's third and fourth graders slipped in reading the first year, and the gap only closed after the district moved reading to the morning and paid for Friday tutoring (Source C). So a first-year dip doesn't prove the schedule fails, but a closed gap doesn't prove it works by itself either. Castellane's own summary, \"We got lucky, and then we worked,\" reads less like a success story than a warning.\n\nEven Pinemont hasn't solved childcare. Its enrichment day serves about 140 children at two of five buildings, paid for mostly by a county grant, and Castellane tells critics to \"ask to see the waiting list.\" A district without that grant is not Pinemont. Harrow Fork has a library turning children away on Fridays and day-cares adding new rates, which suggests the town absorbed the cost instead of the district planning for it. Before a board copies Pinemont's results, it should ask whether it can copy Pinemont's repair work.\n\nThe four-day week is not a budget fix, and it is not a gift. It's a trade: teachers in exchange for a Friday that someone has to cover. A district that pays for that Friday itself has made a fair trade. A district that can't should take Source B's advice, raise the levy and pay teachers directly, because at least that puts the cost where everyone can see it.\n   - \"A district should move to a four-day week only if it will do two things before the first Thursday dismissal: lengthen the remaining days enough to keep total instructional hours the same, and pay for a Friday option for children under twelve.\" - This conditional thesis earns the point because it names specific conditions instead of saying 'it depends.' It also sets up the line of reasoning the essay follows: what the district gains, who pays for it, and why each condition is needed.\n   - \"If it can't afford both, it should find another way to fix its teacher shortage.\" - The thesis also says what happens if the conditions aren't met, so it commits to a stance. The conclusion picks this up again with the levy alternative.\n   - \"Source F shows why. The biggest savings come from parking buses (165,000 dollars) and paying for fewer utilities, and there is no bar for teacher salaries at all\" - This interprets the chart instead of just repeating it. The missing salary bar explains why savings stay near 2 percent, and the writer connects Source F to the figures in Sources A and D.\n   - \"That is the same pattern at the scale of one job, one district, and a whole region, which makes it hard to dismiss as luck.\" - This is synthesis, not stacking: Sources A, C, and D are read as the same effect at three scales. That lets the writer say the hiring benefit is the real case for the schedule.\n   - \"It would be easy to write her off as one frustrated parent, except that Source D found 41 percent of parents of children under 12 reported new childcare costs\" - The writer uses Source D's survey data to show that Source B's single story reflects a wider pattern. Two sources are put in conversation here.\n   - \"local employers, probably the people covering for those parents' missing hours, oppose it 49 to 37\" - The Source E numbers are interpreted, not just reproduced. The writer offers a reason the employer row differs from the teacher row and ties the gap to the essay's point about who pays.\n   - \"Source D's achievement findings suggest the four-day schedule isn't the problem; lost hours are.\" - This reads Source D accurately: it does not claim four-day weeks raise scores. It then uses Source C's reading data to support the first condition in the thesis, which addresses evidence that cuts against adopting the schedule.\n   - \"So a first-year dip doesn't prove the schedule fails, but a closed gap doesn't prove it works by itself either.\" - The essay separates short-run effects from long-run ones and explains why neither settles the question alone. This is the kind of complexity the sophistication point rewards.\n   - \"A district without that grant is not Pinemont.\" - The writer asks whether one district's results transfer to another and builds on that question through the next sentences about Harrow Fork. Treating Pinemont and Harrow Fork as different districts keeps that line of thinking going.\n   - \"It's a trade: teachers in exchange for a Friday that someone has to cover.\" - The question of who gains and who pays runs from the opening paragraph to this conclusion and drives the whole decision. That is why the sophistication is sustained, not a single sentence. The plain, concrete style (for example, 'a day-care bill') also strengthens the argument.\n\nSample response, middle score (4/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 3, Sophistication 0):\nA school district should only move to a four-day week if it is willing to keep the same number of instructional hours and provide care for families on Fridays. Without those two things, the schedule saves a little money but costs students and parents too much.\n\nThe main reason districts do this is money and teachers, and the sources show both benefits are real but small or uneven. In Source A, Harrow Fork saved about 410,000 dollars, which is close to 2 percent of its budget. Source F shows that most of this came from transportation and utilities, and none of it came from teacher salaries. That means the savings are not very big, since salaries are the biggest cost. Source D agrees, saying no district reached the 5 percent that some expected. So if a district is only doing this to save money, it is probably not worth it.\n\nThe better argument for the four-day week is hiring teachers. Source A says Harrow Fork went from 14 unfilled positions to 5, and Source C says Pinemont Valley got eleven applicants for a job that used to get two. Source D also found that four-day districts had 2.4 times as many applicants. These all show that teachers like the schedule, which is also shown in Source E where 84 percent of teachers want to keep it. This is a strong point in favor of the schedule, because a district with empty classrooms has a serious problem.\n\nHowever, the schedule has real downsides for younger kids and for families. Source D says districts that cut total hours had math scores that fell behind by 3 percentile points, especially in grades 2 through 5. Source C backs this up because third and fourth graders lost ground in reading the first year. The Pinemont superintendent fixed it with tutoring, but that took extra effort and money. This is why I think a district must keep its hours up. Families also pay. Source B describes a parent paying 76 dollars a week for day care, and Source D says 41 percent of parents of young children had new childcare costs. Source E shows elementary parents are split, 48 percent to 42 percent, while local employers lean toward going back, which shows that the people who feel the cost are not happy.\n\nSome people might say the teacher hiring is more important than the childcare costs. That is a fair point, but a savings that just moves the cost to parents, as Source B says, is not really a savings. Pinemont Valley also says they \"got lucky, and then we worked,\" so other districts should not assume it will go the same way for them.\n\nIn conclusion, a district should adopt the four-day week only with preserved hours, a funded Friday option for young children, and a plan to check the results. If it cannot do those things, it should keep five days and find another way to pay teachers.\n   - \"A school district should only move to a four-day week if it is willing to keep the same number of instructional hours and provide care for families on Fridays.\" - Clear conditional thesis with two named conditions (preserved hours, Friday care), so it earns the thesis point. The conditions are stated plainly and the essay stays consistent with them.\n   - \"Source F shows that most of this came from transportation and utilities, and none of it came from teacher salaries.\" - Accurate and interpreted. The writer connects the chart to why savings are limited, and ties it to Source D. This is one of the stronger moments of synthesis.\n   - \"These all show that teachers like the schedule, which is also shown in Source E where 84 percent of teachers want to keep it.\" - Source A, C and D are grouped as describing hiring, but the commentary slips to 'teachers like the schedule' and does not explain that these are the same phenomenon at different scales. Source E's figure is mostly reproduced.\n   - \"Source E shows elementary parents are split, 48 percent to 42 percent, while local employers lean toward going back, which shows that the people who feel the cost are not happy.\" - Interprets Source E somewhat, but the essay does not tie the split to Source D's findings about younger children, and the commentary stays general.\n   - \"Source B describes a parent paying 76 dollars a week for day care, and Source D says 41 percent of parents of young children had new childcare costs.\" - Accurate figures from two sources, but they are placed side by side with little explanation of how the anecdote and the survey strengthen each other.\n   - \"Pinemont Valley also says they \"got lucky, and then we worked,\" so other districts should not assume it will go the same way for them.\" - Gestures toward whether one district's experience transfers to another, but it appears in one sentence and is not carried through the essay, so sophistication is not earned.\n\nSample response, low score (2/6; Thesis 0, Evidence and commentary 2, Sophistication 0):\nThe four day school week is a big decision for any district and there are a lot of opinions about it. Some people like it and some people do not. In this essay I will look at both sides and talk about what the sources say.\n\nSource A is about Harrow Fork which switched to four days. The district saved about 410,000 dollars and went from 14 unfilled teaching jobs to 5. That is a lot of money and it shows the four day week works for the budget. But the same article says day care centers added a Friday rate so not everyone is happy.\n\nSource B is written by a mom who owns a hardware store. She says she has to pay 38 dollars a child for Friday day care which is 76 dollars a week. This shows that the four day week costs families money. She also says younger kids get tired by two o'clock. I think this is a good point because little kids cannot focus that long.\n\nSource D is a research brief that looked at 23 districts. It says savings were between 1.2 and 2.5 percent and that 41 percent of parents had new childcare costs. It also says math scores went down in some districts. So the four day week has good things and bad things.\n\nIn conclusion, the four day week has pros and cons. Districts should think carefully about the money and the students and the families before they decide. If they do that they can make the right choice for their district.\n   - \"The four day school week is a big decision for any district and there are a lot of opinions about it.\" - Restates the prompt's premise and takes no position, so no thesis point is earned.\n   - \"Districts should think carefully about the money and the students and the families before they decide.\" - Conclusion only says 'weigh pros and cons'; no named conditions and no resolution, so it still fails the thesis row.\n   - \"That is a lot of money and it shows the four day week works for the budget.\" - Asserts the figure proves the point without noting it is only about 2 percent of the budget or that Source F shows where it came from; thin commentary.\n   - \"This shows that the four day week costs families money.\" - Uses Source B's figures accurately but does not connect them to Source A or D's cost data or to the idea of costs shifting from district to families.\n   - \"So the four day week has good things and bad things.\" - Source D is mostly summarized; the writer skips its key finding that outcomes depend on preserving instructional hours. Sources are stacked one by one, not synthesized.\n   - \"So the four day week has good things and bad things.\" - General level argument with no complex understanding of who gains, who pays, or whether Harrow Fork transfers to other districts."},"set":{"explanation":"Scoring guide (ALTON practice rubric, not official College Board scoring). Each essay is worth 6 points.\nThesis (0-1): A conditional thesis earns the point only if the conditions are named, not merely gestured at ('it depends on the district'). A thesis that says the schedule 'has pros and cons' without resolving them does not earn the point.\n   - 1: States a defensible position on whether, or under what conditions, a district should move to a four-day week. The position may be unconditional (adopt / do not adopt), conditional (adopt only if instructional hours are preserved and a fifth-day option is funded), or alternative (reject the schedule and address teacher shortages another way). A thesis may appear anywhere in the essay and may be more than one sentence, but it must commit to a stance the rest of the essay can defend.\n   - 0: No thesis; a thesis that only restates the prompt (for example, 'There are many opinions about the four-day week'); a thesis that merely lists what the sources say without taking a position; or a thesis that answers a different question (such as whether teachers deserve raises) without connecting it to whether a district should adopt the schedule.\nEvidence and commentary (0-4): Count as 'using' a source only when the writer draws a specific claim, figure, or example from it and attributes it (Source A through Source F). Numbers from Source E or Source F count as evidence when the writer interprets them (for example, that employers oppose the schedule while teachers favor it, and why that gap matters), not when they are simply reproduced. Source C and Source D can be used for more than one stance; credit depends on the writer's explanation, not on which stance the writer takes.\n   - 4: Uses at least three sources with well-chosen, specific evidence throughout and consistently explains how each piece advances the argument. Sources are synthesized rather than stacked: the writer notices, for example, that Source A's reduction in vacancies, Source C's eleven applicants, and Source D's 2.4 multiplier describe the same phenomenon at three scales, or that Source E's split among elementary parents matches the grade-level findings in Source D. The essay engages with evidence that complicates its position and explains why the position still holds.\n   - 3: Uses at least three sources with specific, accurate evidence and explains how most of it supports a clear line of reasoning. The writer may put two sources in conversation (for instance, Source B's cost-transfer argument against Source F's breakdown of savings) but one or two pieces of evidence may be underexplained, or the essay may not account for the strongest counter-evidence.\n   - 2: Uses at least three sources but commentary is thin or repetitive: the writer quotes or paraphrases a figure (such as the 410,000 dollar savings or the 41 percent childcare figure) and then asserts that it proves the point without explaining why. Evidence may be loosely connected to the thesis, or the essay may treat each source in isolation without connecting them.\n   - 1: Uses two of the provided sources, or uses three or more sources mostly by summary or paraphrase with no explanation of how any of them supports the writer's position. Sources may be mentioned in a list ('Sources A, C, and D all discuss savings') without being put to use.\n   - 0: Restates the thesis, offers only personal opinion with no source material, uses fewer than two of the provided sources, or uses sources in a way that shows misreading (for example, claiming Source D proves four-day weeks raise test scores).\nSophistication (0-1): Sophistication is not earned by taking the middle stance automatically. A conditional thesis with vague conditions is no more sophisticated than a one-sided essay; a one-sided essay that honestly confronts Source C's caveats or Source B's cost-transfer logic can earn the point.\n   - 1: Demonstrates a complex understanding of the issue through one or more of the following, carried through the essay rather than appearing in a single sentence: explaining why the same evidence (such as the modest 2 percent savings) can support opposing conclusions; identifying who gains and who pays and treating that distribution as central to the decision; recognizing that Harrow Fork and Pinemont Valley are different districts and reasoning about whether one's experience transfers to another; distinguishing between short-run effects (first-year reading dips) and long-run effects (closed gaps after repair work); or making a conditional argument whose conditions are drawn from and tested against the sources.\n   - 0: Argument stays at a general level ('the district should weigh costs and benefits'), relies on vague praise or blame, uses ornamental but empty language, or acknowledges a counterargument only to drop it.\nNote: Scores are additive: Row A (0-1) plus Row B (0-4) plus Row C (0-1), maximum 6.\nNote: An essay that earns 0 on Row A can still earn credit on Row B for its use of sources. ALTON study guidance (not an official College Board cap): without a position there is usually no line of reasoning for the evidence to support, so scores of 3 or 4 on Row B are rare.\nNote: Misstating a figure from a source (for example, calling the savings 5 percent) counts against accuracy on Row B; one small slip does not by itself lower the score, but a pattern does.\nNote: Writers who cite Source E or Source F must interpret the numbers; an essay that reproduces the table as a sentence earns no more credit than one that mentions the source by letter.\nNote: Essays that reject the four-day week and propose a different remedy for teacher shortages (such as Source B's levy proposal) are fully defensible, provided the writer still engages with the sources' evidence on the schedule itself.\n\nSample response, high score (6/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 4, Sophistication 1):\nWhen people argue about the four-day school week, they usually argue about Fridays. Teachers and teenagers picture a three-day weekend, and working parents picture a day-care bill. But the real question for a district isn't whether Fridays off are good or bad. It's who ends up paying for them. A district should move to a four-day week only if it will do two things before the first Thursday dismissal: lengthen the remaining days enough to keep total instructional hours the same, and pay for a Friday option for children under twelve. If it can't afford both, it should find another way to fix its teacher shortage.\n\nThe case for the schedule is not really about money, even though money is what gets announced. Harrow Fork saved about 410,000 dollars, \"close to 2 percent of its operating budget\" (Source A), and Source D found that savings across 23 districts topped out at 2.5 percent, with none reaching the 5 percent some had promised. Source F shows why. The biggest savings come from parking buses (165,000 dollars) and paying for fewer utilities, and there is no bar for teacher salaries at all, because teachers get paid the same whether school meets four days or five. A district hoping to balance its budget this way will be disappointed. What it can realistically buy is teachers. Harrow Fork went from 14 unfilled positions to 5 (Source A), Pinemont Valley's science posting went from two applications to eleven (Source C), and across the Meridian Basin applicants per vacancy rose 2.4 times (Source D). That is the same pattern at the scale of one job, one district, and a whole region, which makes it hard to dismiss as luck. For a rural district that can't find a science teacher, this is a serious argument.\n\nThe problem is that the hiring benefit goes to the district while the cost goes somewhere else. Marisol Ettinger, a store owner with two kids in elementary school, pays 76 dollars a week, over 2,700 dollars a year, for Friday care, and she argues that \"a saving that moves from the district's ledger to mine is not a saving; it is a transfer\" (Source B). It would be easy to write her off as one frustrated parent, except that Source D found 41 percent of parents of children under 12 reported new childcare costs and 22 percent cut their own work hours. Source E shows the same split: 84 percent of teachers want to keep the calendar, but elementary parents are divided 48 to 42, and local employers, probably the people covering for those parents' missing hours, oppose it 49 to 37. The groups who like the schedule most are the ones it was designed to help, and the people closest to the youngest children are the ones paying for it.\n\nThat is why the conditions matter. Source D's achievement findings suggest the four-day schedule isn't the problem; lost hours are. Districts that kept total hours saw growth \"statistically indistinguishable\" from five-day districts, while those that cut hours fell 3 percentile points behind in math, mostly in grades 2 through 5. Pinemont Valley's third and fourth graders slipped in reading the first year, and the gap only closed after the district moved reading to the morning and paid for Friday tutoring (Source C). So a first-year dip doesn't prove the schedule fails, but a closed gap doesn't prove it works by itself either. Castellane's own summary, \"We got lucky, and then we worked,\" reads less like a success story than a warning.\n\nEven Pinemont hasn't solved childcare. Its enrichment day serves about 140 children at two of five buildings, paid for mostly by a county