This is synthesis, not stacking: Sources A, C, and D are read as the same effect at three scales. That lets the writer say the hiring benefit is the real case for the schedule.\n   - \"It would be easy to write her off as one frustrated parent, except that Source D found 41 percent of parents of children under 12 reported new childcare costs\" - The writer uses Source D's survey data to show that Source B's single story reflects a wider pattern. Two sources are put in conversation here.\n   - \"local employers, probably the people covering for those parents' missing hours, oppose it 49 to 37\" - The Source E numbers are interpreted, not just reproduced. The writer offers a reason the employer row differs from the teacher row and ties the gap to the essay's point about who pays.\n   - \"Source D's achievement findings suggest the four-day schedule isn't the problem; lost hours are.\" - This reads Source D accurately: it does not claim four-day weeks raise scores. It then uses Source C's reading data to support the first condition in the thesis, which addresses evidence that cuts against adopting the schedule.\n   - \"So a first-year dip doesn't prove the schedule fails, but a closed gap doesn't prove it works by itself either.\" - The essay separates short-run effects from long-run ones and explains why neither settles the question alone. This is the kind of complexity the sophistication point rewards.\n   - \"A district without that grant is not Pinemont.\" - The writer asks whether one district's results transfer to another and builds on that question through the next sentences about Harrow Fork. Treating Pinemont and Harrow Fork as different districts keeps that line of thinking going.\n   - \"It's a trade: teachers in exchange for a Friday that someone has to cover.\" - The question of who gains and who pays runs from the opening paragraph to this conclusion and drives the whole decision. That is why the sophistication is sustained, not a single sentence. The plain, concrete style (for example, 'a day-care bill') also strengthens the argument.\n\nSample response, middle score (4/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 3, Sophistication 0):\nA school district should only move to a four-day week if it is willing to keep the same number of instructional hours and provide care for families on Fridays. Without those two things, the schedule saves a little money but costs students and parents too much.\n\nThe main reason districts do this is money and teachers, and the sources show both benefits are real but small or uneven. In Source A, Harrow Fork saved about 410,000 dollars, which is close to 2 percent of its budget. Source F shows that most of this came from transportation and utilities, and none of it came from teacher salaries. That means the savings are not very big, since salaries are the biggest cost. Source D agrees, saying no district reached the 5 percent that some expected. So if a district is only doing this to save money, it is probably not worth it.\n\nThe better argument for the four-day week is hiring teachers. Source A says Harrow Fork went from 14 unfilled positions to 5, and Source C says Pinemont Valley got eleven applicants for a job that used to get two. Source D also found that four-day districts had 2.4 times as many applicants. These all show that teachers like the schedule, which is also shown in Source E where 84 percent of teachers want to keep it. This is a strong point in favor of the schedule, because a district with empty classrooms has a serious problem.\n\nHowever, the schedule has real downsides for younger kids and for families. Source D says districts that cut total hours had math scores that fell behind by 3 percentile points, especially in grades 2 through 5. Source C backs this up because third and fourth graders lost ground in reading the first year. The Pinemont superintendent fixed it with tutoring, but that took extra effort and money. This is why I think a district must keep its hours up. Families also pay. Source B describes a parent paying 76 dollars a week for day care, and Source D says 41 percent of parents of young children had new childcare costs. Source E shows elementary parents are split, 48 percent to 42 percent, while local employers lean toward going back, which shows that the people who feel the cost are not happy.\n\nSome people might say the teacher hiring is more important than the childcare costs. That is a fair point, but a savings that just moves the cost to parents, as Source B says, is not really a savings. Pinemont Valley also says they \"got lucky, and then we worked,\" so other districts should not assume it will go the same way for them.\n\nIn conclusion, a district should adopt the four-day week only with preserved hours, a funded Friday option for young children, and a plan to check the results. If it cannot do those things, it should keep five days and find another way to pay teachers.\n   - \"A school district should only move to a four-day week if it is willing to keep the same number of instructional hours and provide care for families on Fridays.\" - Clear conditional thesis with two named conditions (preserved hours, Friday care), so it earns the thesis point. The conditions are stated plainly and the essay stays consistent with them.\n   - \"Source F shows that most of this came from transportation and utilities, and none of it came from teacher salaries.\" - Accurate and interpreted. The writer connects the chart to why savings are limited, and ties it to Source D. This is one of the stronger moments of synthesis.\n   - \"These all show that teachers like the schedule, which is also shown in Source E where 84 percent of teachers want to keep it.\" - Source A, C and D are grouped as describing hiring, but the commentary slips to 'teachers like the schedule' and does not explain that these are the same phenomenon at different scales. Source E's figure is mostly reproduced.\n   - \"Source E shows elementary parents are split, 48 percent to 42 percent, while local employers lean toward going back, which shows that the people who feel the cost are not happy.\" - Interprets Source E somewhat, but the essay does not tie the split to Source D's findings about younger children, and the commentary stays general.\n   - \"Source B describes a parent paying 76 dollars a week for day care, and Source D says 41 percent of parents of young children had new childcare costs.\" - Accurate figures from two sources, but they are placed side by side with little explanation of how the anecdote and the survey strengthen each other.\n   - \"Pinemont Valley also says they \"got lucky, and then we worked,\" so other districts should not assume it will go the same way for them.\" - Gestures toward whether one district's experience transfers to another, but it appears in one sentence and is not carried through the essay, so sophistication is not earned.\n\nSample response, low score (2/6; Thesis 0, Evidence and commentary 2, Sophistication 0):\nThe four day school week is a big decision for any district and there are a lot of opinions about it. Some people like it and some people do not. In this essay I will look at both sides and talk about what the sources say.\n\nSource A is about Harrow Fork which switched to four days. The district saved about 410,000 dollars and went from 14 unfilled teaching jobs to 5. That is a lot of money and it shows the four day week works for the budget. But the same article says day care centers added a Friday rate so not everyone is happy.\n\nSource B is written by a mom who owns a hardware store. She says she has to pay 38 dollars a child for Friday day care which is 76 dollars a week. This shows that the four day week costs families money. She also says younger kids get tired by two o'clock. I think this is a good point because little kids cannot focus that long.\n\nSource D is a research brief that looked at 23 districts. It says savings were between 1.2 and 2.5 percent and that 41 percent of parents had new childcare costs. It also says math scores went down in some districts. So the four day week has good things and bad things.\n\nIn conclusion, the four day week has pros and cons. Districts should think carefully about the money and the students and the families before they decide. If they do that they can make the right choice for their district.\n   - \"The four day school week is a big decision for any district and there are a lot of opinions about it.\" - Restates the prompt's premise and takes no position, so no thesis point is earned.\n   - \"Districts should think carefully about the money and the students and the families before they decide.\" - Conclusion only says 'weigh pros and cons'; no named conditions and no resolution, so it still fails the thesis row.\n   - \"That is a lot of money and it shows the four day week works for the budget.\" - Asserts the figure proves the point without noting it is only about 2 percent of the budget or that Source F shows where it came from; thin commentary.\n   - \"This shows that the four day week costs families money.\" - Uses Source B's figures accurately but does not connect them to Source A or D's cost data or to the idea of costs shifting from district to families.\n   - \"So the four day week has good things and bad things.\" - Source D is mostly summarized; the writer skips its key finding that outcomes depend on preserving instructional hours. Sources are stacked one by one, not synthesized.\n   - \"So the four day week has good things and bad things.\" - General level argument with no complex understanding of who gains, who pays, or whether Harrow Fork transfers to other districts.","explanation_en":"Scoring guide (ALTON practice rubric, not official College Board scoring). Each essay is worth 6 points.\nThesis (0-1): A conditional thesis earns the point only if the conditions are named, not merely gestured at ('it depends on the district'). A thesis that says the schedule 'has pros and cons' without resolving them does not earn the point.\n   - 1: States a defensible position on whether, or under what conditions, a district should move to a four-day week. The position may be unconditional (adopt / do not adopt), conditional (adopt only if instructional hours are preserved and a fifth-day option is funded), or alternative (reject the schedule and address teacher shortages another way). A thesis may appear anywhere in the essay and may be more than one sentence, but it must commit to a stance the rest of the essay can defend.\n   - 0: No thesis; a thesis that only restates the prompt (for example, 'There are many opinions about the four-day week'); a thesis that merely lists what the sources say without taking a position; or a thesis that answers a different question (such as whether teachers deserve raises) without connecting it to whether a district should adopt the schedule.\nEvidence and commentary (0-4): Count as 'using' a source only when the writer draws a specific claim, figure, or example from it and attributes it (Source A through Source F). Numbers from Source E or Source F count as evidence when the writer interprets them (for example, that employers oppose the schedule while teachers favor it, and why that gap matters), not when they are simply reproduced. Source C and Source D can be used for more than one stance; credit depends on the writer's explanation, not on which stance the writer takes.\n   - 4: Uses at least three sources with well-chosen, specific evidence throughout and consistently explains how each piece advances the argument. Sources are synthesized rather than stacked: the writer notices, for example, that Source A's reduction in vacancies, Source C's eleven applicants, and Source D's 2.4 multiplier describe the same phenomenon at three scales, or that Source E's split among elementary parents matches the grade-level findings in Source D. The essay engages with evidence that complicates its position and explains why the position still holds.\n   - 3: Uses at least three sources with specific, accurate evidence and explains how most of it supports a clear line of reasoning. The writer may put two sources in conversation (for instance, Source B's cost-transfer argument against Source F's breakdown of savings) but one or two pieces of evidence may be underexplained, or the essay may not account for the strongest counter-evidence.\n   - 2: Uses at least three sources but commentary is thin or repetitive: the writer quotes or paraphrases a figure (such as the 410,000 dollar savings or the 41 percent childcare figure) and then asserts that it proves the point without explaining why. Evidence may be loosely connected to the thesis, or the essay may treat each source in isolation without connecting them.\n   - 1: Uses two of the provided sources, or uses three or more sources mostly by summary or paraphrase with no explanation of how any of them supports the writer's position. Sources may be mentioned in a list ('Sources A, C, and D all discuss savings') without being put to use.\n   - 0: Restates the thesis, offers only personal opinion with no source material, uses fewer than two of the provided sources, or uses sources in a way that shows misreading (for example, claiming Source D proves four-day weeks raise test scores).\nSophistication (0-1): Sophistication is not earned by taking the middle stance automatically. A conditional thesis with vague conditions is no more sophisticated than a one-sided essay; a one-sided essay that honestly confronts Source C's caveats or Source B's cost-transfer logic can earn the point.\n   - 1: Demonstrates a complex understanding of the issue through one or more of the following, carried through the essay rather than appearing in a single sentence: explaining why the same evidence (such as the modest 2 percent savings) can support opposing conclusions; identifying who gains and who pays and treating that distribution as central to the decision; recognizing that Harrow Fork and Pinemont Valley are different districts and reasoning about whether one's experience transfers to another; distinguishing between short-run effects (first-year reading dips) and long-run effects (closed gaps after repair work); or making a conditional argument whose conditions are drawn from and tested against the sources.\n   - 0: Argument stays at a general level ('the district should weigh costs and benefits'), relies on vague praise or blame, uses ornamental but empty language, or acknowledges a counterargument only to drop it.\nNote: Scores are additive: Row A (0-1) plus Row B (0-4) plus Row C (0-1), maximum 6.\nNote: An essay that earns 0 on Row A can still earn credit on Row B for its use of sources. ALTON study guidance (not an official College Board cap): without a position there is usually no line of reasoning for the evidence to support, so scores of 3 or 4 on Row B are rare.\nNote: Misstating a figure from a source (for example, calling the savings 5 percent) counts against accuracy on Row B; one small slip does not by itself lower the score, but a pattern does.\nNote: Writers who cite Source E or Source F must interpret the numbers; an essay that reproduces the table as a sentence earns no more credit than one that mentions the source by letter.\nNote: Essays that reject the four-day week and propose a different remedy for teacher shortages (such as Source B's levy proposal) are fully defensible, provided the writer still engages with the sources' evidence on the schedule itself.\n\nSample response, high score (6/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 4, Sophistication 1):\nWhen people argue about the four-day school week, they usually argue about Fridays. Teachers and teenagers picture a three-day weekend, and working parents picture a day-care bill. But the real question for a district isn't whether Fridays off are good or bad. It's who ends up paying for them. A district should move to a four-day week only if it will do two things before the first Thursday dismissal: lengthen the remaining days enough to keep total instructional hours the same, and pay for a Friday option for children under twelve. If it can't afford both, it should find another way to fix its teacher shortage.\n\nThe case for the schedule is not really about money, even though money is what gets announced. Harrow Fork saved about 410,000 dollars, \"close to 2 percent of its operating budget\" (Source A), and Source D found that savings across 23 districts topped out at 2.5 percent, with none reaching the 5 percent some had promised. Source F shows why. The biggest savings come from parking buses (165,000 dollars) and paying for fewer utilities, and there is no bar for teacher salaries at all, because teachers get paid the same whether school meets four days or five. A district hoping to balance its budget this way will be disappointed. What it can realistically buy is teachers. Harrow Fork went from 14 unfilled positions to 5 (Source A), Pinemont Valley's science posting went from two applications to eleven (Source C), and across the Meridian Basin applicants per vacancy rose 2.4 times (Source D). That is the same pattern at the scale of one job, one district, and a whole region, which makes it hard to dismiss as luck. For a rural district that can't find a science teacher, this is a serious argument.\n\nThe problem is that the hiring benefit goes to the district while the cost goes somewhere else. Marisol Ettinger, a store owner with two kids in elementary school, pays 76 dollars a week, over 2,700 dollars a year, for Friday care, and she argues that \"a saving that moves from the district's ledger to mine is not a saving; it is a transfer\" (Source B). It would be easy to write her off as one frustrated parent, except that Source D found 41 percent of parents of children under 12 reported new childcare costs and 22 percent cut their own work hours. Source E shows the same split: 84 percent of teachers want to keep the calendar, but elementary parents are divided 48 to 42, and local employers, probably the people covering for those parents' missing hours, oppose it 49 to 37. The groups who like the schedule most are the ones it was designed to help, and the people closest to the youngest children are the ones paying for it.\n\nThat is why the conditions matter. Source D's achievement findings suggest the four-day schedule isn't the problem; lost hours are. Districts that kept total hours saw growth \"statistically indistinguishable\" from five-day districts, while those that cut hours fell 3 percentile points behind in math, mostly in grades 2 through 5. Pinemont Valley's third and fourth graders slipped in reading the first year, and the gap only closed after the district moved reading to the morning and paid for Friday tutoring (Source C). So a first-year dip doesn't prove the schedule fails, but a closed gap doesn't prove it works by itself either. Castellane's own summary, \"We got lucky, and then we worked,\" reads less like a success story than a warning.\n\nEven Pinemont hasn't solved childcare. Its enrichment day serves about 140 children at two of five buildings, paid for mostly by a county grant, and Castellane tells critics to \"ask to see the waiting list.\" A district without that grant is not Pinemont. Harrow Fork has a library turning children away on Fridays and day-cares adding new rates, which suggests the town absorbed the cost instead of the district planning for it. Before a board copies Pinemont's results, it should ask whether it can copy Pinemont's repair work.\n\nThe four-day week is not a budget fix, and it is not a gift. It's a trade: teachers in exchange for a Friday that someone has to cover. A district that pays for that Friday itself has made a fair trade. A district that can't should take Source B's advice, raise the levy and pay teachers directly, because at least that puts the cost where everyone can see it.\n   - \"A district should move to a four-day week only if it will do two things before the first Thursday dismissal: lengthen the remaining days enough to keep total instructional hours the same, and pay for a Friday option for children under twelve.\" - This conditional thesis earns the point because it names specific conditions instead of saying 'it depends.' It also sets up the line of reasoning the essay follows: what the district gains, who pays for it, and why each condition is needed.\n   - \"If it can't afford both, it should find another way to fix its teacher shortage.\" - The thesis also says what happens if the conditions aren't met, so it commits to a stance. The conclusion picks this up again with the levy alternative.\n   - \"Source F shows why. The biggest savings come from parking buses (165,000 dollars) and paying for fewer utilities, and there is no bar for teacher salaries at all\" - This interprets the chart instead of just repeating it. The missing salary bar explains why savings stay near 2 percent, and the writer connects Source F to the figures in Sources A and D.\n   - \"That is the same pattern at the scale of one job, one district, and a whole region, which makes it hard to dismiss as luck.\" - This is synthesis, not stacking: Sources A, C, and D are read as the same effect at three scales. That lets the writer say the hiring benefit is the real case for the schedule.\n   - \"It would be easy to write her off as one frustrated parent, except that Source D found 41 percent of parents of children under 12 reported new childcare costs\" - The writer uses Source D's survey data to show that Source B's single story reflects a wider pattern. Two sources are put in conversation here.\n   - \"local employers, probably the people covering for those parents' missing hours, oppose it 49 to 37\" - The Source E numbers are interpreted, not just reproduced. The writer offers a reason the employer row differs from the teacher row and ties the gap to the essay's point about who pays.\n   - \"Source D's achievement findings suggest the four-day schedule isn't the problem; lost hours are.\" - This reads Source D accurately: it does not claim four-day weeks raise scores. It then uses Source C's reading data to support the first condition in the thesis, which addresses evidence that cuts against adopting the schedule.\n   - \"So a first-year dip doesn't prove the schedule fails, but a closed gap doesn't prove it works by itself either.\" - The essay separates short-run effects from long-run ones and explains why neither settles the question alone. This is the kind of complexity the sophistication point rewards.\n   - \"A district without that grant is not Pinemont.\" - The writer asks whether one district's results transfer to another and builds on that question through the next sentences about Harrow Fork. Treating Pinemont and Harrow Fork as different districts keeps that line of thinking going.\n   - \"It's a trade: teachers in exchange for a Friday that someone has to cover.\" - The question of who gains and who pays runs from the opening paragraph to this conclusion and drives the whole decision. That is why the sophistication is sustained, not a single sentence. The plain, concrete style (for example, 'a day-care bill') also strengthens the argument.\n\nSample response, middle score (4/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 3, Sophistication 0):\nA school district should only move to a four-day week if it is willing to keep the same number of instructional hours and provide care for families on Fridays. Without those two things, the schedule saves a little money but costs students and parents too much.\n\nThe main reason districts do this is money and teachers, and the sources show both benefits are real but small or uneven. In Source A, Harrow Fork saved about 410,000 dollars, which is close to 2 percent of its budget. Source F shows that most of this came from transportation and utilities, and none of it came from teacher salaries. That means the savings are not very big, since salaries are the biggest cost. Source D agrees, saying no district reached the 5 percent that some expected. So if a district is only doing this to save money, it is probably not worth it.\n\nThe better argument for the four-day week is hiring teachers. Source A says Harrow Fork went from 14 unfilled positions to 5, and Source C says Pinemont Valley got eleven applicants for a job that used to get two. Source D also found that four-day districts had 2.4 times as many applicants. These all show that teachers like the schedule, which is also shown in Source E where 84 percent of teachers want to keep it. This is a strong point in favor of the schedule, because a district with empty classrooms has a serious problem.\n\nHowever, the schedule has real downsides for younger kids and for families. Source D says districts that cut total hours had math scores that fell behind by 3 percentile points, especially in grades 2 through 5. Source C backs this up because third and fourth graders lost ground in reading the first year. The Pinemont superintendent fixed it with tutoring, but that took extra effort and money. This is why I think a district must keep its hours up. Families also pay. Source B describes a parent paying 76 dollars a week for day care, and Source D says 41 percent of parents of young children had new childcare costs. Source E shows elementary parents are split, 48 percent to 42 percent, while local employers lean toward going back, which shows that the people who feel the cost are not happy.\n\nSome people might say the teacher hiring is more important than the childcare costs. That is a fair point, but a savings that just moves the cost to parents, as Source B says, is not really a savings. Pinemont Valley also says they \"got lucky, and then we worked,\" so other districts should not assume it will go the same way for them.\n\nIn conclusion, a district should adopt the four-day week only with preserved hours, a funded Friday option for young children, and a plan to check the results. If it cannot do those things, it should keep five days and find another way to pay teachers.\n   - \"A school district should only move to a four-day week if it is willing to keep the same number of instructional hours and provide care for families on Fridays.\" - Clear conditional thesis with two named conditions (preserved hours, Friday care), so it earns the thesis point. The conditions are stated plainly and the essay stays consistent with them.\n   - \"Source F shows that most of this came from transportation and utilities, and none of it came from teacher salaries.\" - Accurate and interpreted. The writer connects the chart to why savings are limited, and ties it to Source D. This is one of the stronger moments of synthesis.\n   - \"These all show that teachers like the schedule, which is also shown in Source E where 84 percent of teachers want to keep it.\" - Source A, C and D are grouped as describing hiring, but the commentary slips to 'teachers like the schedule' and does not explain that these are the same phenomenon at different scales. Source E's figure is mostly reproduced.\n   - \"Source E shows elementary parents are split, 48 percent to 42 percent, while local employers lean toward going back, which shows that the people who feel the cost are not happy.\" - Interprets Source E somewhat, but the essay does not tie the split to Source D's findings about younger children, and the commentary stays general.\n   - \"Source B describes a parent paying 76 dollars a week for day care, and Source D says 41 percent of parents of young children had new childcare costs.\" - Accurate figures from two sources, but they are placed side by side with little explanation of how the anecdote and the survey strengthen each other.\n   - \"Pinemont Valley also says they \"got lucky, and then we worked,\" so other districts should not assume it will go the same way for them.\" - Gestures toward whether one district's experience transfers to another, but it appears in one sentence and is not carried through the essay, so sophistication is not earned.\n\nSample response, low score (2/6; Thesis 0, Evidence and commentary 2, Sophistication 0):\nThe four day school week is a big decision for any district and there are a lot of opinions about it. Some people like it and some people do not. In this essay I will look at both sides and talk about what the sources say.\n\nSource A is about Harrow Fork which switched to four days. The district saved about 410,000 dollars and went from 14 unfilled teaching jobs to 5. That is a lot of money and it shows the four day week works for the budget. But the same article says day care centers added a Friday rate so not everyone is happy.\n\nSource B is written by a mom who owns a hardware store. She says she has to pay 38 dollars a child for Friday day care which is 76 dollars a week. This shows that the four day week costs families money. She also says younger kids get tired by two o'clock. I think this is a good point because little kids cannot focus that long.\n\nSource D is a research brief that looked at 23 districts. It says savings were between 1.2 and 2.5 percent and that 41 percent of parents had new childcare costs. It also says math scores went down in some districts. So the four day week has good things and bad things.\n\nIn conclusion, the four day week has pros and cons. Districts should think carefully about the money and the students and the families before they decide. If they do that they can make the right choice for their district.\n   - \"The four day school week is a big decision for any district and there are a lot of opinions about it.\" - Restates the prompt's premise and takes no position, so no thesis point is earned.\n   - \"Districts should think carefully about the money and the students and the families before they decide.\" - Conclusion only says 'weigh pros and cons'; no named conditions and no resolution, so it still fails the thesis row.\n   - \"That is a lot of money and it shows the four day week works for the budget.\" - Asserts the figure proves the point without noting it is only about 2 percent of the budget or that Source F shows where it came from; thin commentary.\n   - \"This shows that the four day week costs families money.\" - Uses Source B's figures accurately but does not connect them to Source A or D's cost data or to the idea of costs shifting from district to families.\n   - \"So the four day week has good things and bad things.\" - Source D is mostly summarized; the writer skips its key finding that outcomes depend on preserving instructional hours. Sources are stacked one by one, not synthesized.\n   - \"So the four day week has good things and bad things.\" - General level argument with no complex understanding of who gains, who pays, or whether Harrow Fork transfers to other districts."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-15T15:30:39.091Z","issues":[],"alt":"Where Harrow Fork's First-Year Savings Came From. Bar chart. Horizontal axis: Budget category. Vertical axis: Estimated annual savings (thousands of dollars). Transport: 165, Utilities: 95, Substitutes: 80, Food service: 45, Custodial: 25."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R6-ELA1-FRQ1","fromVersionId":"e9a902d1-3a57-4fa7-8128-c927bafc38a4","fromVersionNo":1},"ap":{"candidate_key":"el-rv:ap_english_language-fb1-k0","item_index":0}},{"patch_id":"R6-ELA1-FRQ3","problem_id":"95f6fb44-e162-4c88-9966-3cf4aa509017","from_version_id":"84cfec0c-cc16-4d17-8a74-915bffbda48c","expect":{"explanation":"Scoring guide (ALTON practice rubric, not official College Board scoring). Each essay is worth 6 points.\nThesis (0-1): The thesis must be about the extent of limits, not merely whether cars exist. 'Cities should limit cars somewhat' earns the point only if the essay makes clear, in the opening or the conclusion, what 'somewhat' means or on what the degree depends. A thesis may appear anywhere in the essay, including the conclusion.\n   - 1: A thesis that takes a defensible, arguable position on the degree to which a city should restrict private cars downtown. The position may be strong (cars should be nearly excluded; cars should be left alone), moderate, or conditional (limits should depend on time of day, size of the city, availability of transit, or the kind of business on the street). A thesis that presents a condition or a qualification still earns the point so long as it commits to something a reader could dispute.\n   - 0: No thesis, or a thesis that only restates the prompt (for example, 'Cities must decide how much to limit cars downtown'), or a thesis that merely summarizes Teague's column, or a thesis that takes a position on a different question (such as whether cars are bad for the environment in general) without connecting it to how far a city should limit cars in its downtown.\nEvidence and Commentary (0-4): Evidence counts whether drawn from the stimulus, from the writer's own town, from reading, or from reasoned hypotheticals, as long as it is specific. The writer is not required to use Teague's column at all. Commentary earns credit when it explains why a given example bears on the degree of limits a city should set, not merely on whether cars are good or bad. An essay that argues at length about climate or public health earns at most a 2 unless it connects those concerns to a decision about how much of downtown to close.\n   - 4: Provides specific, well-chosen evidence and consistently explains how each piece advances the argument. The reasoning is organized around the writer's own distinction (for instance, the difference between people who must reach downtown and people who choose to stay there, or between delivery traffic and commuter traffic, or between cities with and without working transit), and the writer uses that distinction to decide what should and should not be limited. Counterexamples are addressed, not merely mentioned; the nurse's late-night walk, for example, is treated as a problem the writer's position must solve.\n   - 3: Provides specific evidence and explains how it supports the claim, with a line of reasoning the reader can follow from paragraph to paragraph. Commentary may be uneven: one body paragraph explains carefully why a particular kind of business depends on curbside access while another simply asserts that pedestrian streets are 'more pleasant.' The writer may acknowledge an objection but treat it briefly or dismiss it rather than answer it.\n   - 2: Provides some specific evidence, such as a real or plausibly described downtown the writer has visited, a family member's commute, or the baker and the hardware store from the column, but the commentary is thin or repetitive: the writer says the evidence 'proves' the point without explaining the mechanism. Typical 2-level responses also wander among unrelated points (parking, pollution, crime, noise) without a line of reasoning that orders them.\n   - 1: Mostly generalities: the writer asserts that 'studies show' or 'everyone knows' that walkable downtowns thrive or that drivers abandon car-free areas, without a single specific example, scene, or observation. Or the writer lists the three people from the column (hardware store owner, baker, nurse) and says they show the issue is complicated, with no argument built from them.\n   - 0: Only restates the thesis, or offers evidence that has no bearing on the question of car limits (for example, a general essay on pollution with no connection to downtown streets), or consists entirely of summary of the Teague column.\nSophistication (0-1): Sophistication is most often earned by essays that turn Teague's three people into a test for a principle rather than three separate complaints: for example, an essay that argues the nurse and the hardware customer are different problems (one solved by time-of-day rules, the other perhaps not solvable) and says so. Merely proposing a compromise ('close streets on weekends') does not earn the point unless the writer explains why that compromise fits the competing purposes of a downtown.\n   - 1: The essay does one or more of the following in a sustained way, not in a single sentence: works through the real tension between access and lingering that Teague identifies and shows how the writer's position resolves it in particular cases; situates the question in a wider context (who downtown is for, how a city treats people who cannot walk far or who work odd hours, what happens to the highway strip when downtown closes); considers unintended consequences of the writer's own position and adjusts the position accordingly; or sustains a voice and style that are precise, controlled, and persuasive throughout.\n   - 0: The argument is one-sided or treats the question as settled; or the essay gestures at complexity ('there are many sides to this issue') without actually working through tensions; or the prose is ornate but does not sharpen the argument.\nNote: Score the essay as a whole; a strong conclusion can supply the thesis for Row A.\nNote: Do not reward or penalize the writer's political stance; a well-argued case for leaving cars alone can earn a 6, and a poorly argued case for a full car ban can earn a 2.\nNote: The stimulus is optional. Essays that never mention Verrick Falls are scored on exactly the same criteria.\nNote: Reasoned hypotheticals ('imagine a pharmacy whose customers cannot carry a week of groceries eight blocks') count as evidence if they are concrete and the writer explains what they show.\nNote: Row B scores of 3 and 4 require a visible line of reasoning; if the body paragraphs could be shuffled without loss, the ceiling is 2.\nNote: An essay that argues only about a single consideration (for example, only emissions) with excellent commentary may earn a 3 on Row B but is unlikely to earn the sophistication point because it does not engage the tension the prompt names.\n\nSample response, high score (6/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 4, Sophistication 1):\nMost of the fighting about car-free downtowns assumes there are only two choices: close the street or leave it alone. Marisol Teague's column about Carrow Street shows why that framing doesn't work. The hardware store owner, the baker, and the nurse are not just three opinions about cars. They are three different kinds of trips. A city should limit private cars downtown only on the blocks and during the hours when people come to stay, and it should keep car access wherever people come to fetch something heavy or have to leave after dark. How much a city restricts cars should depend on why people are on the street, not on one rule for all six blocks.\n\nStart with the hardware store and the bakery, since they seem like opposites. The baker's customers come to linger. They buy a roll, sit down, maybe buy another, and Teague says people linger \"when nothing is idling at their backs.\" For them the closed street is the product. The hardware customer is on an errand. Nobody lingers with a stack of two-by-fours, and nobody carries them eight blocks to a garage. So the closure costs the hardware store exactly the thing it sells, which is convenience. The fix is not to reopen the whole street. The city could put short loading bays at each end of the closed blocks, or let pickup trucks in before 9 a.m. on Saturdays. I'll admit this might not save every lumber sale. If the store is in the middle of the six blocks, a bay at the corner is still a long haul. In that case the honest answer is that some businesses don't fit a pedestrian block, and the city should either leave that one block open to slow traffic or help the store pay for delivery. Pretending the bakery's success makes up for the hardware store's loss is how the city ended up with a quarrel instead of a plan.\n\nThe nurse looks like the same complaint, but it isn't. Her problem has nothing to do with carrying anything. It's about time and safety. At eleven at night the bakery is closed and nobody is lingering, so the reason for closing the street is gone, but the closure is still pushing her eight blocks from her car. This is the easiest case to solve, because lingering happens at predictable hours. In my town, Main Street closes for the farmers market on Saturday mornings, and by one o'clock the barricades are gone and cars are parked at the curb again. Nobody fights about it, because the closure lines up with when people actually want to walk around. Carrow Street could do the same and reopen to cars at nine every night. Then the nurse isn't trading her safety for someone else's croissant.\n\nGetting the degree wrong has consequences that reach past downtown. Teague mentions that the hardware customers now drive to \"the outlet strip on the highway.\" If a city closes too much, it doesn't get rid of cars. It moves them, and it sorts people. Downtown ends up for people with free time and good legs, and everyone running errands, working night shifts, or unable to walk far gets sent to the highway. That's also why the right amount of restriction should change from city to city. A city with buses running late can close more, because the nurse has another way home. A small city like Verrick Falls, where the car is the only option for a lot of people, has to close less, and it has to close more carefully.\n\nTeague says a downtown must be a place people can reach and a place people want to stay. Those two purposes don't really have to compete if the city stops treating the street as one thing. Close it when people are staying and open it when they need to reach it. The limits should be as big as the lingering and no bigger.\n   - \"A city should limit private cars downtown only on the blocks and during the hours when people come to stay, and it should keep car access wherever people come to fetch something heavy or have to leave after dark.\" - This earns the thesis point. It takes a position on how far the limits should go, not just whether to have them, and it says what the degree depends on (blocks, hours, purpose of the trip). A reader could disagree with it.\n   - \"How much a city restricts cars should depend on why people are on the street, not on one rule for all six blocks.\" - This states the principle the body paragraphs will use, so it sets up a line of reasoning and doesn't just repeat the prompt.\n   - \"So the closure costs the hardware store exactly the thing it sells, which is convenience.\" - This is commentary, not summary. It explains why an errand business is hurt by a closure when a lingering business is helped, which ties the evidence to how much of the street to close.\n   - \"I'll admit this might not save every lumber sale. If the store is in the middle of the six blocks, a bay at the corner is still a long haul.\" - The writer answers a counterexample to their own fix instead of just mentioning it, and offers alternatives (keep one block open, help pay for delivery).\n   - \"In my town, Main Street closes for the farmers market on Saturday mornings, and by one o'clock the barricades are gone\" - Specific personal observation. It is used to show that lingering happens at predictable times, which supports the time-of-day rule.\n   - \"At eleven at night the bakery is closed and nobody is lingering, so the reason for closing the street is gone\" - The nurse's situation is treated as a problem the position has to solve, and the writer applies their own reasoning to solve it. This is the kind of commentary that reaches the 4 level.\n   - \"The nurse looks like the same complaint, but it isn't. Her problem has nothing to do with carrying anything. It's about time and safety.\" - The essay uses Teague's three people as a test for one principle and argues that the nurse and the hardware customer are different problems with different solutions. The rubric names this move as a way to earn sophistication.\n   - \"It moves them, and it sorts people.\" - Puts the question in a wider context: who downtown is for, and what happens to the highway strip. This continues through the paragraph about transit.\n   - \"A city with buses running late can close more, because the nurse has another way home.\" - The writer adjusts their position for different conditions (whether a city has transit), showing they have thought about unintended consequences of their own argument.\n   - \"The limits should be as big as the lingering and no bigger.\" - A short, controlled closing line that sharpens the thesis instead of just decorating it.\n\nSample response, middle score (4/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 3, Sophistication 0):\nA city should limit private cars downtown only part of the time and only on the blocks where people mostly come to browse and eat, while keeping curbside access on the blocks where people come to carry things away. In other words, how much a city limits cars should depend on what each street is used for, not on one rule for the whole downtown.\n\nThe column about Verrick Falls shows why one rule does not work. The hardware store owner lost his Saturday customers because they used to park at the curb and load lumber, and now they drive to the outlet strip on the highway. You cannot carry lumber several blocks from a parking garage. This shows that a store that sells heavy things depends on cars, and if the city closes the street in front of it, the store loses business to the highway. The bakery owner has the opposite experience, since she sells more rolls when people can linger with nothing idling behind them. Bakeries and cafes sell a pleasant stop, so a quiet street helps them. So the closing of Carrow Street helped one business and hurt another, which means a city should look at what businesses are on a street before it closes it.\n\nI have seen something like this in my own town. The street near my school with the coffee shops and the used bookstore has a farmers market on Sundays with the road blocked off, and it is always crowded and people seem happy. But the same weekend nobody would want the pharmacy two streets over to be blocked off, because older people drive there to pick up prescriptions and they cannot walk far. A closing that works for a market would not work for a pharmacy.\n\nThe nurse in the column is a harder problem. She gets off at eleven at night and has to walk eight blocks to the garage. I think the answer is time of day. The street can be closed during the day and on weekends when it is busy, and opened to cars at night so she can park close and feel safe. This costs the city something, because the street is less of a park at night, but there are few people lingering at that hour anyway, so the loss is small. Nobody is hurt much by letting cars back in at night, while the nurse could be hurt a lot.\n\nSome people will say that any cars at all ruin a walkable downtown. But a downtown that nobody can reach is not walkable either, it is just empty. The best position is a middle one, with closures on the blocks and hours when lingering matters most, and cars allowed where people have to reach something. That way the city gets both purposes the column names, a place people can reach and a place they want to stay.\n   - \"A city should limit private cars downtown only part of the time and only on the blocks where people mostly come to browse and eat, while keeping curbside access on the blocks where people come to carry things away.\" - Defensible, conditional thesis about the degree of limits (by time and by kind of block), not just whether cars exist. Earns the thesis point.\n   - \"You cannot carry lumber several blocks from a parking garage.\" - Uses the hardware store from the column and gives a real mechanism (heavy goods need curb access), and the point is stated rather than developed.\n   - \"So the closing of Carrow Street helped one business and hurt another, which means a city should look at what businesses are on a street before it closes it.\" - Links the two column examples back to the thesis, giving a followable line of reasoning, though the point is fairly general.\n   - \"A closing that works for a market would not work for a pharmacy.\" - Personal example is specific enough to count, but the pharmacy is described briefly and the commentary is thin and repeats the earlier point about different businesses.\n   - \"I think the answer is time of day.\" - Treats the nurse as a problem the position must solve, which is good, but the solution is stated simply and the claim that few people linger at night is asserted without support.\n   - \"But a downtown that nobody can reach is not walkable either, it is just empty.\" - Brief rebuttal of the opposing view; it is a single sentence and the essay does not explore unintended consequences, so no sustained sophistication.\n\nSample response, low score (1/6; Thesis 0, Evidence and commentary 1, Sophistication 0):\nCities have to decide how much to limit cars downtown and there are good points on both sides. In my opinion a city should limit cars but not too much, because downtowns need both cars and people walking.\n\nIn the column, Verrick Falls closed six blocks of Carrow Street and now people are arguing. The hardware store owner says his customers go to the highway now. The bakery lady says she sells more rolls. And the nurse is worried about walking to the garage at night. This shows that the issue is complicated because different people want different things. Some businesses like the closing and some do not.\n\nI think that closing streets is good because people like to walk around. When there are no cars it is quieter and cleaner and people stay longer. Also it is safer for kids. A lot of cities have done this and it works for them. Studies show that walkable downtowns do better, so cities should do it more.\n\nHowever, cars are important too. Not everybody can walk far, like old people or people who work late. If a city closes everything then people will just go somewhere else, like the outlet strip. That would hurt downtown. So a city should not close all of it.\n\nIn conclusion, a city should close some streets but leave some open for cars. This way the bakery and the hardware store can both be happy and the nurse can get to her car. It is a balance and each city has to find the right amount for them.\n   - \"a city should limit cars but not too much, because downtowns need both cars and people walking\" - Takes a position, but 'not too much' is vague and the essay never says what the limit should depend on or where the line falls. This is close to restating the prompt's tension, so the thesis point is likely not earned.\n   - \"Cities have to decide how much to limit cars downtown and there are good points on both sides.\" - Restates the prompt almost word for word and gestures at 'both sides' without committing to anything arguable.\n   - \"This shows that the issue is complicated because different people want different things.\" - Summarizes the hardware owner, baker and nurse and concludes it is 'complicated' with no argument built from them. This is the level-1 pattern of listing the three figures.\n   - \"Studies show that walkable downtowns do better, so cities should do it more.\" - Generic appeal to unnamed studies, with no specific example or explanation of the mechanism. This is the kind of generality that caps evidence at 1.\n   - \"A lot of cities have done this and it works for them.\" - No city is named and no scene is described, so this is assertion rather than evidence. Commentary is absent.\n   - \"This way the bakery and the hardware store can both be happy and the nurse can get to her car.\" - Proposes a compromise but never explains how leaving some streets open would serve each purpose, or why the hardware customer and the nurse are different problems. The compromise is not tested against the tension Teague names, so there is no sophistication.\n   - \"It is a balance and each city has to find the right amount for them.\" - Falls back on 'it's a balance' and leaves the tension unresolved. This gestures at complexity without working through it.","explanation_en":"Scoring guide (ALTON practice rubric, not official College Board scoring). Each essay is worth 6 points.\nThesis (0-1): The thesis must be about the extent of limits, not merely whether cars exist. 'Cities should limit cars somewhat' earns the point only if the essay makes clear, in the opening or the conclusion, what 'somewhat' means or on what the degree depends. A thesis may appear anywhere in the essay, including the conclusion.\n   - 1: A thesis that takes a defensible, arguable position on the degree to which a city should restrict private cars downtown. The position may be strong (cars should be nearly excluded; cars should be left alone), moderate, or conditional (limits should depend on time of day, size of the city, availability of transit, or the kind of business on the street). A thesis that presents a condition or a qualification still earns the point so long as it commits to something a reader could dispute.\n   - 0: No thesis, or a thesis that only restates the prompt (for example, 'Cities must decide how much to limit cars downtown'), or a thesis that merely summarizes Teague's column, or a thesis that takes a position on a different question (such as whether cars are bad for the environment in general) without connecting it to how far a city should limit cars in its downtown.\nEvidence and Commentary (0-4): Evidence counts whether drawn from the stimulus, from the writer's own town, from reading, or from reasoned hypotheticals, as long as it is specific. The writer is not required to use Teague's column at all. Commentary earns credit when it explains why a given example bears on the degree of limits a city should set, not merely on whether cars are good or bad. An essay that argues at length about climate or public health earns at most a 2 unless it connects those concerns to a decision about how much of downtown to close.\n   - 4: Provides specific, well-chosen evidence and consistently explains how each piece advances the argument. The reasoning is organized around the writer's own distinction (for instance, the difference between people who must reach downtown and people who choose to stay there, or between delivery traffic and commuter traffic, or between cities with and without working transit), and the writer uses that distinction to decide what should and should not be limited. Counterexamples are addressed, not merely mentioned; the nurse's late-night walk, for example, is treated as a problem the writer's position must solve.\n   - 3: Provides specific evidence and explains how it supports the claim, with a line of reasoning the reader can follow from paragraph to paragraph. Commentary may be uneven: one body paragraph explains carefully why a particular kind of business depends on curbside access while another simply asserts that pedestrian streets are 'more pleasant.' The writer may acknowledge an objection but treat it briefly or dismiss it rather than answer it.\n   - 2: Provides some specific evidence, such as a real or plausibly described downtown the writer has visited, a family member's commute, or the baker and the hardware store from the column, but the commentary is thin or repetitive: the writer says the evidence 'proves' the point without explaining the mechanism. Typical 2-level responses also wander among unrelated points (parking, pollution, crime, noise) without a line of reasoning that orders them.\n   - 1: Mostly generalities: the writer asserts that 'studies show' or 'everyone knows' that walkable downtowns thrive or that drivers abandon car-free areas, without a single specific example, scene, or observation. Or the writer lists the three people from the column (hardware store owner, baker, nurse) and says they show the issue is complicated, with no argument built from them.\n   - 0: Only restates the thesis, or offers evidence that has no bearing on the question of car limits (for example, a general essay on pollution with no connection to downtown streets), or consists entirely of summary of the Teague column.\nSophistication (0-1): Sophistication is most often earned by essays that turn Teague's three people into a test for a principle rather than three separate complaints: for example, an essay that argues the nurse and the hardware customer are different problems (one solved by time-of-day rules, the other perhaps not solvable) and says so. Merely proposing a compromise ('close streets on weekends') does not earn the point unless the writer explains why that compromise fits the competing purposes of a downtown.\n   - 1: The essay does one or more of the following in a sustained way, not in a single sentence: works through the real tension between access and lingering that Teague identifies and shows how the writer's position resolves it in particular cases; situates the question in a wider context (who downtown is for, how a city treats people who cannot walk far or who work odd hours, what happens to the highway strip when downtown closes); considers unintended consequences of the writer's own position and adjusts the position accordingly; or sustains a voice and style that are precise, controlled, and persuasive throughout.\n   - 0: The argument is one-sided or treats the question as settled; or the essay gestures at complexity ('there are many sides to this issue') without actually working through tensions; or the prose is ornate but does not sharpen the argument.\nNote: Score the essay as a whole; a strong conclusion can supply the thesis for Row A.\nNote: Do not reward or penalize the writer's political stance; a well-argued case for leaving cars alone can earn a 6, and a poorly argued case for a full car ban can earn a 2.\nNote: The stimulus is optional. Essays that never mention Verrick Falls are scored on exactly the same criteria.\nNote: Reasoned hypotheticals ('imagine a pharmacy whose customers cannot carry a week of groceries eight blocks') count as evidence if they are concrete and the writer explains what they show.\nNote: Row B scores of 3 and 4 require a visible line of reasoning; if the body paragraphs could be shuffled without loss, the ceiling is 2.\nNote: An essay that argues only about a single consideration (for example, only emissions) with excellent commentary may earn a 3 on Row B but is unlikely to earn the sophistication point because it does not engage the tension the prompt names.\n\nSample response, high score (6/6; Thesis 1, Evidence and commentary 4, Sophistication 1):\nMost of the fighting about car-free downtowns assumes there are only two choices: close the street or leave it alone. Marisol Teague's column about Carrow Street shows why that framing doesn't work. The hardware store owner, the baker, and the nurse are not just three opinions about cars. They are three different kinds of trips. A city should limit private cars downtown only on the blocks and during the hours when people come to stay, and it should keep car access wherever people come to fetch something heavy or have to leave after dark. How much a city restricts cars should depend on why people are on the street, not on one rule for all six blocks.\n\nStart with the hardware store and the bakery, since they seem like opposites. The baker's customers come to linger. They buy a roll, sit down, maybe buy another, and Teague says people linger \"when nothing is idling at their backs.\" For them the closed street is the product. The hardware customer is on an errand. Nobody lingers with a stack of two-by-fours, and nobody carries them eight blocks to a garage. So the closure costs the hardware store exactly the thing it sells, which is convenience. The fix is not to reopen the whole street. The city could put short loading bays at each end of the closed blocks