between temperature and the volume of oxygen produced from 10 °C to 37 °C."},{"label":"D","mode":"explain","points":1,"prompt":"Justify your prediction about the volume of oxygen produced at 70 °C, which is much higher than the optimum temperature of catalase."}]},"set":{"explanation":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [1 pt] An enzyme lowers the activation energy of a reaction by binding the substrate at its active site.\n   - 1 pt: Gives a correct description of the biological concept or process\n(B) [1 pt] The dependent variable is the volume of oxygen gas produced in 2 minutes, which is measured by collecting the gas.\n   - 1 pt: Identifies an appropriate feature of the experimental design and gives the reason\n(C) [1 pt] The volume of oxygen produced will increase as the temperature increases from 10 °C to 37 °C, and the volume produced at 70 °C will be lower than the volume produced at 37 °C.\n   - 1 pt: Makes an appropriate prediction of the results\n(D) [1 pt] Less oxygen will be produced at 70 °C because the high temperature denatures the enzyme, changing the shape of the active site.\n   - 1 pt: Justifies the prediction or result with biological reasoning","explanation_en":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [1 pt] An enzyme lowers the activation energy of a reaction by binding the substrate at its active site.\n   - 1 pt: Gives a correct description of the biological concept or process\n(B) [1 pt] The dependent variable is the volume of oxygen gas produced in 2 minutes, which is measured by collecting the gas.\n   - 1 pt: Identifies an appropriate feature of the experimental design and gives the reason\n(C) [1 pt] The volume of oxygen produced will increase as the temperature increases from 10 °C to 37 °C, and the volume produced at 70 °C will be lower than the volume produced at 37 °C.\n   - 1 pt: Makes an appropriate prediction of the results\n(D) [1 pt] Less oxygen will be produced at 70 °C because the high temperature denatures the enzyme, changing the shape of the active site.\n   - 1 pt: Justifies the prediction or result with biological reasoning","statements":[{"label":"A","mode":"explain","points":1,"prompt":"Describe how an enzyme speeds up a chemical reaction."},{"label":"B","mode":"explain","points":1,"prompt":"Identify the dependent variable in the investigation, and describe how it is measured."},{"label":"C","mode":"explain","points":1,"prompt":"Predict the relationship between temperature and the volume of oxygen produced from 10 °C to 37 °C, and predict how the volume of oxygen produced at 70 °C compares with the volume produced at 37 °C."},{"label":"D","mode":"explain","points":1,"prompt":"Justify your prediction about the volume of oxygen produced at 70 °C, which is much higher than the optimum temperature of catalase."}]},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-15T15:30:39.091Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R6-BIO1-FRQ3CD","fromVersionId":"9c2bd420-9caf-48df-aafb-ef2771ad24fe","fromVersionNo":1},"ap":{"candidate_key":"bio-s2-frq:ap_biology-f07-k0","item_index":0}},{"patch_id":"R6-BIO2-FRQ2","problem_id":"8e64e190-e66e-4a60-9200-16bffe0244df","from_version_id":"de85bf78-b614-473b-ab3b-a4374f7b0a80","expect":{"question":"Results with graphing: A student measures the rate of oxygen pr — answer every part.","explanation":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [1 pt] The light reactions capture light energy and use it to make ATP and NADPH, which the Calvin cycle uses to fix carbon dioxide into sugar.\n   - 1 pt: Gives a correct description of the biological concept\n(B) [4 pt] A line graph with Light intensity (arbitrary units) on the horizontal axis and Rate of oxygen production (µL per minute) on the vertical axis, with all 6 points plotted and connected.\n   - 1 pt: Labels both axes with the variable and the units, with the independent variable on the horizontal axis\n   - 1 pt: Uses an appropriate, evenly spaced scale for each axis\n   - 1 pt: Plots all of the data points correctly\n   - 1 pt: Draws an appropriate type of graph, with the points connected\n(C) [2 pt] (i) rate = (30 - 0) / (50 - 0) = 0.60 µL per minute per unit of light. (ii) Carbon dioxide concentration (or the amount of enzymes of the Calvin cycle) limits the rate at high light.\n   - 1 pt: Gives the correct average rate of increase (answer)\n   - 1 pt: Identifies a valid limiting factor other than light\n(D) [2 pt] The rate of oxygen production would increase. At high light intensity the rate is limited by carbon dioxide in the Calvin cycle, so more carbon dioxide allows more carbon fixation and more use of the products of the light reactions.\n   - 1 pt: Makes a reasonable prediction\n   - 1 pt: Justifies the prediction with biological reasoning","explanation_en":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [1 pt] The light reactions capture light energy and use it to make ATP and NADPH, which the Calvin cycle uses to fix carbon dioxide into sugar.\n   - 1 pt: Gives a correct description of the biological concept\n(B) [4 pt] A line graph with Light intensity (arbitrary units) on the horizontal axis and Rate of oxygen production (µL per minute) on the vertical axis, with all 6 points plotted and connected.\n   - 1 pt: Labels both axes with the variable and the units, with the independent variable on the horizontal axis\n   - 1 pt: Uses an appropriate, evenly spaced scale for each axis\n   - 1 pt: Plots all of the data points correctly\n   - 1 pt: Draws an appropriate type of graph, with the points connected\n(C) [2 pt] (i) rate = (30 - 0) / (50 - 0) = 0.60 µL per minute per unit of light. (ii) Carbon dioxide concentration (or the amount of enzymes of the Calvin cycle) limits the rate at high light.\n   - 1 pt: Gives the correct average rate of increase (answer)\n   - 1 pt: Identifies a valid limiting factor other than light\n(D) [2 pt] The rate of oxygen production would increase. At high light intensity the rate is limited by carbon dioxide in the Calvin cycle, so more carbon dioxide allows more carbon fixation and more use of the products of the light reactions.\n   - 1 pt: Makes a reasonable prediction\n   - 1 pt: Justifies the prediction with biological reasoning","statements":[{"label":"A","mode":"explain","points":1,"prompt":"Describe the role of the light reactions in photosynthesis, including one product that is used by the Calvin cycle."},{"label":"B","mode":"draw","points":4,"prompt":"Construct an appropriately labeled graph of the data in the table. Use the space provided to draw your graph."},{"label":"C","mode":"calculate","points":2,"prompt":"(i) Calculate the average rate of increase in oxygen production per unit of light intensity between 0 and 50 units. Show your work. (ii) Identify a factor other than light intensity that most likely limits the rate at the highest light intensities."},{"label":"D","mode":"explain","points":2,"prompt":"Predict how the rate of oxygen production at the highest light intensity would change if the carbon dioxide concentration available to the plant were doubled, and justify your prediction."}]},"set":{"question":"Results with graphing: oxygen production at different light intensities — answer every part.","explanation":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [1 pt] The light reactions capture light energy and use it to make ATP and NADPH, which the Calvin cycle uses to fix carbon dioxide into sugar.\n   - 1 pt: Gives a correct description of the biological concept\n(B) [4 pt] A line graph with Light intensity (arbitrary units) on the horizontal axis and Rate of oxygen production (µL per minute) on the vertical axis, with all 6 points plotted and connected.\n   - 1 pt: Labels both axes with the variable and the units, with the independent variable on the horizontal axis\n   - 1 pt: Uses an appropriate, evenly spaced scale for each axis\n   - 1 pt: Plots all of the data points correctly\n   - 1 pt: Draws an appropriate type of graph, with the points connected\n(C) [2 pt] (i) rate = (30 - 0) / (50 - 0) = 0.60 µL per minute per unit of light. (ii) Carbon dioxide concentration (or the amount of enzymes of the Calvin cycle) limits the rate at high light.\n   - 1 pt: Gives the correct average rate of increase (answer)\n   - 1 pt: Identifies a valid limiting factor other than light\n(D) [2 pt] If carbon dioxide is the factor limiting the rate at high light intensity (as in part C), the rate of oxygen production would increase, because more carbon dioxide allows more carbon fixation and more use of the products of the light reactions. If a different factor, such as the amount of Calvin-cycle enzymes, is limiting, doubling carbon dioxide would cause little or no change.\n   - 1 pt: Makes a reasonable prediction consistent with the limiting factor named in part (C)\n   - 1 pt: Justifies the prediction with biological reasoning","explanation_en":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [1 pt] The light reactions capture light energy and use it to make ATP and NADPH, which the Calvin cycle uses to fix carbon dioxide into sugar.\n   - 1 pt: Gives a correct description of the biological concept\n(B) [4 pt] A line graph with Light intensity (arbitrary units) on the horizontal axis and Rate of oxygen production (µL per minute) on the vertical axis, with all 6 points plotted and connected.\n   - 1 pt: Labels both axes with the variable and the units, with the independent variable on the horizontal axis\n   - 1 pt: Uses an appropriate, evenly spaced scale for each axis\n   - 1 pt: Plots all of the data points correctly\n   - 1 pt: Draws an appropriate type of graph, with the points connected\n(C) [2 pt] (i) rate = (30 - 0) / (50 - 0) = 0.60 µL per minute per unit of light. (ii) Carbon dioxide concentration (or the amount of enzymes of the Calvin cycle) limits the rate at high light.\n   - 1 pt: Gives the correct average rate of increase (answer)\n   - 1 pt: Identifies a valid limiting factor other than light\n(D) [2 pt] If carbon dioxide is the factor limiting the rate at high light intensity (as in part C), the rate of oxygen production would increase, because more carbon dioxide allows more carbon fixation and more use of the products of the light reactions. If a different factor, such as the amount of Calvin-cycle enzymes, is limiting, doubling carbon dioxide would cause little or no change.\n   - 1 pt: Makes a reasonable prediction consistent with the limiting factor named in part (C)\n   - 1 pt: Justifies the prediction with biological reasoning","statements":[{"label":"A","mode":"explain","points":1,"prompt":"Describe the role of the light reactions in photosynthesis, including one product that is used by the Calvin cycle."},{"label":"B","mode":"draw","points":4,"prompt":"Construct an appropriately labeled graph of the data in the table. Use the space provided to draw your graph."},{"label":"C","mode":"calculate","points":2,"prompt":"(i) Calculate the average rate of increase in oxygen production per unit of light intensity between 0 and 50 units. Show your work. (ii) Identify a factor other than light intensity that most likely limits the rate at the highest light intensities."},{"label":"D","mode":"explain","points":2,"prompt":"Using the factor you identified in part (C), predict how the rate of oxygen production at the highest light intensity would change if the carbon dioxide concentration available to the plant were doubled, and justify your prediction."}]},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-15T15:30:39.091Z","issues":[],"alt":"Table with columns Light intensity (arbitrary units), Rate of oxygen production (µL per minute) and 6 rows."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R6-BIO2-FRQ2","fromVersionId":"de85bf78-b614-473b-ab3b-a4374f7b0a80","fromVersionNo":1},"ap":{"candidate_key":"bio-s2-frq:ap_biology-f05-k0","item_index":0}}],"sets":[{"set_group_id":"6f4473a5-0b9c-47ab-a0e4-1fdbb7c07c4b","set_id":"41cece8b-ced9-445a-b791-f75a8fd3f85a","name":"AP Biology — Practice Exam 1","version_no":2,"format":"ap_fixed","problem_ids":["b724f500-e5aa-4330-b4f6-938f7cb23e8a","eeb446bd-4642-4135-a934-6af1b68ffae9","16a33027-1c97-44ae-bb72-a44c7a4c3881"],"patch_ids":["R6-BIO1-FRQ1D","R6-BIO1-FRQ2T","R6-BIO1-FRQ3CD"]},{"set_group_id":"34569222-4ad0-4fc0-8c04-be069c35cf10","set_id":"a4a49873-257e-48ae-be5d-0668f697db67","name":"AP Biology — Practice Exam 2","version_no":3,"format":"ap_fixed","problem_ids":["8e64e190-e66e-4a60-9200-16bffe0244df"],"patch_ids":["R6-BIO2-FRQ2"]},{"set_group_id":"0952b774-6156-4d09-a5fa-fb4943b20dac","set_id":"0989f718-a192-456f-a845-30b097e8df04","name":"AP Chemistry — Practice Exam 2","version_no":2,"format":"ap_fixed","problem_ids":["e45902d7-9d9c-4c98-a72c-dcd21a7000b0"],"patch_ids":["R6-CHEM2-MC29"]},{"set_group_id":"65efce5c-39ff-44dc-a868-c87e707027ec","set_id":"1678706f-5f94-4e5f-a3da-ae7df55c0a62","name":"AP Computer Science A — Practice Exam 2","version_no":1,"format":"ap_fixed","problem_ids":["660364db-1156-4d7c-99d7-57ea9c6b9212"],"patch_ids":["R6-CSA2-FRQ4"]},{"set_group_id":"591a9abd-bf5d-4387-9d79-a138ef814967","set_id":"416bf3b5-77a0-434a-a3d9-17979cb27983","name":"AP English Language — Practice Exam 1","version_no":2,"format":"ap_fixed","problem_ids":["a0d8b10e-5ed9-4740-994c-48fa1f29e1ee","95f6fb44-e162-4c88-9966-3cf4aa509017"],"patch_ids":["R6-ELA1-FRQ1","R6-ELA1-FRQ3"]},{"set_group_id":"43bb7a0c-7010-4930-8da3-e5e168ade116","set_id":"4863745e-6eca-43ea-9cbb-e22f14500e6f","name":"AP English Language — Practice Exam 2","version_no":2,"format":"ap_fixed","problem_ids":["8a522f7d-2718-4820-8d8f-eaba2e0161e9","e5fb78ec-f380-4c98-9474-c3c81026102e"],"patch_ids":["R6-ELA2-FRQ1","R6-ELA2-FRQ2"]},{"set_group_id":"11cc6603-0671-4ef7-93b7-02b2011294d4","set_id":"d734d73e-2f4d-4c6a-ba53-97400dfc0172","name":"AP Macroeconomics — Practice Exam 1","version_no":1,"format":"ap_fixed","problem_ids":["5885525e-029f-4d59-b3fd-f248ba74dbd6","92765650-9861-477c-9737-7d1412949a13"],"patch_ids":["R6-MACRO1-MC28","R6-MACRO1-FRQ1DE"]},{"set_group_id":"f981bddb-2e31-4bf0-bdc6-da8f68582ed0","set_id":"77fc31d6-741c-4b80-a0ae-e2c070f65553","name":"AP Microeconomics — Practice Exam 1","version_no":2,"format":"ap_fixed","problem_ids":["ce9ec65e-9f24-436a-a8ac-c2af6df55454"],"patch_ids":["R6-MICRO1-MC53"]},{"set_group_id":"1f165064-c082-4d5e-bd17-0a0f3863e6f8","set_id":"a71cdb95-8351-4c7c-b7fd-29a752cfb324","name":"AP Microeconomics — Practice Exam 2","version_no":2,"format":"ap_fixed","problem_ids":["41e1e9a4-0c6b-411b-b96b-eb3bc09c718f","07516ae5-0353-4b3f-976c-ec50de46dd9e"],"patch_ids":["R6-MICRO2-FRQ1E","R6-MICRO2-FRQ3C"]},{"set_group_id":"c21312d0-2b9a-4ee5-8196-e98aa5f2fc17","set_id":"c8013db8-07f5-437a-b8ae-03b75ba3820f","name":"AP Physics 1 — Practice Exam 1","version_no":2,"format":"ap_fixed","problem_ids":["b6d4eaa9-9661-4b23-9b56-33c86e25a0fd","4fbf6e9e-b653-41ee-892d-1cb5cefe0403"],"patch_ids":["R6-PHYS1-MC9","R6-PHYS1-FRQ1D"]},{"set_group_id":"8b284820-f11f-43ad-a33b-816df5479f26","set_id":"4ee4c6c6-a5e5-4173-af93-ca1a591341df","name":"AP Physics 1 — Practice Exam 2","version_no":2,"format":"ap_fixed","problem_ids":["c76a314a-f22b-4bb2-a1c2-6f17148d1333"],"patch_ids":["R6-PHYS2-FRQ1D"]},{"set_group_id":"834d3096-cb62-42c4-be52-c6a76bc88e81","set_id":"818083fe-1350-4d12-8cd7-b1512697c702","name":"AP Psychology — Practice Exam 1","version_no":1,"format":"ap_fixed","problem_ids":["be808ea5-604b-495a-96b8-4af052b26940"],"patch_ids":["R6-PSY1-EBQ"]},{"set_group_id":"6a25f2b5-92a6-48a3-a4b7-8a2c420f8e19","set_id":"508ecb4c-2696-41e2-ae74-0d90d5c78c52","name":"AP Psychology — Practice Exam 2","version_no":1,"format":"ap_fixed","problem_ids":["6e7e25f1-b0f0-4b2a-9d48-d60aa25e3498","fed394b6-24d0-41d6-b465-6e266d227fa4","5239ef26-b201-478a-829b-33096f21ecd5","b51a659b-a9d3-44ac-94ec-8c9841a7ad4a"],"patch_ids":["R6-PSY2-MC58","R6-PSY2-MC59","R6-PSY2-MC60","R6-PSY2-EBQ"]},{"set_group_id":"a57a7d5e-850c-43f2-991d-785450e72018","set_id":"4999b55a-b65b-48e7-bf55-3166375689f9","name":"AP Statistics — Practice Exam 1","version_no":3,"format":"ap_fixed","problem_ids":["b597391d-c790-4fc0-8a88-c6ef3ff3c592"],"patch_ids":["R6-STAT1-FRQ4D"]},{"set_group_id":"f99e778c-3b8f-4e4a-8116-c5bc29a338a9","set_id":"0e125500-dea4-4b5f-84bd-a86f30712685","name":"AP Statistics — Practice Exam 2","version_no":2,"format":"ap_fixed","problem_ids":["74b5384e-aae6-4ca7-acf7-e693ca0bbcbd","58571074-1835-4031-a86b-91d697e02e1a"],"patch_ids":["R6-STAT2-MC28","R6-STAT2-FRQ1C"]}]}$audit_plan$::jsonb;
  v_actor uuid := (select id from profiles where role = 'admin' order by created_at limit 1);
  u jsonb; s jsonb; a jsonb;
  v_prob problems%rowtype; v_cur problem_versions%rowtype; v_have jsonb; v_want jsonb; v_new uuid;
  v_src mock_exam_sets%rowtype; v_new_set uuid; v_cols text; v_cols_i text; v_pids uuid[];
  v_n int; v_stale int; v_bad text; v_done_p int := 0; v_skip_p int := 0; v_done_s int := 0; v_skip_s int := 0;
begin
  if v_actor is null then raise exception 'No admin profile found.'; end if;

  -- Phase 1: problems -> new published problem version each (same flow as the admin problem editor)
  for u in select * from jsonb_array_elements(v_plan->'problems') loop
    select * into v_prob from problems where id = (u->>'problem_id')::uuid;
    if v_prob.id is null then raise exception '[%] problem % not found', u->>'patch_id', u->>'problem_id'; end if;
    select * into v_cur from problem_versions where id = v_prob.published_version_id;
    if v_cur.id is null then raise exception '[%] problem % has no published version', u->>'patch_id', v_prob.id; end if;
    -- final field values = current published version, overridden by the planned new values
    a := jsonb_build_object('passage', v_cur.passage, 'question', v_cur.question, 'options', v_cur.options, 'correct_index', v_cur.correct_index, 'answers', v_cur.answers,
                            'explanation', v_cur.explanation, 'explanation_en', v_cur.explanation_en, 'figure', v_cur.figure, 'statements', v_cur.statements, 'difficulty', v_cur.difficulty,
                            'figure_checked', coalesce(v_cur.figure_checked, false), 'evidence_target', v_cur.evidence_target, 'evidence_span', v_cur.evidence_span,
                            'answer_rationale', v_cur.answer_rationale, 'distractor_error_types', v_cur.distractor_error_types);
    v_have := (select coalesce(jsonb_object_agg(k, a->k), '{}'::jsonb) from jsonb_object_keys(u->'expect') k);
    v_want := (select coalesce(jsonb_object_agg(k, (a || (u->'set'))->k), '{}'::jsonb) from jsonb_object_keys(u->'expect') k);
    if v_have = v_want then v_skip_p := v_skip_p + 1; raise notice '[%] already applied, skipped', u->>'patch_id'; continue; end if;
    if v_have <> u->'expect' then raise exception '[%] DRIFT: the published version of problem % no longer matches the expected old content (re-run the dry-run)', u->>'patch_id', v_prob.id; end if;
    a := a || (u->'set');
    if exists (select 1 from problem_versions where problem_id = v_prob.id and status in ('draft', 'in_review')) then
      raise exception '[%] problem % has an open draft/in_review version', u->>'patch_id', v_prob.id;
    end if;
    v_new := public.save_problem_draft_version(
      p_problem_id => v_prob.id, p_passage => a->>'passage', p_options => nullif(a->'options', 'null'::jsonb), p_correct_index => (a->>'correct_index')::int,
      p_explanation => a->>'explanation', p_difficulty => a->>'difficulty', p_actor_id => v_actor, p_answers => nullif(a->'answers', 'null'::jsonb),
      p_figure => nullif(a->'figure', 'null'::jsonb), p_figure_checked => coalesce((a->>'figure_checked')::boolean, false), p_statements => nullif(a->'statements', 'null'::jsonb),
      p_question => a->>'question', p_repair_status => null, p_explanation_en => a->>'explanation_en', p_evidence_target => a->>'evidence_target',
      p_evidence_span => a->>'evidence_span', p_answer_rationale => a->>'answer_rationale', p_distractor_error_types => nullif(a->'distractor_error_types', 'null'::jsonb));
    perform public.set_problem_render_check(v_new, u->'render_check');
    perform public.set_problem_quality(v_new, coalesce(v_cur.quality, '{}'::jsonb) || jsonb_build_object('auditFix', u->'audit_fix'));
    perform public.confirm_and_publish_problem_version(v_new, v_actor);
    if u ? 'ap' then perform public.ap_attach_new_version(u->'ap'->>'candidate_key', (u->'ap'->>'item_index')::int, v_new, v_actor); end if;
    v_done_p := v_done_p + 1;
    raise notice '[%] problem % -> new version %', u->>'patch_id', v_prob.id, v_new;
  end loop;

  -- Phase 2: sets -> new set version per affected published set (items of the patched problems point to the new problem versions; snapshots of the rest are copied)
  for s in select * from jsonb_array_elements(v_plan->'sets') loop
    select array_agg(x::uuid) into v_pids from jsonb_array_elements_text(s->'problem_ids') x;
    select * into v_src from mock_exam_sets where set_group_id = (s->>'set_group_id')::uuid and status = 'published' and archived_at is null order by version_no desc limit 1 for update;
    if v_src.id is null then raise exception 'Set group % has no published version (%).', s->>'set_group_id', s->>'name'; end if;
    select count(*) into v_n from mock_exam_set_items i where i.exam_set_id = v_src.id and i.problem_id = any (v_pids);
    if v_n <> cardinality(v_pids) then raise exception 'Set % v% holds % of % target problems.', v_src.name, v_src.version_no, v_n, cardinality(v_pids); end if;
    select count(*) into v_stale from mock_exam_set_items i join problems p on p.id = i.problem_id
     where i.exam_set_id = v_src.id and i.problem_id = any (v_pids) and p.published_version_id is distinct from i.problem_version_id;
    if v_stale = 0 then v_skip_s := v_skip_s + 1; raise notice 'set % v% already up to date, skipped', v_src.name, v_src.version_no; continue; end if;
    perform pg_advisory_xact_lock(hashtextextended('mock_exam_new_version:' || v_src.set_group_id::text, 0));
    if exists (select 1 from mock_exam_sets where set_group_id = v_src.set_group_id and status = 'draft') then
      raise exception 'A draft version already exists for set % - publish or archive it first.', v_src.name;
    end if;
    v_new_set := gen_random_uuid();
    select string_agg(quote_ident(column_name), ', ' order by ordinal_position) into v_cols
      from information_schema.columns where table_schema = 'public' and table_name = 'mock_exam_sets' and is_generated = 'NEVER'
       and column_name not in ('id', 'version_no', 'status', 'created_by', 'created_at', 'published_at', 'published_by', 'archived_at', 'access_tier');
    execute format('insert into mock_exam_sets (id, version_no, status, created_by, access_tier, %s) select $1, $2, ''draft'', $3, ''tutoring'', %s from mock_exam_sets where id = $4', v_cols, v_cols)
      using v_new_set, (select max(version_no) + 1 from mock_exam_sets where set_group_id = v_src.set_group_id), v_actor, v_src.id;
    select string_agg(quote_ident(column_name), ', ' order by ordinal_position),
           string_agg(case column_name
                        when 'problem_version_id' then 'case when i.problem_id = any ($3) then coalesce(p.published_version_id, i.problem_version_id) else i.problem_version_id end'
                        when 'content_snapshot' then 'case when i.problem_id = any ($3) then null else i.content_snapshot end'
                        else 'i.' || quote_ident(column_name) end, ', ' order by ordinal_position)
      into v_cols, v_cols_i
      from information_schema.columns where table_schema = 'public' and table_name = 'mock_exam_set_items' and is_generated = 'NEVER' and column_name not in ('id', 'exam_set_id', 'created_at');
    execute format('insert into mock_exam_set_items (exam_set_id, %s) select $1, %s from mock_exam_set_items i join problems p on p.id = i.problem_id where i.exam_set_id = $2', v_cols, v_cols_i)
      using v_new_set, v_src.id, v_pids;
    if v_src.format = 'mst' and not coalesce((public.mock_exam_validate_mst_set(v_new_set)->>'ready')::boolean, false) then
      raise exception 'New version of % is not ready (4-module blueprint check failed).', v_src.name;
    end if;
    select string_agg(x.problem_id::text || ':' || x.issue, ', ') into v_bad from (select * from public.mock_exam_set_item_issues(v_new_set) where issue is not null limit 5) x;
    if v_bad is not null then raise exception 'New version of % has item issues: %', v_src.name, v_bad; end if;
    perform public.mock_exam_publish_set(v_new_set, v_actor);
    v_done_s := v_done_s + 1;
    raise notice 'set % v% -> v% (%)', v_src.name, v_src.version_no, v_src.version_no + 1, v_new_set;
  end loop;
  raise notice 'DONE problems applied=% skipped=%; sets applied=% skipped=%', v_done_p, v_skip_p, v_done_s, v_skip_s;
end
$audit$;
